"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Technical controls': {
        'body': 'Technical controls are device settings that keep enforcing a chosen rule after the clinic staff go home. Technical controls include host firewalls, network access lists, volume encryption, and directory lockout counters. Technical controls also include inline intrusion prevention that drops a packet instead of merely recording it. Technical controls matter at 23:41 because a memo cannot cut off a live session while the building is empty. Technical controls work by comparing a packet, a sign-in, or a file with a condition stored on the device. Technical controls then allow that request, deny it, or write a log line an analyst can pull later. Technical controls on the clinic edge deny TCP 22 unless the source is jump host 10.8.0.4. Technical controls on that edge also deny TCP 3389 from every address except jump host 10.8.0.4. Technical controls logged DENY TCP 22 from 203.0.113.50 to payroll host 10.1.4.20 at 23:41. Technical controls raised a hit count of fourteen while the firewall console showed no interactive user. Technical controls became the label on Friday ticket 4418 for those payroll administration attempts. Technical controls evidence for ticket 4418 is the rule id, the deny line, and the matching clock time. Technical controls are a different fact from the signed privacy page that forbids copying customer lists. Technical controls are a different fact from the Friday sandbox restore the service desk actually performs. Technical controls answer the category question: the enforcement sits in hardware or software. Technical controls remain that category when a written rule only ordered someone to configure the box. Technical controls that drop packets are preventive by timing and technical by where they live. Technical controls that only listen, such as signatures in tap mode, are detective by timing. Technical controls include AES-256 full-disk encryption, which withholds plaintext if the volume key is absent. Technical controls include a directory setting that locks an account after five bad password attempts. Technical controls include a mail-gateway block that stops a customer spreadsheet from leaving the company. Technical controls usually close a ruleset with an implicit deny so an unnamed flow is discarded. Technical controls lose that protection when an any-any allow is inserted above the closing deny. Technical controls running as inline Snort or inline Suricata drop frames that match a signature. Technical controls left in tap mode only copy frames, so read the deploy mode before naming a function. Technical controls on Linux servers are often printed from iptables or nftables by the host admin. Technical controls on laptops often sit in Windows Defender Firewall profiles for domain and public links. Technical controls at sign-in include a multi-factor prompt the identity platform is set not to skip. Technical controls on a roaming laptop still filter traffic after the clinic edge can no longer see it. Technical controls should be rechecked after every edit because one broad allow can shadow a careful deny. Technical controls for administration belong on a management path rather than on the guest wireless segment.',
        'r0': 'Technical controls are allow, deny, or log actions a device performs with nobody at the keyboard. Technical controls compare packets, credentials, or files with a rule stored on that device. Technical controls commonly deny TCP 22 and TCP 3389 from the internet toward internal hosts. Technical controls also include AES-256 volume encryption and an account lockout after repeated failures. Technical controls in inline prevention drop a matching packet, while tap mode only observes it. Technical controls log lines should show source, destination, protocol, time, and a hit counter. Technical controls are not the signed order that told an admin to build the rule. Technical controls are not the restore drill people practice and initial on Friday. Technical controls are the right pick when the stem shows the box itself enforcing the rule. Technical controls on a laptop still apply when that laptop leaves the building network.',
        'r1': 'Technical controls let the machine enforce a rule alone. Technical controls keep working while the office is empty. Technical controls can refuse a request with nobody nearby. Technical controls differ from a rule that lives on paper. Technical controls differ from a habit people must remember. Technical controls are the lock built into the machine. Technical controls still refuse someone who is rushing. Technical controls leave a mark each time they block. Technical controls should be read from the machine record. Technical controls matter after the staff have gone home. Technical controls block even when a poster says please wait. Technical controls are the box saying the answer itself.',
        'r2': 'Technical controls resemble a subway turnstile whose arms stay locked until a valid token is offered. Technical controls let one rider through and then lock again without caring that the rider is late. Technical controls keep that bar in place after the attendant steps away for a meal. Technical controls: A paper notice can ask people to take turns, yet technical controls are the metal arms rather than the notice. Technical controls: A morning practice where staff line up neatly is useful, yet technical controls still do the blocking when practice ends. Technical controls: Someone who climbs the railing has left the path technical controls were built to manage. Hold technical controls on the mechanism that moves by itself. Technical controls: The crowd does not bargain with technical controls, because the arms answer only to the token. Technical controls stay the picture of a bar that does not negotiate.',
        'r4': 'Filter Technical controls deny lines from the clinic edge for last night. Export Technical controls evidence showing source, destination, time, and hit count. Paste Technical controls log text into ticket 4418 before any opinion. Match Technical controls rule ids against the change that was approved. Mark Technical controls only when the device itself enforced the block. Separate Technical controls evidence from the signed page and from the drill sheet. Stop Technical controls review once the hit count sits on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'A device deny is technical controls even when a policy demanded the rule. Technical controls: The signed page alone is managerial controls, not technical controls. Technical controls: A restore rehearsal is operational controls, not technical controls. Technical controls: A closet lock is outside technical controls. Technical controls: Dropping packets is preventive timing, while the firewall category remains technical controls. Technical controls: A warning sign or a guard is not technical controls.',
    },
    'Managerial controls': {
        'body': 'Managerial controls are decisions written down with a named owner, a review date, and a place auditors can open. Managerial controls take the form of a policy, a standard, a chosen baseline, or a risk acceptance that expires. Managerial controls matter because firewalls and Friday drills drift when nobody has signed what good looks like. Managerial controls work when a leader approves the text, assigns the owner, and stores the page in the register. Managerial controls show up as acceptable-use language, a data-classification standard, or a password standard the directory must follow. Managerial controls also show up as a risk acceptance that names the gap, the reason, and an end date such as June. Managerial controls: Clinic privacy lead Helen Nguyen signed policy POL-17, which forbids copying customer lists into personal mailboxes. Managerial controls on POL-17 list Helen as owner, set a March review, and state the consequence for a violation. Managerial controls: Ticket 4520 was not a blocked message. Managerial controls were missing because the idea had only been said in a meeting. Managerial controls became the right label once staff could point at the signature rather than at a hallway conversation. Managerial controls differ in kind from the mail gateway that can actually stop a spreadsheet from leaving. Managerial controls differ in kind from the annual awareness exercise people attend and initial. Managerial controls: A gateway deny stays a device action even when managerial controls ordered that the deny be built. Managerial controls: A Friday restore practice stays a human habit even when managerial controls required the practice on the calendar. Managerial controls are the answer on governance items that show a signature, a board minute, or an accepted exception. Managerial controls: NIST CSF and ISO/IEC 27001 may inspire the wording, yet the adopted page and the mapping memo are managerial controls. A standard under managerial controls is stricter than a policy because it states the required setting, not only the aim. Managerial controls: A procedure lists steps, while managerial controls are the decision to adopt that procedure and name who owns it. Risk acceptance remains managerial controls when a director writes that a gap may stand until a dated review. Managerial controls: An unsigned slide deck is not managerial controls yet, no matter how confident the presenter sounded. Managerial controls: Guidelines that say should are weaker than standards that say must, and both can still be managerial controls if an owner exists. Managerial controls fail quietly when the owner leaves and the review month passes with nobody inheriting the page. Every exception filed as managerial controls needs an owner, a reason, and an expiry rather than an open-ended shrug. Managerial controls: Do not call the intrusion console managerial controls, and do not call the badge drill managerial controls. Managerial controls: Asked what was decided, choose managerial controls. Asked what dropped the packet, choose the device layer instead. Managerial controls for a financial system often support separation of duties because a later audit will ask who accepted the change risk.',
        'r0': 'Managerial controls are governance records: policies, standards, baselines, and signed risk acceptances. Managerial controls carry an owner and a review date, and they state intent rather than dropping packets. Managerial controls: A password standard, a classification policy, and a June risk acceptance are managerial controls. Technical settings implement managerial controls inside firewalls, encryption, and lockout thresholds. Practiced drills carry out managerial controls and remain a separate label when people do the work. Managerial controls: NIST CSF or ISO/IEC 27001 may shape the draft, but the adopted page is managerial controls. Choose managerial controls when the stem shows a signature, an owner, or a dated exception. Managerial controls: An unsigned meeting note is not yet managerial controls. Managerial controls on POL-17 name Helen Nguyen and a March review for the customer-list rule. Managerial controls expire when the acceptance date passes and nobody renews the signature.',
        'r1': 'Managerial controls are rules a boss writes down and owns. Managerial controls name the person who must answer. Managerial controls can accept a risk until a date. Managerial controls are the signed page people can open. Managerial controls are separate from the blocking machine. Managerial controls are separate from Friday practice. Managerial controls should show a review date. Managerial controls weaken if nobody inherits the page. Read managerial controls from the signed document itself. Managerial controls: A hallway talk alone is not managerial controls. Managerial controls: A poster with no owner is thin managerial controls. Managerial controls tell staff what leadership already chose.',
        'r2': 'Managerial controls resemble a clinic board where the director posts who may enter the records room and signs the sheet. Managerial controls are that signed sheet, including the name of the person who will answer questions later. Managerial controls do not turn into the door lock, and they do not turn into footsteps in the hall. Managerial controls: A visitor can ignore a sheet, so managerial controls need other layers, yet the sheet is still the decision. When names change, managerial controls change only after a new sheet is signed and the old sheet is taken down. Hold managerial controls on the owned decision rather than on metal or on rehearsal. Managerial controls stay the picture of ink, a name, and a date on the board. Managerial controls: Staff who merely remember the rule have not refreshed managerial controls until the board is updated.',
        'r4': 'Managerial controls: Open the policy register and locate Managerial controls by the id POL-17. Confirm Managerial controls show Helen Nguyen as owner and a review date still ahead. Record Managerial controls owner on ticket 4520 instead of a department nickname. Match Managerial controls wording to the customer-list behavior the requester described. Separate Managerial controls from the gateway rule that enforces the wording. Separate Managerial controls from the attendance sheet for the awareness exercise. Stop when Managerial controls are cited with signature and review date.',
        'exam': 'Managerial controls: A signed policy or a dated risk acceptance is managerial controls. Managerial controls: The gateway that carries out the text is not managerial controls. Managerial controls: The training drill is not managerial controls. Managerial controls: A meeting comment without a signature is not yet managerial controls. Managerial controls: Framework titles do not replace the adopted page under managerial controls. Managerial controls: If the stem shows an owner and a signature, choose managerial controls.',
    },
    'Operational controls': {
        'body': 'Operational controls are habits people carry out, such as awareness practice, a restore drill, a visitor log, or a talked-through tabletop. Operational controls matter because a polished policy still fails if nobody has rehearsed the motion before the bad Friday. Operational controls work by putting the habit on a calendar, doing it, and writing the result where the next shift can see it. Operational controls: Every Friday the service desk restores one payroll file into a sandbox and records the minutes required. Last Friday operational controls captured eighteen minutes from request to open file, with Priya Ortiz initialing drill sheet DR-18. Operational controls: Ticket 4602 asked whether the clinic actually practices restores, and the sheet was the evidence rather than the backup brochure. Operational controls: A poster that says practice restores is not operational controls until a person practices and leaves a note. Operational controls are distinct from the policy that ordered the Friday slot and named an owner for it. Operational controls are distinct from the backup software, the firewall, and the encryption that run even if the desk is absent. Operational controls: The product can be a device control while the rehearsal remains operational controls, and the stem chooses which fact it wants. Operational controls: Security awareness that includes a practiced phishing exercise with a completion record is operational controls. Operational controls: Reading a slide once with no roster is a weak substitute and usually fails as operational controls. Operational controls: A visitor log filled at the front desk, with time in and host name, is operational controls. Operational controls: The locked door beside that desk is a physical barrier and is not operational controls. Operational controls: Incident steps that staff have walked in a tabletop, with notes on who spoke, are operational controls. Operational controls: An alert that fired by itself is a device detection event and is not the drill called operational controls. Operational controls should record the time, the person, and the outcome, because a drill with blank lines cannot be shown later. Operational controls decay when the Friday slot is skipped for a month and nobody writes the miss. Operational controls: Ticket 4602 should list a skipped date as a failed operational controls check and then name the makeup Friday. Choose operational controls when the stem shows people performing a repeated security habit and keeping a record. Do not choose operational controls for a signature alone, and do not choose them for a firewall hit counter alone. Operational controls: If the stem says the team practiced and logged the result, operational controls is the label to write. Operational controls: If the stem says only that a standard exists, leave operational controls and name the written decision instead. Operational controls include a visitor-badge surrender habit at the end of the day when the log is actually marked. Operational controls include a quarterly call-tree test where the on-call person answers and the time is written down. Operational controls are evidenced by initials, dates, and results, not by the existence of a tool license.',
        'r0': 'Operational controls are human-executed practices with a recorded outcome, not a device acting alone. Operational controls include restore drills, visitor logs, awareness exercises, and tabletops that produce notes. Operational controls: A restore drill that logs eighteen minutes and Priya Ortiz initials is operational controls. Operational controls: The backup platform under that drill is a device setting and is not itself operational controls. Operational controls: Phishing practice with a completion roster is operational controls, while a single unread slide is not. Operational controls: The policy that mandated Friday practice is a written decision and is not operational controls. Choose operational controls when the stem shows the habit being performed and written down. Operational controls: A skipped month of drills is a failed operational controls check even if the software stayed licensed. Operational controls evidence is the sheet: person, date, and result.',
        'r1': 'Operational controls are habits people practice on purpose. Operational controls include a restore drill with notes. Operational controls include a visitor log at the desk. Operational controls include practice that staff repeat. Operational controls are more than a poster on a wall. Operational controls differ from a signed policy page. Operational controls differ from a machine that blocks alone. Operational controls should show who practiced and when. Operational controls fade when the team skips the day. Write operational controls so the next shift can see them. Operational controls: A licensed tool alone is not operational controls. Operational controls need a date and a result.',
        'r2': 'Operational controls resemble a night crew walking the same fire route until each turn feels dull. Operational controls are the walking, the headcount, and the note of how long the route took. Operational controls are not the framed order on the wall, and they are not a door that shuts by a spring. Operational controls: The first walk is clumsy, and later walks count as operational controls only if the crew still does them unseen. Operational controls: A skipped walk leaves a blank line, and that blank is how operational controls fail. Hold operational controls on the repeated human action and on the record of that action. Operational controls stay the picture of people moving and then writing the time. Operational controls: A shiny extinguisher that nobody has lifted is not operational controls.',
        'r4': 'Operational controls: Pull the Friday sheet and confirm Operational controls list minutes and initials. Operational controls: Repeat one sandbox restore and time the Operational controls steps yourself. Operational controls: Write the minute count beside Operational controls on ticket 4602. Check that Operational controls happened rather than that a poster exists. Separate Operational controls from the policy that ordered the Friday slot. Separate Operational controls from the backup product configuration screen. Stop when Operational controls evidence shows a person, a date, and a result.',
        'exam': 'Operational controls: People doing a repeated habit is operational controls. Operational controls: Training, restore drills, and visitor logs are the usual operational controls examples. Operational controls: The policy that orders the drill is not operational controls. Operational controls: The backup software is not operational controls. Operational controls: A poster nobody follows is not operational controls. Pick operational controls only when practice is evidenced. Operational controls: A license certificate does not prove operational controls.',
    },
    'Physical controls': {
        'body': 'Physical controls are tangible barriers and presence: a lock, a badge reader, a camera, a guard, a safe, or a cable lock. Physical controls matter because knowing the wireless password still does not open a closet that wants a badge. Physical controls work by putting matter or a person in the path, so the unwanted action has to defeat something you can touch. Physical controls: Ticket 4701 described a contractor who knew the server-closet code rumor and still could not enter, because the reader wanted a live badge. Physical controls on that closet are the reader, the strike, and the door, not the password stored in the directory. Physical controls: A camera in the hall is physical controls, and so is the guard who can walk over when the camera shows a prop against the frame. Physical controls differ from preventive timing, which says the control acts before harm, and from deterrent effect, which says it discourages a try. Physical controls: A badge reader can be physical controls and preventive at once, and the stem will say whether it wants the tangible layer or the timing. Physical controls: A visible camera dome can be physical controls and also a deterrent, while the recording it stores is often used as detection after the fact. Physical controls include locks on racks, port locks on switches, and safes for offline backup media. Physical controls include fencing, bollards, and a staffed desk, which later lessons split into their own names when the stem is that specific. For this label, physical controls are the broad category: real-world barriers rather than a policy, a drill, or a firewall rule. Wifi knowledge is not physical controls, and a shoulder-surfed password is not physical controls either. Physical controls fail when the door is propped, the reader is in fail-open, or a spare key sits on the lobby counter. Physical controls: Ticket 4701 should note whether the closet fails open or fails locked during a power cut, because that behavior is part of physical controls. Physical controls: Cable locks on clinic laptops are physical controls against a quick grab in an exam room. Physical controls: A safe with a dual custody pin for backup tapes is physical controls around the media, separate from the encryption on the tapes. Physical controls do not replace encryption, because a stolen drive can still be read if the volume was left open. Physical controls do not replace a visitor procedure, because a guard who never asks for a name is only furniture. Physical controls: Mantraps, now usually called access-control vestibules, are physical controls when the question stays at the category layer. Physical controls: If the stem names the two-door room specifically, use the narrower label, and keep physical controls for the general tangible class. Physical controls: Lighting that removes a hiding spot supports physical controls but is its own topic when the stem is about illumination. Physical controls evidence is a photo of the lock, a badge-deny event, or a guard report, not a firewall screenshot.',
        'r0': 'Physical controls are tangible measures: locks, badge readers, cameras, guards, safes, cable locks, and fences. Physical controls still stop a person who already knows a password, because the closet door is not the wireless network. Physical controls: A badge deny at the server closet is physical controls evidence on the door controller. Physical controls: Preventive and deterrent are timing or effect labels, while physical controls name the tangible category. A camera is physical controls, often detective when reviewed later and deterrent when obvious. Physical controls: Fail-secure doors stay locked on power loss, and fail-safe doors unlock, which matters for physical controls on exits. Encryption is not physical controls, though a safe holding tapes is physical controls around that media. Choose physical controls when the stem shows a barrier in the building rather than a packet filter. A propped door defeats physical controls until the habit and the closer are fixed.',
        'r1': 'Physical controls are real barriers you can touch. Physical controls include a lock on a door. Physical controls include a badge reader at a closet. Physical controls include a camera and a guard. Physical controls still work if someone knows a password. Physical controls are not a rule on paper. Physical controls are not a practice drill by themselves. A propped door defeats physical controls. Physical controls can be a safe or a cable lock. Physical controls: Look at the building when you name physical controls. Physical controls: A firewall rule is not physical controls. Physical controls stand in the way in the real room.',
        'r2': 'Physical controls resemble a ship hatch dogged down with metal handles rather than a note asking sailors to stay out. Physical controls keep the stores closed even for a crew member who knows every story told on deck. Physical controls are the hatch, the dogs, and the person posted beside them, not the standing order in a binder. Physical controls: Someone who memorizes a passphrase still meets physical controls at the steel. Physical controls: A camera looking at the hatch is physical controls of a watching kind, and it does not replace the dogs. Hold physical controls on things you could tap with a knuckle. Physical controls stay the picture of matter in the way. Physical controls: A paper list of names is not physical controls until a barrier or a person uses it at the hatch.',
        'r4': 'Physical controls: Photograph the closet door and attach it to Physical controls on ticket 4701. Physical controls: Export the badge-deny event that shows Physical controls refused the contractor. Note whether Physical controls fail locked or unlocked when power drops. Separate Physical controls from the wireless password the contractor already knew. Separate Physical controls from a preventive or deterrent label unless the stem asks timing. Check Physical controls for a propped door or a key left on the counter. Stop when Physical controls names the tangible barrier that actually stood in the way.',
        'exam': 'Physical controls: A lock, reader, camera, or guard is physical controls. Physical controls: A password or a firewall is not physical controls. Physical controls: Preventive means it stops a try, which can overlap physical controls but is a different axis. Physical controls: Deterrent means it discourages, which can overlap a visible camera that is still physical controls. Physical controls: If the stem stresses the tangible barrier, choose physical controls. Fail-open doors are a physical controls failure mode.',
    },
    'Preventive controls': {
        'body': "Preventive controls stop an unwanted action before that action succeeds, which is a timing claim rather than a claim about metal or software. Preventive controls matter because a careful log of a loss is a weak comfort when the payroll file is already gone. Preventive controls work by refusing the step, whether the refusal is a strike that stays shut or a rule that discards a packet. Preventive controls: Ticket 4814 followed a stranger at the clinic rack door whose badge came back unknown, so the strike never let go. Preventive controls on that reader ended the approach before a hand could reach a server, and the timestamp sits on the door controller. Preventive controls in that scene are also a tangible barrier, so a second question may want the physical category instead of the timing. Preventive controls are not deterrent controls, which try to make the attempt look expensive so many people never begin. Preventive controls: A painted warning with no working strike is deterrent in effect, and it is not preventive controls if a determined person can still enter. Preventive controls are not a smoke-style alarm that only shouts after the event, because the shout arrives too late to be this label. Preventive controls: An alert about a door that already stood open is detection, and it is not preventive controls. Preventive controls include a firewall deny, volume encryption that keeps plaintext unavailable, and an unused account that has been disabled. Preventive controls include inline prevention that drops a packet, while a listen-only sensor on a span port does not qualify. Preventive controls include a payment flow that refuses to let one clerk both create and approve the same transfer. Preventive controls collapse when a door is propped or when a broad allow is placed where a refusal used to sit. Preventive controls: Ticket 4814 should carry the badge-deny time as proof the refusal came first, not a video review written the next morning. Choose preventive controls when the evidence is a block, a refusal, or a lock that actually held. Leave preventive controls off the answer when the stem offers only a sign, a visible dome with no barrier, or a log read after the loss. Preventive controls can live in a device, in a building, or in a hard procedural refusal, so category and timing stay separate answers. Preventive controls: A warning banner at login is not preventive controls, because someone who ignores the text can still open the session. Preventive controls: An application allow list is preventive controls when it refuses an unknown program before that program can run. Preventive controls: Quarantine after an infection is repair work, and it is not preventive controls, since the bad action already landed. Preventive controls: Patching a flaw before any exploit is preventive controls, while the same patch after a breach is a cleanup. Preventive controls: Removing access on the contractor's last afternoon is preventive controls against a later login that should never exist.",
        'r0': 'Preventive controls are refusals that land before the unwanted action succeeds. Preventive controls include locks that hold, firewall denies, disabled accounts, and inline drops. Preventive controls: A badge reader that never releases the strike is preventive controls, whatever category name also fits. Preventive controls: Deterrent measures only discourage, so a sign without a refusal is not preventive controls. Preventive controls: Detective measures speak after the fact, so an alert on an event that already happened is not preventive controls. Listen-only sensors are not preventive controls, because they do not stop the packet. Patching before exploitation is preventive controls, and patching after compromise is repair. Choose preventive controls when the stem shows the harm did not finish because something refused it. Preventive controls: Encryption that denies plaintext is preventive controls for secrecy even if the device is stolen.',
        'r1': 'Preventive controls stop trouble before it starts. Preventive controls refuse the step itself. Preventive controls can be a lock that stays shut. Preventive controls can be a machine that says no. Preventive controls are stronger than a warning sign. Preventive controls are earlier than an alarm after the fact. Preventive controls: A sign alone is not preventive controls. Preventive controls: A video review the next day is not preventive controls. Preventive controls need a real refusal. Preventive controls: Point at the block when you say preventive controls. Preventive controls: Cleaning up later is not preventive controls. Preventive controls keep the bad act from finishing.',
        'r2': 'Preventive controls resemble a bicycle lock clicked shut before anyone can ride the bike away. Preventive controls do their job in the moment a hand tries the wheel, not after the rack is empty. Preventive controls: A barking dog next door may scare some people, yet preventive controls are the lock, not the noise. Preventive controls: A photo of the empty rack the next morning is a record, and it is not preventive controls. Hold preventive controls on the lock that was already closed. Preventive controls: If the lock was left open, preventive controls were not present, however bright the porch was. Preventive controls stay the picture of stopping the taking, not of scolding or of mopping up. Preventive controls: The owner who later buys a new bike is past the moment preventive controls could have helped.',
        'r4': 'Preventive controls: Pull the door-controller event for Preventive controls on ticket 4814. Confirm Preventive controls show an unknown badge and a strike that stayed closed. Preventive controls: Attach the timestamp that proves Preventive controls acted before entry. Separate Preventive controls from the hallway sign that only warns. Separate Preventive controls from next-morning video review. Note Preventive controls only if you can point at the refusal. Preventive controls: Stop when the badge-deny event is stored on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'Preventive controls: A refusal before success is preventive controls. Preventive controls: A sign or a visible camera without a barrier is not preventive controls. Preventive controls: An alert after the door opened is not preventive controls. Inline drops are preventive controls, and listen-only modes are not. Patch-before-exploit is preventive controls, and patch-after-breach is not. Preventive controls: The physical category can still be true of the same lock. Pick preventive controls when timing, not the material, is the question.',
    },
    'Deterrent controls': {
        'body': "Deterrent controls raise the apparent cost of a bad act so many people choose not to try, without necessarily making the act impossible. Deterrent controls matter because some losses never start when a passerby believes they will be seen, challenged, or punished. Deterrent controls work by being noticeable: a sign, a lit entrance, a camera dome people can see, a guard in view, or a warning banner. Deterrent controls: Ticket 4906 covered a side door where a sign, a dome, and a lamp over the frame led a passerby to keep walking at 01:10. Deterrent controls in that scene did not physically hold the person, and the door was still a normal latch a tool could defeat. The useful evidence for deterrent controls is that the approach stopped while the person was still outside, plus photos of the sign and the light. Deterrent controls are not preventive controls, which actually refuse the act, such as a reader that never releases a strike. Deterrent controls: If the passerby had forced the latch and the door still opened, deterrent controls would have failed and a preventive layer would be the missing piece. Deterrent controls are not the same thing as a hidden camera that nobody knows about, because a hidden camera does not change a stranger's choice in the moment. Deterrent controls: A hidden camera is mainly a later record, whereas deterrent controls depend on being perceived. Deterrent controls: A login banner that says only authorized people may continue is a classic deterrent controls example on a device. Deterrent controls: That banner does not stop a password that works, so it is not a refusal and it is not preventive controls. Visible guards are deterrent controls when their presence changes behavior, and they may also be preventive if they physically block a path. Deterrent controls: Lighting that removes a dark pocket is deterrent controls when it makes sneaking feel exposed, and it is not itself a lock. Deterrent controls: Signs that state trespass will be reported are deterrent controls, and they do nothing once someone has decided the sign is a bluff. Deterrent controls fail against a person who does not care about being seen, which is why they are paired with refusals rather than used alone. Deterrent controls: Ticket 4906 should not be labeled preventive merely because the person walked away, unless a barrier actually stopped them. Fake domes are still deterrent controls if people believe they are real, and they are a poor detective control because they record nothing. Deterrent controls: A fence people can see is partly deterrent controls and partly a delay, and the stem may want the specific fence topic instead. Deterrent controls: For a broad effect question, visible fencing, lighting, and signage often land on deterrent controls. Deterrent controls: Legal warnings on banners support later discipline, but the control type on the exam for the banner itself is deterrent controls. Do not pick deterrent controls for an access list that drops packets, because that drop is a refusal.",
        'r0': 'Deterrent controls discourage attempts by making the act look costly or visible. Deterrent controls: Signs, working lights, obvious camera domes, visible guards, and login banners are deterrent controls. Deterrent controls: A login banner does not block a valid password, so it is deterrent controls rather than a refusal. Hidden cameras are weak deterrent controls because the person may not know they exist. Deterrent controls: A badge reader that stays locked is a refusal, not deterrent controls, even if it also looks serious. Fake domes can be deterrent controls and cannot be a recording control. Choose deterrent controls when the stem stresses warning or visibility, not a packet drop. Deterrent controls fail against someone who ignores being seen, so they are not a complete design. Deterrent controls: Lighting that removes darkness supports deterrent controls at doors and fences.',
        'r1': 'Deterrent controls warn people away before they try. Deterrent controls can be a sign people can read. Deterrent controls can be a bright light over a door. Deterrent controls can be a camera people notice. Deterrent controls can be a guard standing where they show. Deterrent controls do not have to make the act impossible. Deterrent controls: A hidden watcher is weak as deterrent controls. Deterrent controls: A lock that truly holds is more than deterrent controls. Deterrent controls change a mind that cares about being seen. Deterrent controls: A bluff sign fails when someone stops believing deterrent controls. Use deterrent controls together with a real stop. Deterrent controls: Visibility is the heart of deterrent controls.',
        'r2': 'Deterrent controls resemble a bright lamp and a painted warning on a shed that makes a stranger decide the alley is a bad idea. Deterrent controls are the lamp and the warning, which work only while the stranger can see them and still cares. Deterrent controls: A lock on the shed is a different picture, and deterrent controls do not become that lock. Deterrent controls: A camera buried under the eaves that nobody notices is not the same picture as deterrent controls. Deterrent controls: If the lamp is dark, deterrent controls in this picture go quiet even if the paint remains. Hold deterrent controls on the choice the stranger makes before touching the door. Deterrent controls: Someone who ignores the lamp and the paint has walked past what deterrent controls can do. Deterrent controls: The picture stops at the discouraged approach, not at a bar that physically prevents entry.',
        'r4': 'Deterrent controls: Photograph the sign, dome, and lamp as Deterrent controls for ticket 4906. Deterrent controls: Confirm the lamp was lit at 01:10 so Deterrent controls were visible. Deterrent controls: Ask whether any barrier actually held, and keep that fact out of Deterrent controls. Deterrent controls: Note the login banner text if Deterrent controls are the device warning instead. Deterrent controls: Do not label a packet drop as Deterrent controls. Deterrent controls: Do not label next-day log review as Deterrent controls. Deterrent controls: Stop when visibility, not refusal, is the evidence for Deterrent controls.',
        'exam': 'Visible warning is deterrent controls. Deterrent controls: A hard refusal is not deterrent controls. Deterrent controls: A hidden sensor is a poor deterrent controls answer. Login banners are deterrent controls on most stems. Fake cameras can be deterrent controls and record nothing. Do not select deterrent controls for a firewall deny. Deterrent controls: Pairing a sign with a lock does not make the lock into deterrent controls.',
    },
    'Detective controls': {
        'body': "Discovery in this category begins after an action has already landed. The control raises a signal a person can investigate. It does not freeze the session, and it does not rebuild the host. What you can hold onto is the notice itself: a clock time, an account, and a fact that was easy to miss from the hallway. Without that notice, quiet misuse can continue through several shifts because nobody has a reason to open the record.\n\nA watcher and a condition do the work. Logs, door sensors, or recordings arrive where a rule can compare them, and a match opens an alert with a time attached. A sensor that only copies traffic still lets the traffic pass, so the record is the gain. A device that discards the packet is a refusal, even if it also writes a line, and that refusal wears a different label. Windows Security event 4624 can show that user m.okonkwo signed in successfully. A separate rule can decide the success is strange enough to page the on-call analyst. The page is the control you name. A folder of unread logs is only raw material until someone looks.\n\nThursday night produced ticket SEC-1844 at 04:12 UTC. The alert named m.okonkwo, source address 185.203.118.14, and a country that does not appear in that clinician's travel profile. The directory had already accepted the password. Analyst Priya copied the alert name, the source address, and the timestamp into the ticket before she touched the account. That sequence matters more than the theory. The signal proves a session existed. A password reset that follows is a later action with its own clock, and the write-up should not pretend the alert refused the login.\n\nGive the queue an owner. A page that nobody accepts is a control that fired and still helped no one. Directory clocks and alert clocks should agree, or the line in SEC-1844 cannot be tied to the sign-in record. Once a quarter, a lab account should sign in from a flagged address so a silent rule is not mistaken for a calm night. After the signal is confirmed, isolation and repair keep their own names. Leave the alert described as discovery, then record who accepted the page and how many minutes passed.\n\nStudents swap this label with preventive, corrective, compensating, and directive controls. A reader that never released the door strike stopped the approach, so it is not discovery after the fact. Restoring the prior night's clean server image repairs damage, so that restore is a cleanup. A jump host placed around an old billing application was chosen because a second factor would not fit, which is a stand-in decided before any incident. A procedure that demands a change number before a firewall edit is an order to a person, not news that an edit already occurred. Time is the separating fact. This control speaks after the event, and the ticket should contain the alert that shows the order of events.\n",
        'r0': 'This control raises a signal after an event, later than a refusal and earlier than a repair. Useful forms include a SIEM page, a listen-only sensor, a log review, and a camera review of a door already propped. Impossible travel is one such signal when two sign-ins imply a speed no traveler could meet. Windows Security event 4624 can feed the rule when the sign-in succeeded, while event 4625 counts failures. An inline drop is not this control, because the packet never arrives. A restore from the prior night is not this control either. Ticket SEC-1844 shows the shape: user m.okonkwo, source 185.203.118.14, 04:12 UTC, country absent from the profile. Choose this label when the evidence is an alert, a log hit, or a recording of what already occurred. A queue with no owner wastes the page.\n',
        'r1': 'This kind of control speaks after something already happened. It raises a signal a person can check. A strange sign-in alert fits this idea. A next-morning camera review fits it too. The signal does not lock the door. The signal does not rebuild the server. A refusal that held is a different idea. A cleanup after damage is a different idea. A written order is a different idea. Someone must read the page or it sits unused. Write down the time on the alert. Point at the notice when you name this control.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a smoke alarm in a clinic break-room kitchen. The alarm shouts after a pan has already started to scorch. It does not pour water, and it does not stop the next cook from lighting a match. Its value is the shout in time for someone to walk over. A lock on the supply closet is a different object in that hallway. A mop after the scorch mark is a different object too. A card that orders staff to turn the burner off is only an instruction. In the real control, the page about m.okonkwo is the shout, not the lock and not the restore. If nobody walks over when that page arrives, the night does not change.\n',
        'r4': 'Open ticket SEC-1844 and copy the user, source address, and timestamp. Match the alert to the directory sign-in before you name a cause. Confirm the session already existed when the page fired. Leave any later password reset in a separate note. Do not describe the alert as a block. Do not describe a restore as this same control. Stop when the signal and its clock time are both on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'An alert after the event is this control. A lock that held is not. A restore after damage is not. A jump-host stand-in chosen beforehand is not. A mandatory procedure is not. Listen-only capture fits this label, and an inline drop does not. An ignored queue makes the signal useless in practice. Time order is the fact that separates the neighbors.\n',
    },
    'Corrective controls': {
        'body': "Corrective controls repair damage or return a system to a known-good state after something has already gone wrong. Corrective controls matter because detection alone leaves the clinic unable to pay staff if the payroll server stays encrypted. Corrective controls work by restoring clean copies, resetting exposed accounts, rebuilding hosts, or failing over to a spare that still works. Corrective controls on ticket 5120 began after ransomware notes appeared, when the team restored last night's clean image and reset the affected accounts. Corrective controls in that ticket are the restore and the resets, not the alert that first named the ransom note. Corrective controls are later than detective controls, which only raised the signal that the notes were on disk. Corrective controls are not compensating controls, which would have been a stand-in chosen before the incident because a preferred safeguard was missing. Corrective controls are not directive controls, which would be the written order telling people which restore steps to follow. Corrective controls: A mop after a spill is the right timing picture for corrective controls, and the mop does not prevent the next spill by itself. Corrective controls: Restoring from a backup that was made before the encryption is the textbook corrective controls move. Corrective controls: Reimaging a laptop from a known-good build is corrective controls when the machine was already compromised. Corrective controls: Resetting passwords and revoking sessions after a theft is corrective controls for the accounts that were exposed. Corrective controls: Patching a hole after attackers used it is corrective controls, while patching the same hole before any exploit is a refusal done earlier. Corrective controls: Failover to a warm spare can be corrective controls when it returns service after the primary has failed. Corrective controls fail when the only backup is also encrypted or when nobody rehearsed the restore path. Corrective controls: Ticket 5120 should record which backup generation was clean, who reset the accounts, and how long users were down. Corrective controls match stems about cleanup, restore, or a return to a trusted state. Corrective controls are the wrong label for the page that merely announced the ransom note. Corrective controls are the wrong label for a jump host that limited an old application before any incident. Corrective controls: Quarantine of a malicious file after it ran is often taught as corrective controls because it limits ongoing damage.",
        'r0': 'Corrective controls return a known-good state after an unwanted event has already succeeded. Corrective controls: Backups restored from a pre-incident copy, password resets, reimaging, and failover are corrective controls. Patching after exploitation is corrective controls, whereas patching before exploitation is a preventive timing label. Corrective controls: The alert that named the ransom note is detective controls, not corrective controls. Corrective controls: A documented stand-in such as a jump host is compensating controls, not corrective controls. Corrective controls: The written restore procedure is directive controls, not the restore action itself. Choose corrective controls when the stem shows repair, restore, quarantine after execution, or a return to trust. Corrective controls fail if the backup generation is also encrypted. Corrective controls: Ticket 5120 evidence is the clean image id, the account resets, and the elapsed downtime. Corrective controls: Warm-spare failover that restores service is corrective controls when it happens after the failure.',
        'r1': 'Corrective controls clean up after the mess. Corrective controls bring back a good copy. Corrective controls can reset accounts that were exposed. Corrective controls happen after the bad act. Corrective controls are not the alarm that only shouted. Corrective controls are not a backup plan written on paper. Corrective controls are not a substitute lock chosen earlier. Corrective controls: A mop is the picture for corrective controls. An untouched spill means corrective controls have not started. Corrective controls: Name the restore when you mean corrective controls. Corrective controls: A warning sign is not corrective controls. Rehearse corrective controls before you need the mop.',
        'r2': 'Corrective controls resemble a mop and bucket brought out only after soup has already hit the floor. Corrective controls do not stop the bowl from tipping, and they do not shout when the bowl leaves the counter. The job of corrective controls is to return the floor to a walkable state. Corrective controls: A bell that rang as the bowl slipped is a different picture and is not corrective controls. Corrective controls: A second tray used because the first tray was cracked last week is a different picture and is not corrective controls. Corrective controls: A card that orders staff to mop is a different picture and is not corrective controls. Hold corrective controls on the cleanup that follows the spill. Corrective controls: If the bucket is empty, corrective controls cannot finish no matter how loud the bell was.',
        'r4': 'Corrective controls: Identify the last clean image before applying Corrective controls on ticket 5120. Corrective controls: Restore that image and record the generation id under Corrective controls. Corrective controls: Reset exposed accounts and list them beside Corrective controls. Separate Corrective controls from the alert that only announced the note. Separate Corrective controls from the written mop-up procedure. Corrective controls: Time how long users lacked service while Corrective controls ran. Corrective controls: Stop when the clean state and the account resets are both on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'Corrective controls: A restore after harm is corrective controls. Corrective controls: The alert that revealed the harm is not corrective controls. Corrective controls: A pre-planned jump host is not corrective controls. Corrective controls: A procedure page is not corrective controls. Patch-after-breach is corrective controls, and patch-before-exploit is not. Corrective controls: Quarantine after execution is usually corrective controls. Corrective controls: A backup that is also encrypted cannot serve corrective controls.',
    },
    'Compensating controls': {
        'body': 'When the safeguard you wanted cannot be installed yet, another control has to carry the same goal by a different restraint. That stand-in is what this label names. It is chosen before an incident, because a gap is already known, and it should say plainly that the preferred safeguard is still missing. Pretending the original control is present is how these write-ups fail an assessor.\n\nThe work starts from the objective, not from the gadget. Write what the missing control was supposed to achieve, why it will not fit, what restraint you added, who owns it, and the date you will look again. Payment-card reviews expect that set of facts when a required control is impractical. Extra logging around a system you cannot patch counts only if a person actually reads those logs against the original goal. A note that says be careful, with no restraint behind it, does not qualify.\n\nMedBill 4.2 is the clinic billing application, and its sign-in screen cannot prompt a second factor. Engineers proved that limit in a test tenant. Change CHG-2190 then allowed the application only from admin jump host jh-adm-01 at 10.20.8.15, and the firewall drops every other source. The review date on the exception is 30 June 2026. The jump host is the stand-in. It is not multi-factor logic inside the old program, and the ticket should not describe it that way. A tester who tries the billing address from a nursing-station PC should see the connection fail.\n\nKeep the exception alive on purpose. Before the review date, either install a real second factor or renew the write-up with fresh evidence that the path is still tight. A jump host that was temporary in March should not still be the only story in December with no owner. If staff begin using a second admin workstation because the first one is slow, the restraint has quietly widened. Defenders recheck the firewall rule, the account list on the jump host, and the logs of who signed in there.\n\nStudents call an after-the-fact page this kind of control, but a page only notices misuse and does not stand in for the missing prompt. A restore after ransomware is cleanup, not a substitute chosen because a control would not fit. A poster that tells staff to avoid the old application is an instruction, not an enforced path. The separating fact is the gap you already knew about: the preferred control cannot be used yet, and a different restraint is doing its job until a dated review.\n',
        'r0': 'Use this label when a preferred safeguard cannot be implemented yet and a different restraint carries the same objective. MedBill 4.2 cannot prompt a second factor, so CHG-2190 allows it only from jump host 10.20.8.15, with a review on 30 June 2026. That host does not become multi-factor code inside the old binary. Card-data reviews expect the objective, the constraint, the substitute, and the upkeep. An alert, a post-incident restore, and a posted slogan are different labels. Extra logging qualifies only when someone reviews it against the original goal. An exception with no owner and no end date turns into a permanent hole. Ticket evidence is the missing prompt, the single source address, and the review date.\n',
        'r1': 'This control fills in when the lock you wanted will not fit. It is a stand-in, not the original lock. Name why the first choice failed. Name the restraint you added instead. Name who watches that restraint. Name the date you will look again. A guard at a door under repair can be this idea. An alarm after the fact is not this idea. A cleanup after a break-in is not this idea. A poster that says be careful is not this idea. Do not pretend the missing lock is already there.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a loading-dock door at a clinic warehouse while the badge reader is still on order. A guard and a sign-in clipboard stand in the doorway during receiving hours, and a chain holds the door after the shift. The clipboard is not the reader that was ordered. It is a brace with a date beside it. A bell that rings after someone is already inside is a different picture. A mop after mud is tracked across the floor is a different picture too. On the real application, the jump host is that brace, and the June review is the date the reader is supposed to arrive. If nobody checks the brace, the stand-in has stopped doing the job.\n',
        'r4': 'Name the preferred safeguard that will not fit before you use this label. Record jump host 10.20.8.15 as the stand-in on CHG-2190. Set the 30 June 2026 review and assign an owner. Separate this write-up from alerts that only notice misuse. Separate it from any restore that happens after damage. Attach the reason the second factor cannot be prompted. Stop when the gap, the restraint, and the review date are all on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A stand-in for a missing preferred control is this label. An alert is not. A restore is not. A poster is not. Jump-host-only access for an old application that cannot prompt a second factor is the classic stem. A write-up and an end date belong with a defensible answer. Do not describe the stand-in as if the preferred control were already installed.\n',
    },
    'Directive controls': {
        'body': 'Some controls exist as orders. They tell a person the step that is required, in a procedure, on a posted card, or in a change path, and they expect the person to obey. The control is the instruction. It is not the firewall that may also refuse a packet, and it is not the log that later shows who typed anyway.\n\nThe order has to be specific enough to follow and visible before the action. A sentence that says must is stronger than a sentence that says should. The page needs an owner and a place the night shift actually opens. If the only copy sits in a folder nobody uses, the order is theoretical. Awareness classes that people are required to attend are orders of this kind. The roster that proves they sat in the room is a separate fact about a habit being practiced.\n\nSOP-CHG-04 is the clinic change procedure. It states that a firewall edit is forbidden until a change number is written on the ticket. On CHG-2301 the night engineer quoted that sentence in the work note and waited for the number CAB-7741 before opening the ruleset. The quote is the evidence for this label. The later firewall log, which shows the edit at 22:16, is a record of what was typed. It does not replace the sentence that told the engineer to wait. A banner that only says authorized use may continue is a warning, and it becomes this kind of control only if it commands a specific act, such as locking the screen when you leave.\n\nDefenders test the order the way they test a device rule: can the person who must obey it find the current sentence, and does that sentence still match the work. When remote wipe becomes mandatory, the card that still says optional is a failed order even if a console setting changed. Quote the must sentence and the document revision in the ticket. Do not cite a hit counter as if it were the instruction.\n\nStudents see a policy page and want one label for every axis. The same page can be a governed decision because a leader signed it, and it can still be an order because it tells staff what they must do. The stem chooses the axis. A device that refuses the edit is not the order. An alert about an edit with no ticket is discovery after the fact. Rolling the ruleset back is cleanup. A jump host around an old application is a stand-in. The separating fact is the instruction itself: if the evidence you can point at is a mandatory sentence, this is the label.\n',
        'r0': 'This control is a mandatory instruction: a procedure, a posted order, or a required step such as a change number before a firewall edit. SOP-CHG-04 forbids the edit until the number is on the ticket, and CHG-2301 quotes that sentence before CAB-7741. The firewall log at 22:16 is not the instruction. Alerts, restores, and jump-host stand-ins are different labels. A signed policy can be both a governed document and an order, and the stem picks the axis. Must language points here, while should language is a weaker guide. A required class is an order, separate from the attendance roster of the habit. A login banner that only warns is usually a deterrent unless it commands a specific act. Evidence is the sentence people are supposed to obey.\n',
        'r1': 'This control tells people what they must do. It is an order, not a hint. A required step on a card fits. A posted instruction fits too. The words expect a person to obey. A machine that blocks is not this order. A late alarm is not this order. A cleanup afterward is not this order. A stand-in lock is not this order. Read for the word must. An old page sends people the wrong way. Point at the sentence when you use this label.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a warehouse pick card that tells the clerk to scan the bin before lifting the box. The card is the order, and the job counts on the clerk to follow it. The card does not grab the clerk's wrists. A buzzer after the wrong box is on the pallet is a different picture. Repacking the pallet later is a different picture too. A temporary cage used because the scanner is broken is yet another picture. On the real change path, the sentence that demands a ticket number is that pick card. If the card says should instead of must, the order has gone soft.\n",
        'r4': 'Quote the must sentence from SOP-CHG-04 on ticket CHG-2301. Record the document revision beside that quote. Check that the night engineer can open the current page before the edit. Keep the firewall log in a separate note from the instruction. Keep any alert about a missing ticket in a separate note. Keep a ruleset rollback in a separate note. Stop when the ticket cites the order itself.\n',
        'exam': 'A mandatory instruction is this label. A device refusal is not. An alert after an edit is not. A restore is not. A stand-in control is not. Must versus should is the language test. The same signed page can still be a governed document when the stem asks who owns the decision. A login banner that only warns is a deterrent unless it commands a specific act. Quote the must sentence, not a firewall hit counter.\n',
    },
    'Confidentiality': {
        'body': "Confidentiality means only approved readers can view the data, which is a different claim from whether the data was altered or reachable. Confidentiality matters because a payroll workbook in the wrong mailbox exposes salaries even when every total is still accurate and the site is up. Confidentiality fails in ticket 5510 when a clerk forwards the payroll sheet to a personal mailbox outside the clinic directory. Confidentiality in that ticket is broken by disclosure, not by a changed number and not by a slow page. Confidentiality is protected by limiting who can open the file, by encrypting it at rest, and by encrypting it while it moves. Confidentiality at rest on clinic laptops is commonly AES-256 full-disk encryption, which withholds plaintext without the volume key. Confidentiality in transit is commonly TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3, so a browser session rides HTTPS rather than cleartext HTTP. Confidentiality for file transfer belongs on SFTP or another protected channel, not on plain FTP that anyone on the path can read. Confidentiality attacks include shoulder surfing, dumpster diving, eavesdropping on an open network, and social tricks that ask an employee to read a secret aloud. Confidentiality tools include data loss prevention on the mail gateway, which can block the payroll sheet before it leaves. Confidentiality also depends on need-to-know, so a nurse who can open a chart still should not open a neighbor's payroll row. Confidentiality is not integrity, which would ask whether the salary figures were edited after the manager approved them. Confidentiality is not the question of whether the payroll site answered in time for Friday close. Confidentiality: Hashing a file with SHA-256 does not provide confidentiality, because a hash is not a secret and does not hide the bytes. Steganography can support confidentiality by hiding the fact that a message exists, which is different from encrypting a message everyone can see is there. A shared mailbox login destroys confidentiality boundaries, because everyone with the password is an approved reader in practice. Confidentiality regulations for health details and for personal data punish unauthorized disclosure, which is why the personal mailbox is not a harmless shortcut. Confidentiality: Ticket 5510 should name the file, the sender, the personal destination, and whether the gateway blocked or missed the forward. Confidentiality is the label when the stem shows someone saw data they were not approved to see. Confidentiality is the wrong label when the stem shows a total was changed, or when the stem shows the site was too slow to use. Encryption supports confidentiality, and authenticated modes such as AES-GCM also help integrity, so read which harm the question describes. Confidentiality: Screen privacy filters and clean-desk habits support confidentiality against shoulder surfing and leftover printouts. Dumpster diving attacks confidentiality of paper, so a shredder is part of the same goal as the mail control. Write confidentiality when the evidence is unwanted reading, copying, or forwarding. Confidentiality can be lost even though the original file is unchanged and the service never went down. Keep the three ideas apart: confidentiality is who may see it.",
        'r0': 'Confidentiality restricts reading to approved parties. Confidentiality: AES-256 at rest and TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3 in transit are standard confidentiality mechanisms. HTTPS and SFTP support confidentiality, while cleartext HTTP and FTP expose payloads to anyone on the path. SHA-256 hashes do not provide confidentiality. Confidentiality: Shoulder surfing, dumpster diving, eavesdropping, and mailbox forwarding are confidentiality failures. Data loss prevention can enforce confidentiality on outbound mail. Need-to-know is a confidentiality rule inside an already authenticated workforce. Confidentiality: Integrity concerns alteration, and availability concerns usable service, so neither replaces confidentiality. Ticket 5510 is a confidentiality case because the payroll sheet reached a personal mailbox. Confidentiality: Steganography hides existence, which is related to confidentiality and is not the same as a hash. Choose confidentiality when the harm is unauthorized viewing or copying.',
        'r1': 'Confidentiality means only the right people may see it. Confidentiality breaks when a secret reaches a stranger. Confidentiality can fail by a sheet in the wrong mailbox. Confidentiality is about seeing, not about changing. Confidentiality is about seeing, not about a slow page. Confidentiality: A lock on a diary is a picture of confidentiality. A shredder supports confidentiality for paper scraps. Peeking over a shoulder attacks confidentiality. Confidentiality still matters if the numbers stayed correct. Confidentiality: Hiding a note is part of confidentiality. A shared mailbox weakens confidentiality. Confidentiality: Ask who was allowed to look before naming confidentiality.',
        'r2': "Confidentiality resembles a diary closed with a clasp so a roommate cannot read the page. Confidentiality is the clasp and the habit of shutting it, not a promise that the sentences inside never change. Confidentiality: If someone photographs the open page, confidentiality is already lost even though the ink is untouched. Confidentiality: A torn page would be a different harm, and confidentiality is not that tear. Confidentiality: A diary locked in a drawer that nobody can reach during the day raises a different concern about getting work done. Hold confidentiality on who is allowed to read. Confidentiality: The clasp does not prove who wrote a line, and confidentiality does not try to settle that argument. Confidentiality: When the diary is copied into someone else's bag, confidentiality is the harm to name.",
        'r4': 'Trace Confidentiality on ticket 5510 from the payroll sheet to the personal mailbox. Confidentiality: Record whether a gateway rule stopped or missed that Confidentiality failure. Confidentiality: Note the people approved to read the sheet before judging Confidentiality. Separate Confidentiality from any edited total in the same workbook. Separate Confidentiality from any slowness of the payroll site. Confidentiality: Attach the sender, the destination, and the time beside Confidentiality. Confidentiality: Stop when the evidence is unwanted reading rather than a changed value or an outage.',
        'exam': 'Unauthorized viewing is confidentiality. A changed total is not confidentiality. Confidentiality: A site that is up but unusably slow is not confidentiality. A hash does not create confidentiality. AES-256 and TLS protect confidentiality, and they are not labels for integrity by themselves. Confidentiality: Shoulder surfing and dumpster diving target confidentiality. Confidentiality: Forwarding payroll to a personal mailbox is confidentiality loss even when the file is unaltered.',
    },
    'Integrity': {
        'body': "Integrity means the contents stayed as an approved person left them, so a later reader can trust that nobody slipped in a quiet edit. Integrity matters because an invoice total changed after approval can move money while the document still looks normal to a hurried clerk. Integrity on ticket 5622 failed when the total on an approved invoice was edited from 18400 to 81400 after the manager had signed. Integrity in that ticket is about the altered bytes, not about who was allowed to see the invoice and not about whether the portal was online. Integrity is checked with cryptographic hashes such as SHA-256 or SHA-3, which change when even one bit of the file changes. Integrity checks also use HMAC when both sides share a key, so an outsider cannot forge a matching tag as easily as with a bare hash. Integrity plus a claim about who produced the bytes is what a digital signature adds, using the signer's private key. Integrity: MD5 and SHA-1 are the wrong tools for integrity disputes on the exam, because practical collisions exist. Integrity: A cyclic redundancy check can catch accidental line noise and is not a serious integrity control against an attacker who can recompute it. File integrity monitoring, such as AIDE comparing hashes to a baseline, is how hosts notice integrity drift in system files. Integrity is not confidentiality, which would hide the invoice from unapproved readers rather than prove it was unaltered. Integrity is not the question of whether the accounts-payable site opened when the clerk needed it. Integrity: AES-GCM can support both secrecy and integrity, but a hash alone never hides the document. Integrity: An attacker who changes the total and recomputes a published MD5 has not been stopped by that weak integrity check. Digital signatures support integrity and also support non-repudiation, so if the stem asks who cannot deny the approval, do not stop at integrity. Integrity: Ticket 5622 should show the original approved total, the altered total, and the hash mismatch or the missing signature. Integrity is the label when the stem shows unauthorized modification, a failed hash, or a broken seal on the data. Integrity is the wrong label for a file that was merely copied to a personal mailbox with the bytes unchanged. Integrity is the wrong label for a portal that timed out during Friday close. Integrity: Salami-style small edits and man-in-the-middle changes to a message are integrity attacks when the content no longer matches what was sent. Write integrity when you can point at a change that an approved person did not make. Integrity baselines must themselves be stored where the attacker cannot quietly update the expected hash. Protect the baseline or the integrity monitor will bless the attacker's version. Integrity: Keep the invoice story focused on the edited total, which is the heart of integrity.",
        'r0': 'Integrity means approved content was not altered by anyone else. SHA-256 and SHA-3 are appropriate integrity hashes, while MD5 and SHA-1 are collision-prone choices. Integrity: HMAC adds a shared key so a bare hash cannot be swapped as easily. Digital signatures bind integrity to a private key and also support non-repudiation. Integrity: CRC detects accidents and is not an attacker-resistant integrity control. AIDE-style file integrity monitoring compares live hashes to a protected baseline. Integrity: Confidentiality is about readers, and availability is about usable service, so neither is integrity. Integrity: AES-GCM authenticates ciphertext, but a hash by itself does not provide confidentiality. Ticket 5622 is integrity because the approved invoice total changed. Choose integrity when the stem shows modification, a hash mismatch, or a broken data seal. Store integrity baselines where the same attacker cannot rewrite them.',
        'r1': 'Integrity means the message arrives as it was sent. Integrity fails when someone edits a total after approval. Integrity is about change, not about who may look. Integrity is about change, not about a closed shop. Integrity: A sealed bag is a picture of integrity. A broken seal means integrity is in doubt. A matching seal supports integrity. Integrity: Copying a page without editing it is not an integrity miss. Integrity: A slow counter does not by itself break integrity. Integrity: Check the numbers against the approved copy for integrity. Integrity needs a trustworthy record of the original. Do not call mere secrecy integrity.',
        'r2': 'Integrity resembles a sealed evidence bag that shows whether someone opened it after the clerk sealed the mouth. Integrity is the seal, not the question of who is allowed to read the label on the outside. Integrity: If the seal is torn and the paper inside has a new total, integrity is what failed. Integrity: A stranger reading the label through the plastic is a different harm and is not integrity. Integrity: A bag that never arrives at the lab is a different harm and is not integrity. Hold integrity on whether the contents still match what was sealed. Integrity: A seal that the same suspect can reprint at home is a weak picture of integrity. Integrity: When the receiving clerk compares the seal to the log and they disagree, integrity is the finding.',
        'r4': 'Integrity: Compare the approved total with the current total for Integrity on ticket 5622. Integrity: Hash the invoice with SHA-256 and match it to the stored Integrity baseline. Record the mismatch beside Integrity before discussing who could see the file. Separate Integrity from a forward of an unchanged sheet. Separate Integrity from a portal outage. Protect the Integrity baseline from the same accounts that edit invoices. Integrity: Stop when the evidence is an unapproved change rather than a disclosure or downtime.',
        'exam': 'Integrity: An edited total after approval is integrity. Integrity: An unchanged file in the wrong mailbox is not integrity. An unreachable site is not integrity. MD5 and SHA-1 are weak integrity choices on the exam. Integrity: SHA-256, HMAC, and digital signatures are the integrity tools to remember. CRC is not a strong integrity control against an attacker. Integrity: A signature question about denying an action later is non-repudiation, not only integrity.',
    },
    'Availability': {
        'body': 'Availability means an approved person can use the service at the moment the work requires it, including at a usable speed. Availability matters because a payroll site that answers but takes minutes per click still blocks Friday timesheets. Availability failed on ticket 5730 when the payroll site stayed technically up while submissions timed out all Friday afternoon. Availability in that ticket is about usable service, not about whether salaries were leaked and not about whether a total was edited. Availability is built with redundancy, such as RAID, paired servers, load balancers, and a second network path. Availability from power protection uses an uninterruptible power supply for short cuts and a generator for longer ones. Availability: RAID 1, RAID 5, RAID 6, and RAID 10 are availability designs for disks, and none of them is a backup. Availability: A backup is a separate copy you can restore, which supports recovery, while RAID only helps when a disk fails inside the running set. Availability targets are often stated as RTO, how long a restore may take, and RPO, how much recent work may be lost. Availability: MTBF describes how long a component tends to run before failure, and MTTR describes how long repair tends to take. Availability is attacked by denial-of-service floods, including SYN floods and traffic that simply fills a link. Availability is not confidentiality, which would be a payroll sheet sent to a personal mailbox. Availability is not integrity, which would be an invoice total changed after approval. Availability: A control that locks a clinic door all morning can protect secrets and still destroy availability of care, so say which goal the stem is grading. Availability: Ticket 5730 should include the timeout rate, the hour it started, and whether a dependency such as the identity service was the bottleneck. Choose availability when users cannot complete the real task in the required time, even if a monitoring page says the process is running. Availability: Choose a different label when the process is fast but the data was exposed or altered. Load balancing and clustering raise availability by letting another node take work. Availability: Hot, warm, and cold recovery sites are availability and recovery choices with different time and cost, and the stem will hint how ready the spare site is. Backups support availability only after someone restores them, which is why an untested backup is not proof the service can return. Write availability when the harm is delay, outage, or unusable performance. Capacity exhaustion is an availability failure even without an attacker, and a flood is an availability attack with an attacker. Availability: Keep Friday timesheets in the story, because slowness that blocks the job is still an availability miss. Availability: Do not treat a green dashboard as availability if the business action cannot finish. Availability planning should name a person who can declare the service usable again after a restore.',
        'r0': 'Availability means the approved service is usable when the work needs it, not merely that a process is listed as running. Unusable slowness is an availability failure, which ticket 5730 showed on Friday payroll submissions. Availability: RAID 1, RAID 5, RAID 6, and RAID 10 raise disk availability and are not backups. UPS units and generators protect availability against power loss on different time scales. Availability: RTO is the allowed recovery time, and RPO is the allowed data loss window. Availability: MTBF and MTTR describe typical time between failures and typical time to repair. Denial-of-service and SYN floods attack availability. Availability: Confidentiality is disclosure, and integrity is unauthorized change, so neither is availability. Availability: Load balancers, clusters, and alternate sites support availability with different readiness. Availability: An untested backup does not prove availability can be restored. Availability: A lock that blocks legitimate work can harm availability while helping secrecy.',
        'r1': 'Availability means the needed work can finish on time. Availability fails when the page is too slow to finish. Availability fails when the service is down. A spare part supports availability. Availability: A battery for short power cuts supports availability. Availability: A second copy you can restore supports getting availability back. Availability: Seeing a secret is not an availability problem. Availability: Changing a total is not an availability problem. Availability: A green light can lie about availability. Availability: Time the real task before you claim availability. Availability: A locked front door can hurt availability of care. Availability: Ask whether the work could be finished.',
        'r2': 'Availability resembles a drinking fountain that must actually run when the lunch line arrives. Availability is the water coming out in time, not a sign that says the fountain exists. Availability: If the fountain trickles so slowly that nobody can drink before the bell, availability has failed. Availability: A fountain that sprays private notes onto the floor is a different harm and is not availability. Availability: A fountain whose instruction plaque was rewritten is a different harm and is not availability. Hold availability on whether the approved person can finish the drink when it matters. Availability: A second fountain in the next hall raises availability if people can reach it. Availability: An empty tank with a beautiful handle is not availability, however new the metal looks.',
        'r4': 'Availability: Time a real payroll submission before calling the state Availability on ticket 5730. Availability: Record timeouts and the clock hour beside Availability. Availability: Check whether identity or another dependency caused the Availability miss. Separate Availability from any leaked spreadsheet. Separate Availability from any edited invoice total. Availability: Note RAID or backup facts only if they explain the Availability gap. Availability: Stop when the evidence is unusable service rather than disclosure or tampering.',
        'exam': 'Unusable slowness is an availability failure even if the process looks up. A leaked file is not availability. An edited total is not availability. Availability: RAID is not a backup and is not proof of availability recovery. RTO and RPO are the availability timing terms. Denial-of-service attacks hit availability. Availability: A control that blocks legitimate work can damage availability.',
    },
    'Non-repudiation': {
        'body': "Non-repudiation means the actor cannot credibly deny the act later because the proof is bound to that actor alone. Non-repudiation matters when a payment approval is disputed and the clinic needs more than a shared password on a team account. Non-repudiation on ticket 5804 is the signed approval that names which manager released the vendor payment. Non-repudiation in that record uses a digital signature made with the manager's private key, so coworkers who lack the key cannot forge the same signature. Non-repudiation is weak if the private key is shared, exported to a common laptop, or left without a passphrase. Non-repudiation is also weak when several people use one login, because the log then proves only that the shared name was used. Non-repudiation from a symmetric message code is shaky, since both sides hold the same secret and either side could have produced the tag. Non-repudiation is not authentication, which answers who is at the door right now rather than who must stand behind yesterday's approval. Non-repudiation is not authorization, which answers whether that person was allowed to release payments at all. Non-repudiation: A signature can support integrity and non-repudiation together, and the stem that asks whether the manager can shrug the payment off wants non-repudiation. Non-repudiation evidence should include the signed object, the time, and the certificate that links the public key to the manager. Non-repudiation fails if the certificate was expired, revoked, or issued to a different person than the ticket claims. Trusted timestamps help non-repudiation because a signature without a reliable clock can be argued to have been made at some other hour. Non-repudiation logs that an admin can edit afterward are poor proof, so the approval record should be tamper-evident. Non-repudiation: Ticket 5804 should show the signature validation result and the key identifier, not merely a screenshot of a green button. Choose non-repudiation when the dispute is about denying an action after the fact. Non-repudiation: Choose authentication when the dispute is about how someone proved identity during the session. Non-repudiation: Choose authorization when the dispute is about whether the role included the payment permission. Non-repudiation: Shared mailboxes and break-glass accounts need extra care or they will hollow out non-repudiation for the actions taken under them. Write non-repudiation only when the proof would still identify one actor if that actor tried to deny the event. Non-repudiation is a property of the evidence, not a feeling that the right person probably clicked. Non-repudiation: Keep the payment story pointed at the signature, which is the core of non-repudiation. Non-repudiation planning includes who holds the private key and how a departure revokes it the same day. Non-repudiation reviews should sample old approvals and confirm the public key still chains to a trusted issuer. Non-repudiation checks must fail if the signed bytes and the signature do not match.",
        'r0': "Non-repudiation binds an action to one actor so a later denial is not credible. Non-repudiation: Digital signatures with a private key are the usual non-repudiation mechanism. A symmetric tag is weak non-repudiation because both parties can create it. Shared accounts destroy non-repudiation even when the log line looks complete. Non-repudiation: Authentication proves identity in the moment, and authorization decides permission, so neither replaces non-repudiation. Certificate expiry or revocation undermines non-repudiation of a signature. Trusted time strengthens non-repudiation of when the signature was made. Ticket 5804 is non-repudiation because a named manager's signature released the payment. Editable logs are poor non-repudiation evidence. Choose non-repudiation when the stem asks who cannot deny the act later.",
        'r1': 'Non-repudiation lets you show who did it later. Non-repudiation ties the act to one person. Non-repudiation fails if many people share one login. Non-repudiation is about later proof, not the door check. Non-repudiation is not the question of what the person may do. A unique signature supports non-repudiation. Non-repudiation: A shared secret that both sides know weakens non-repudiation. Non-repudiation: Keep the proof where nobody can quietly rewrite it. Non-repudiation matters when someone says they never approved. Non-repudiation: Ask which person must stand behind the act. Non-repudiation: A green button alone is weak non-repudiation. Non-repudiation: Name the signer when you claim non-repudiation.',
        'r2': "Non-repudiation resembles a delivery slip signed in ink that names the courier and the hour the parcel left. Non-repudiation is that signature, which makes it hard for the courier to say the parcel never moved. Non-repudiation: A slip that any clerk can stamp with a shared mark is a poor picture of non-repudiation. Non-repudiation: Checking a photo at the desk is a different picture and is not non-repudiation. Non-repudiation: Deciding which rooms the courier may enter is a different picture and is not non-repudiation. Hold non-repudiation on the proof that still points at one person after the argument starts. Non-repudiation: If the slip can be rewritten overnight, non-repudiation in this picture collapses. Non-repudiation: The parcel's contents staying unchanged is helpful, yet non-repudiation is about who cannot deny sending it.",
        'r4': 'Non-repudiation: Validate the signature on the payment approval before calling it Non-repudiation. Non-repudiation: Record the key identifier and the result beside Non-repudiation on ticket 5804. Non-repudiation: Check that the private key was not a shared team secret. Separate Non-repudiation from the sign-in that happened the same morning. Separate Non-repudiation from the role that allowed payments. Non-repudiation: Note whether the approval log can be edited after the fact. Non-repudiation: Stop when one actor is bound to the release and cannot credibly deny it.',
        'exam': 'Non-repudiation: A later denial defeated by a private-key signature is non-repudiation. Non-repudiation: A sign-in check is authentication, not non-repudiation. Non-repudiation: A permission decision is authorization, not non-repudiation. Shared passwords destroy non-repudiation. Symmetric codes are weak non-repudiation because both holders can mint them. Non-repudiation: Integrity can ride along with a signature, but the denial question is non-repudiation.',
    },
    'Authentication': {
        'body': 'Authentication answers who is asking, by checking a secret, a possession, or a body trait before any session is trusted. Authentication matters because authorization and logs are meaningless if the clinic cannot tell which human presented the name. Authentication on ticket 5911 was a password plus a prompt on a phone the user carries, which is two different factors. Authentication is not satisfied by two passwords, because both are something the person knows and that is still one factor. Authentication factors taught for the exam are something you know, something you have, something you are, and often somewhere you are or something you do. Authentication of a claim starts with identification, which is merely stating the name, and authentication is the proof that follows. Authentication with time-based one-time codes follows the common six-digit pattern from a shared seed, and the code expires quickly. Authentication with a hardware security key or a platform authenticator is a possession factor that resists simple phishing of passwords. Authentication biometrics are judged with false accept rate, false reject rate, and the crossover point where those curves meet. Authentication that uses only a biometric can be replayed or hard to revoke, so many designs pair it with another factor. Authentication on the network may use certificates or a ticket-granting service, but the exam point is still that identity was proved. Authentication is not non-repudiation, which is about whether that person can deny the act tomorrow. Authentication is not authorization, which is about which chart or payment the proved identity may touch. Authentication: A photo check at a desk is the everyday picture of authentication, and it does not by itself open the pharmacy safe. Authentication: Ticket 5911 should say which factors were checked and whether both had to succeed. Choose authentication when the stem shows a proof of identity. Authentication: Pick the permission label when the stem moves past proof into what the identity may touch. Authentication: Account lockout and password spraying are about attacks on authentication, and the control that blocks the spray is separate from the definition. Authentication: Push prompts that a user approves without reading are a weak authentication habit called prompt fatigue. Write authentication when you can name the proof, not merely the username that was typed. Single sign-on still performs authentication, then reuses the result, and it does not remove the need for the original proof. Reset flows are part of authentication security because a mailbox takeover becomes a password reset takeover. Authentication: Keep ticket 5911 pointed at the password and the phone, which is the authentication evidence. Authentication logs should show which factor failed without recording the secret itself. Authentication recovery codes are possession-like backups and must be stored with the same care as the phone. Authentication session cookies are not a new proof, so stealing a cookie bypasses the factor check until the session ends.',
        'r0': 'Authentication proves identity with knowledge, possession, inherence, and sometimes location or behavior. Authentication: Two passwords are one factor and are not multi-factor authentication. Identification claims a name, and authentication proves the claim. Authentication: Time-based one-time passwords and hardware keys are possession-style authentication proofs. Authentication: False accept rate, false reject rate, and crossover error rate describe biometric authentication. Authentication is not authorization and is not non-repudiation. Ticket 5911 is authentication because a password and a phone prompt were both required. Prompt fatigue weakens push-based authentication. Choose authentication when the stem asks how identity was proved. Single sign-on reuses an authentication result and still depends on that proof. Authentication: A mailbox reset path is part of the authentication attack surface.',
        'r1': 'Authentication asks you to prove you are you. Authentication happens before the door opens. Authentication can use a secret only you should know. Authentication can use a phone you carry. Authentication can use a body trait. Authentication with two similar secrets is still one factor. Authentication: Saying your name is not yet authentication. Authentication is not the list of rooms you may enter. Authentication is not a later argument about who signed. Authentication: A desk check of a photo is authentication. Tired tapping of approve weakens authentication. Authentication: Name the proof when you say authentication.',
        'r2': "Authentication resembles a desk clerk who compares a face to a photo before handing over a chart. Authentication is that comparison, and it ends when the clerk is satisfied the person matches the name. Authentication: A second password written on a card in the same wallet is not a richer authentication picture, because it is still something the person carries as knowledge. Authentication: Deciding that this person may not enter the pharmacy is a different picture and is not authentication. Authentication: A signed slip from yesterday is a different picture and is not authentication. Hold authentication on the proof offered at the moment of the request. Authentication: If the clerk waves everyone through, authentication in this picture is not happening. Authentication: The chart's contents staying private is a later concern, and authentication only settled who stood at the desk.",
        'r4': 'List the factors checked for Authentication on ticket 5911. Authentication: Confirm the phone prompt is a different factor than the password. Authentication: Reject a second password as multi-factor Authentication. Separate Authentication from the permissions granted after success. Separate Authentication from any signature used to deny a later dispute. Authentication: Note prompt fatigue if users approved Authentication prompts blindly. Authentication: Stop when the proof of identity is what the ticket records.',
        'exam': 'Proof of identity is authentication. Two passwords are not multi-factor authentication. Authentication: Permissions after login are authorization, not authentication. Authentication: A signature that blocks a later denial is non-repudiation, not authentication. Authentication: Biometric false accept and false reject rates belong to authentication. Push fatigue is an authentication failure mode. Identification without proof is not authentication.',
    },
    'Authorization': {
        'body': "Authorization answers what an already identified person or service is allowed to do, which starts only after identity has been proved. Authorization matters because a nurse who is truly the nurse can still be forbidden to approve a drug order reserved for a physician. Authorization on ticket 6020 let the nurse read the chart and refused the order entry that policy keeps for doctors. Authorization in that ticket is the permission decision, not the badge check that proved the nurse's identity and not a signature dispute from last week. Authorization is commonly expressed as access control lists, role assignments, or attributes such as department, shift, and location. Role-based authorization gives permissions to a role and then gives the role to people, so you change the role instead of every person. Attribute-based authorization evaluates rules about the user, the resource, and the context at the time of the request. Discretionary authorization lets the owner grant access, while mandatory authorization follows labels the owner cannot freely override. Rule-based authorization applies conditions such as time of day or source network on top of who the user is. Least privilege is the authorization habit of granting only the rights the job needs today. Separation of duties is an authorization design that stops one person from holding two conflicting rights, such as create and approve. Authorization: A 401 response means the caller is not authenticated, and a 403 response means authentication succeeded but authorization refused the action. Authorization is not authentication, and a successful sign-in does not imply authorization for every record. Authorization is not non-repudiation, which would be about whether the nurse can later deny having read the chart. Authorization: Ticket 6020 should cite the role, the denied action, and the rule that reserves orders for physicians. Choose authorization when the stem shows a permission boundary after identity is known. Authorization: Choose authentication when the stem stops at proving the name. Privileged access tools implement authorization for administrators by brokering just-in-time rights instead of standing domain admin. Group sprawl breaks authorization when old projects leave people in powerful groups nobody reviews. Write authorization when you can point at an allow or a deny of a specific action. Authorization decisions should be logged, but the log is accounting, not the decision itself. Emergency access procedures are still authorization if they are pre-defined rights, not a shared password taped to a monitor. Authorization: Keep the nurse and the drug order in view, because that split is the heart of authorization. Authorization reviews should sample privileged groups each quarter and remove people who changed jobs. Authorization for service accounts still needs an owner, because a backup job is a subject with rights. Authorization denials deserve the same log detail as allows, or ticket 6020 cannot show the refusal.",
        'r0': 'Authorization decides permitted actions after identity is known. Authorization: Access lists, roles, attributes, labels, and time rules are authorization mechanisms. Authorization: Role-based designs attach rights to roles, and attribute-based designs evaluate context at request time. Authorization: Discretionary control is owner-granted, and mandatory control follows system labels. Authorization: Least privilege and separation of duties are authorization principles. Authorization: HTTP 401 points at missing authentication, and HTTP 403 points at failed authorization. Authorization: Authentication proves the nurse, and non-repudiation concerns a later denial, so neither is authorization. Ticket 6020 is authorization because chart read was allowed and drug approval was refused. Standing admin rights violate least-privilege authorization. Choose authorization when the stem asks what the identified subject may do. Just-in-time admin brokering is an authorization control for privileged tasks.',
        'r1': 'Authorization answers what you are allowed to do. Authorization comes after people know who you are. Authorization: Being known is not the same as authorization. Authorization: A nurse may read a chart under authorization. Authorization: A nurse may still be blocked from a drug order. Authorization can follow a job role. Authorization can follow the time of day. Authorization: A door to the lobby is not every door. Authorization is not the photo check at the desk. Authorization is not a later fight about a signature. Extra rights sitting unused weaken authorization. Authorization: Name the allowed action when you say authorization.',
        'r2': 'Authorization resembles a lobby key that opens the front hall and still does nothing at the pharmacy cabinet. Authorization is the difference between those two doors after the person has already been recognized. Authorization: The photo check at the entrance is a different picture and is not authorization. Authorization: A signed delivery note from last week belongs to another scene and is not authorization. Hold authorization on the specific door the key is cut for. Authorization: If every key opens every cabinet, authorization in this picture has collapsed into a single yes. Authorization: A log of who took a key is useful, and it is not the authorization decision itself. Authorization: The cabinet that stays shut for the nurse is authorization doing its job.',
        'r4': 'Authorization: Read the role and the denied action for Authorization on ticket 6020. Authorization: Confirm identity was already proved before judging Authorization. Authorization: Cite the rule that reserves drug orders for physicians. Separate Authorization from the sign-in factors. Separate Authorization from any signature on an older approval. Authorization: Check for extra group memberships that widen Authorization by accident. Authorization: Stop when the allow and the deny are both explained.',
        'exam': 'A permission after identity is authorization. A sign-in proof is not authorization. Authorization: A later denial problem is not authorization. 401 is not the authorization failure, and 403 is. Least privilege is an authorization idea. Authorization: A role that can both create and approve breaks separation inside authorization. Authorization: Reading a chart is not the same authorization decision as signing a drug order.',
    },
    'Accounting': {
        'body': 'Accounting records who did what, and when, so a later reviewer can reconstruct the action without asking people to remember. Accounting matters because a payment system that cannot show the user, the amount, and the time cannot be explained to an auditor. Accounting on ticket 6133 is the transfer log that writes the clerk, the amount, the account, and the timestamp for every payment. Accounting in that log is the record, not the decision that allowed the payment and not a comparison of missing safeguards. Accounting is the third part of the familiar trio with authentication and authorization, and it is the part that remembers. Accounting events should answer who, what, when, where, and whether the attempt succeeded. Accounting: Syslog listeners on UDP 514, or TLS syslog on TCP 6514, are common pipes for accounting records from devices. RADIUS accounting on UDP 1813 records session start and stop for network access, which is accounting rather than the authentication exchange on 1812. Accounting: Windows security events such as 4624 for a logon and 4634 for a logoff are accounting records of access. Accounting is not gap analysis, which compares the controls you have with the controls a baseline requires. Accounting is not adaptive identity, which changes how hard the sign-in is when the risk signals change. Accounting: A sign-out sheet for a narcotics key is the everyday picture of accounting, and it is not the lock on the cabinet. Accounting: Ticket 6133 should include a sample line with user, amount, and time, plus where the log is stored and who can alter it. Accounting fails when clocks differ, when logs rotate away before review, or when administrators can edit the trail they are in. Write-once or tamper-evident storage strengthens accounting for records that will be challenged. Choose accounting when the stem shows a record of activity for later review. Accounting: Choose authentication when the stem shows how identity was proved, and choose authorization when it shows what was permitted. Gap analysis may use accounting data as input, but the comparison itself is not accounting. Accounting: Adaptive identity may read location and device health, and that decision is not accounting even if it is later logged. Write accounting when you can point at the trail. Accounting: Retention and access control on the log are part of making accounting trustworthy. Without accounting, incident work becomes folklore. Accounting: Keep the payment line in the story, because the user, the amount, and the time are the accounting facts. Accounting exports for auditors should be complete copies, not screenshots that omit the account field. Accounting correlation works only when device clocks stay within a small skew of the same time source. Accounting access should be separate from the people whose actions are being recorded. Accounting reviews should sample lines each month so gaps are found before an audit.',
        'r0': "Accounting is the audit trail of who did what and when. Accounting: Authentication and authorization are the other two parts of that trio, and they are not accounting. Useful accounting answers who, what, when, where, and success or failure. Accounting: UDP 514 syslog, TCP 6514 syslog, and UDP 1813 RADIUS accounting are common accounting channels. Accounting: Windows events 4624 and 4634 are accounting records of logon and logoff. Accounting: Gap analysis compares a program to a baseline and is not accounting. Accounting: Adaptive identity changes the sign-in challenge and is not accounting. Ticket 6133 is accounting because each transfer stores clerk, amount, and time. Editable logs weaken accounting. Choose accounting when the stem's evidence is the record itself. Accounting: Retention and restricted rights on the log are part of trustworthy accounting.",
        'r1': 'Accounting writes down who did the thing. Accounting also writes when it happened. Accounting lets someone review the act later. Accounting is a record, not a permission. Accounting is a record, not a harder question at the door. Accounting: A sign-out sheet is a picture of accounting. A missing line means accounting failed. Accounting: A clock that is wrong hurts accounting. Accounting: People who can edit the sheet quietly hurt accounting. Keep accounting where reviewers can still read it. Accounting: Do not call a missing safeguard list accounting. Accounting: Name the user and the time for accounting.',
        'r2': 'Accounting resembles a clipboard outside a locked cabinet where each nurse writes a name, a time, and the key they took. Accounting is the writing on that clipboard, which lets a supervisor reconstruct the afternoon. Accounting: The lock on the cabinet is a different picture and is not accounting. Accounting: A guard who asks a stranger for extra proof is a different picture and is not accounting. Accounting: A checklist of missing extinguishers is a different picture and is not accounting. Hold accounting on the lines that say who, what, and when. Accounting: If anyone can erase a line, accounting in this picture is not trustworthy. Accounting: The clipboard does not decide who may take a key, and accounting does not pretend to.',
        'r4': 'Accounting: Pull a transfer line that shows user, amount, and time for Accounting on ticket 6133. Confirm the clock source so Accounting timestamps can be trusted. Note where the Accounting log is stored and who can change it. Separate Accounting from the rule that allowed the payment. Separate Accounting from any risk-based sign-in challenge. Separate Accounting from a baseline comparison of missing controls. Accounting: Stop when the trail itself is the evidence on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'Accounting: A record of who, what, and when is accounting. A permission decision is not accounting. Accounting: A proof of identity is not accounting. Accounting: A baseline comparison is gap analysis, not accounting. Accounting: A risk-based extra challenge is adaptive identity, not accounting. UDP 1813 and syslog are accounting transports to remember. Accounting: Logs that admins can edit are weak accounting.',
    },
    'Gap analysis': {
        'body': 'A gap analysis sets the control a rule requires next to the control you can show today, and it writes down the difference. It does not install the missing piece. It does not score how likely a loss is. The product is a named delta: this requirement, this evidence, these assets still short.\n\nYou build it line by line. Take a standard clause, collect a query or a screenshot that shows the live state, and list every asset that fails the clause. Opinion without the query is not a gap record. Write the asset tags in the report so a later check can find the same machines. A vulnerability scan can feed the evidence, but the scan is not the comparison by itself. A risk register may later rate the delta. That rating is a second document. Shrinking the standard until the blank disappears, without an owner and a reason, hides the gap instead of naming it.\n\nSTD-ENC-01 requires disk encryption on every clinic laptop. The 12 March inventory query returned loaner tags LN-440 through LN-451, twelve machines, with encryption status none. Report GAP-77 lists those tags, the standard clause, and the query time. It does not claim that images were encrypted that afternoon. A manager who says the loaners rarely leave the building has described a wish, not a closed gap. The twelve blanks remain until a later inventory shows encryption or a signed exception names each tag.\n\nDefenders attach three things to the report: the requirement identifier, the evidence query, and the owner who will accept the delta. Hand GAP-77 to the change that deploys the laptop image, then run the same query after deployment. If two loaners are in a trunk and miss the image, the gap record stays open for those two. Do not mark the standard met because the project plan says the work is scheduled. A scheduled intention is not a present control.\n\nStudents confuse the comparison with the repair project, with a risk score, and with a full audit opinion. The project that turns encryption on is the change that follows. A risk score asks likelihood and impact. An audit may use this comparison and still include interviews and samples the comparison does not replace. The separating fact is the side-by-side result. If you can point at twelve specific loaners that lack a required control, you are looking at the gap, not at the score and not at the finished fix.\n',
        'r0': 'A gap analysis compares a required control with the control you can evidence, then names the shortfall. STD-ENC-01 demands disk encryption on every laptop. The 12 March query found twelve loaners, LN-440 through LN-451, with status none. GAP-77 records the clause, the tags, and the query. It does not turn encryption on, and it does not compute likelihood times impact. A vulnerability scan can supply evidence and is not the comparison by itself. A signed exception can cover a tag only when it names that tag. Re-run the query after the image change. Two machines still blank means the gap stays open for those two. A project plan date is not a completed control.\n',
        'r1': 'This comparison sets a required control beside what you have. It writes down the difference by name. It does not install the missing piece. It does not score how likely a loss is. Twelve loaner laptops with no disk encryption are a difference. A query should list the actual asset tags. A plan to fix them later is not a closed gap. A risk score is a second document. An audit can use the comparison and still be wider. Point at the missing control when you use this name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a building inspector walking a clinic supply room with a checklist. One line calls for a lock on the medication cupboard. The cupboard has a latch and no lock, so the inspector writes the blank instead of installing hardware on the spot. The blank is not a guess about how often someone might open the door. It is not the work order that will arrive next week. It is the named miss. A later score of the miss can live on another form. In the laptop story, GAP-77 is that blank line for twelve loaners. The encryption project starts after the blank is written, and it does not erase the blank until a new check passes.\n',
        'r4': 'Open GAP-77 and copy the standard clause and the query date. List tags LN-440 through LN-451 rather than writing loaners in general. Do not mark encryption present because a project is scheduled. Keep any likelihood score in a separate risk note. Assign an owner to the delta before you close the report. Re-run the same query after the image is deployed. Stop when each short laptop is either encrypted or named on a signed exception.\n',
        'exam': 'The side-by-side shortfall is this analysis. The project that installs the missing control is not the analysis. A likelihood-and-impact score is not the analysis. A scan without the comparison is only evidence. Twelve unencrypted loaners against a rule that requires encryption is the stem to recognize. A plan date does not close the gap. Recheck after the change or the record goes stale.\n',
    },
    'Adaptive identity': {
        'body': "Adaptive identity changes how hard a sign-in is, using signals such as place, device health, time, and behavior instead of one static challenge for every session. Adaptive identity matters because a quiet login from the usual clinic laptop should not be treated like the same account appearing in a new country. Adaptive identity on ticket 6355 stayed silent for the office laptop and demanded an extra check when that account appeared from an unfamiliar country the same afternoon. Adaptive identity in that case is the step-up decision, not the log line that merely recorded it and not a spreadsheet of missing laptop encryption. Adaptive identity signals include impossible travel, a new device, a failed health check, an odd hour, and a pattern that does not match the user's history. Adaptive identity can allow, deny, or ask for another factor, and the choice is recomputed rather than stored as a permanent role. Adaptive identity is not accounting, which would only write down what happened. Adaptive identity is not gap analysis, which would compare the program to a baseline and name missing safeguards. Adaptive identity: A doorman who waves at regulars and stops visitors for a name is the picture of adaptive identity, with the extra question tied to unfamiliarity. Adaptive identity fails when every login gets the hardest challenge, because that is static strictness rather than adaptation. Adaptive identity also fails when the foreign login is waved through because the account is an executive. Adaptive identity: Impossible travel is a classic adaptive identity signal when two locations cannot be reached in the elapsed time. Adaptive identity: Device posture, such as a missing disk encryption agent or a stale patch level, can raise adaptive identity friction. Adaptive identity: Ticket 6355 should name the signals that were quiet and the signal that forced the extra check. Choose adaptive identity when the stem shows the challenge changing with risk. Adaptive identity: Choose accounting when the stem shows only the record. Adaptive identity: A missing control against a written requirement is gap analysis, not this signal-based challenge. Adaptive identity is a zero-trust input, and it is not the whole policy engine by itself. Write adaptive identity when you can point at a signal and a changed challenge. Adaptive identity: Static group membership is not adaptive identity even if the group is named high risk. Adaptive identity: Keep the office laptop and the new country in the story, because that contrast is adaptive identity. Adaptive identity should explain the step-up to the user without revealing how to game the signals. Review false step-ups so adaptive identity does not train people to approve prompts blindly. Adaptive identity decisions ought to be logged, and the log is still accounting beside the decision. Adaptive identity tuning should track how often step-up prompts are approved without the user reading them. Adaptive identity false alarms on clinic roaming between buildings need a documented exception that is still explained.",
        'r0': 'Adaptive identity varies assurance using context such as location, device posture, time, and behavior. Adaptive identity: A known clinic laptop can pass with less friction than the same account from a new country. Adaptive identity: Impossible travel and unhealthy devices are adaptive identity signals. Step-up prompts are adaptive identity actions, while a permanent role is not. Adaptive identity: Accounting records the event and is not adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: Gap analysis finds missing baseline items and is not adaptive identity. Ticket 6355 is adaptive identity because the challenge changed with the country signal. Adaptive identity: Forcing the hardest check on every login is not adaptive identity. Choose adaptive identity when risk signals change the proof demanded. Adaptive identity can feed a broader decision system and is not that whole system by itself. Adaptive identity: Executive exceptions that ignore signals break adaptive identity.',
        'r1': 'Adaptive identity asks a harder question in a stranger place. Adaptive identity stays quieter for a usual device. Adaptive identity watches place, device, and habits. Adaptive identity can ask for one more proof. Adaptive identity can refuse when the story looks wrong. Adaptive identity: A fixed hard question for everyone is not adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: A written diary of the login is not adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: A list of missing protections is not adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: The doorman who greets regulars fits adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: Name the signal when you claim adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: Do not give leaders a free pass around adaptive identity.',
        'r2': 'Adaptive identity resembles a building doorman who waves familiar faces through and stops to ask visitors for a name. Adaptive identity is the change in questioning, not a book that records every wave. Adaptive identity is not a checklist of missing fire gear. Hold adaptive identity on why this person earned an easier or harder greeting. Adaptive identity: If every person gets the same long interview, the picture is no longer adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: If a famous tenant skips the question on a strange night, adaptive identity has been switched off for them. Adaptive identity: A notebook of who entered is useful and is not adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: The picture stops at the adjusted greeting.',
        'r4': 'Adaptive identity: Compare the office login with the foreign login for Adaptive identity on ticket 6355. Adaptive identity: List the signals that stayed quiet and the signal that raised Adaptive identity friction. Record whether Adaptive identity allowed, stepped up, or denied. Separate Adaptive identity from the audit line that only stored the event. Separate Adaptive identity from any baseline gap about missing encryption. Adaptive identity: Check that executives are not exempt from Adaptive identity signals. Adaptive identity: Stop when the changed challenge is explained by a named signal.',
        'exam': 'Adaptive identity: A challenge that changes with risk is adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: A log of the login is not adaptive identity. Adaptive identity: A missing-control comparison is not adaptive identity. Impossible travel is an adaptive identity signal. Adaptive identity: The same hard prompt for every user is not adaptive identity. Device health can drive adaptive identity step-up. Adaptive identity: Waiving signals for senior staff breaks adaptive identity.',
    },
    'Threat scope reduction': {
        'body': 'This design goal limits how far one compromise can travel. It does not claim you have fewer adversaries, and it does not claim the first device is safe. A guest laptop can still be lost. The win is that the lost laptop cannot open the payroll servers, because the path between them is gone.\n\nYou shrink reach with routes, accounts, and placement. Separate networks, deny the paths you do not need, and give each subject only the systems its job requires. A flat interior, where any address on the office wifi can talk to every server, is the opposite result. Adding a monitoring page does not shrink a path. The packets still arrive. A spare site in another city does not shrink a path either. Record the blocked destinations beside the change so a review has something concrete to inspect. It gives you somewhere to recover after the path has already been used.\n\nGuest VLAN 40 uses 10.40.0.0/16. Payroll VLAN 12 uses 10.12.0.0/16. The core switch ACL denies the guest range toward the payroll range, including host 10.12.4.20. When a guest laptop was infected, endpoint tooling on that laptop showed connection attempts toward the payroll host, and the switch log showed DENY for those attempts. Ticket IR-88 records both lines. Payroll jobs kept running. The infection was real, and the scope stopped at the ACL. A request the next week to allow any guest address to any server port would have undone the ticket.\n\nDefenders draw the paths they mean to keep, then deny the rest and test from the low-trust side. From a guest port, a permitted name lookup may work while payroll ports stay closed. Review rules that punch a hole for a vendor laptop, and put an end date on them. When an administrator account can reach every VLAN from a daily workstation, scope has grown again even if the guest ACL looks strict. Least privilege on accounts is part of the same goal as the network cut.\n\nStudents hear the phrase and think it means a smaller threat list, or they credit an alert with reducing scope because the team learned about the malware. Learning is discovery. Scope changes only when a later attempt can touch fewer systems. A backup does not do that. The separating fact is reach. After the change, name a subject and the systems it can no longer open, the way IR-88 names the guest laptop and the payroll host it failed to reach.\n',
        'r0': 'Threat scope reduction limits how far one foothold can reach. It does not reduce the count of adversaries, and it does not clean the first device. Guest 10.40.0.0/16 is denied toward payroll 10.12.0.0/16, so attempts from a compromised guest laptop to 10.12.4.20 show DENY on the core switch in IR-88. An alert about those attempts is discovery, not a smaller path. A hot spare in another city is recovery, not a smaller path. Test from the guest side and confirm payroll ports stay closed. Vendor any-any rules reopen scope unless they carry an end date. Admin accounts that can touch every VLAN widen scope again. The proof is a subject and the systems it can no longer open.\n',
        'r1': 'This work makes one broken machine less able to reach the rest. It does not erase the first break. It does not shrink the list of enemies. Separate networks are a common way to do it. A guest laptop should not open payroll. An alert about the laptop does not close that path. A spare building does not close that path either. Test from the weak side and see what still answers. A wide allow rule grows the reach again. Name what the broken machine can no longer touch.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse fire wall between a paint cage and the office file room. A flare in the cage can ruin the paint and still stop at the wall, so the files do not burn in the same minute. The wall does not prevent the flare, and it does not shorten the list of things that can spark. A smoke alarm tells you the cage is hot and leaves the doorway unchanged. A second warehouse across town saves the business later and does not stop this doorway. On the clinic network, the deny between the guest range and payroll is that wall. IR-88 is the scorch mark that failed to cross it.\n',
        'r4': 'Draw the path from guest VLAN 40 to payroll host 10.12.4.20. Confirm the core ACL denies 10.40.0.0/16 toward 10.12.0.0/16. From a guest port, check that payroll ports do not answer. Attach both the endpoint attempt and the DENY line to IR-88. Do not treat the alert alone as a smaller scope. Put an end date on any vendor rule that reopens the path. Stop when you can name the subject and the systems it cannot reach.\n',
        'exam': 'Smaller reach after one compromise is this goal. A shorter list of adversaries is not the same claim. An alert does not shrink a path. A backup or a spare site does not shrink a path. A flat office network is the failure mode. The proof is a subject that can no longer open a named system. An any-any exception undoes the work.\n',
    },
    'Policy-driven access': {
        'body': "Policy-driven access grants or refuses each request from rules, instead of assuming that an inside address is already safe. Policy-driven access matters because a laptop on clinic wifi is not a reason to open payroll, the file share, or mail. Policy-driven access on ticket 6577 opened a file share only when the user, the device health, and the hour all matched the written rule. Policy-driven access in that allow is the habit of judging the request, not the shrunk blast radius by itself and not the component that ferries the decision. Policy-driven access rechecks identity, device posture, and context rather than trusting a session forever because it started on the office LAN. Policy-driven access is the practice, while a policy engine is the decider and a policy administrator is the courier of the result. Policy-driven access is not threat scope reduction, which you prove by showing what a compromised host can no longer reach. Policy-driven access rejects implicit trust zones, where being on the inside network is treated as a free pass. Policy-driven access: A pharmacy window that opens only when the prescription and the identity both match is the picture of policy-driven access. Policy-driven access fails when an any-any rule or a remembered VPN pool bypasses the checks. Policy-driven access also fails when device health is collected and then ignored in the rule. Policy-driven access: Ticket 6577 should quote the rule conditions and the observed user, posture, and time. Choose policy-driven access when the stem shows a decision made from rules on every request. Policy-driven access: Segmentation outcomes point at threat scope reduction rather than at this per-request decision habit. Policy-driven access: Choose the engine or the administrator only when the stem names that component's job. Policy-driven access: Continuous evaluation is part of mature policy-driven access, so a laptop that falls out of health loses the share. Write policy-driven access when location alone is not accepted as proof. Policy-driven access: Legacy apps that cannot speak the checks need a documented exception, or they become a hole in policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Keep the file share conditions in the story, because user plus health plus hour is policy-driven access. Policy-driven access should be explainable to the person who was denied, at least at the level of which condition failed. Policy-driven access: Service accounts and backup jobs are requests too, and policy-driven access should name them as subjects. Policy-driven access: A standing allow for the whole subnet is the opposite of policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Review rules after organizational change so policy-driven access does not encode last year's departments. Policy-driven access pairs naturally with least privilege, and it is still its own label. Policy-driven access denials should tell the user which condition failed without exposing the full rulebook. Policy-driven access test plans ought to include a healthy device at a forbidden hour and an unhealthy device at a good hour.",
        'r0': 'Policy-driven access evaluates each request against rules instead of trusting the inside network. Policy-driven access: User, device health, and time are typical policy-driven access inputs, as in ticket 6577. Policy-driven access: The decision component and the courier component are not the label policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Threat scope reduction is about blast radius and is not the same phrase as policy-driven access. Implicit inside-means-safe designs contradict policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: VPN pools that skip checks undo policy-driven access. Choose policy-driven access when every request is judged from rule conditions. Policy-driven access: Continuous reevaluation belongs to mature policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Service accounts are in scope for policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: A subnet-wide allow is not policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Exceptions for old apps must be explicit or they bypass policy-driven access.',
        'r1': 'Policy-driven access lets the rule decide every time. Policy-driven access does not treat the inside as automatically safe. Policy-driven access can require the person, the device, and the hour. Policy-driven access looks again instead of trusting forever. Policy-driven access checks the person and the request again. Policy-driven access: A guest wristband that opens every room breaks policy-driven access. Policy-driven access is a habit of judging, not a smaller fire. Policy-driven access is not the messenger who carries the answer. Policy-driven access: Name the conditions when you describe policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: A wide open hallway is not policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Old exceptions need a written reason under policy-driven access.',
        'r2': "Policy-driven access resembles a pharmacy window that opens only when the script and the identity both match on that visit. Policy-driven access is the fresh check, not a wristband from the fair that works all day. Policy-driven access is not a fire door's blast area, and it is not the nurse who walks a note to the guard. Policy-driven access: If the window stays up because the person is already in the building, policy-driven access has been skipped. Hold policy-driven access on the rule being applied again. Policy-driven access: A remembered favor from yesterday is not policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: The picture stops when the match of conditions is what opens the window. Policy-driven access: A smaller kitchen is a different picture and is not policy-driven access.",
        'r4': 'Policy-driven access: Quote the rule conditions for Policy-driven access on ticket 6577. Policy-driven access: Compare the live user, device health, and hour with Policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Show a deny when any required condition fails Policy-driven access. Separate Policy-driven access from a segmentation diagram alone. Separate Policy-driven access from the component that only forwards the decision. Policy-driven access: Find subnet allows that bypass Policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Stop when the per-request rule match is the evidence.',
        'exam': 'Policy-driven access: Judging every request from rules is policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Trusting the office network is not policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Blast-radius design is not the same label as policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: The decision brain and the decision courier are different labels from policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: User, posture, and time are typical policy-driven access conditions. Policy-driven access: A permanent subnet allow contradicts policy-driven access. Policy-driven access: Re-checking health during a session supports policy-driven access.',
    },
    'Policy administrator': {
        'body': "In a zero trust design, the policy administrator is the component that carries a decision to the gate. The decision is allow, deny, or revoke. This component does not weigh the signals. It takes the result from the engine and tells the enforcement point to open a path, keep it closed, or tear down a path that should no longer exist.\n\nThe handoff is easy to skip in a diagram because the boxes sit close together. The engine thinks. The administrator acts on that thought by configuring the gate, often with a session-specific credential the subject must present. If the result is deny, the administrator instructs the gate not to complete the session. If a user who was allowed at noon should be cut off at 12:40, revocation has to travel the same path. A decision that stays inside the engine never becomes a closed session. A human job title with the same words, the person who edits the written policy, is a different role. Do not mix the staff job with this component when you read a stem.\n\nTicket ZT-441 follows subject j.patel asking for the mail application. The engine returned deny. The administrator log then shows decision_id 9f3c, action DENY, resource mail-app, sent onward to gateway gw-app-02. The gateway drop comes after that message. Priya's note records three timestamps in order: engine result, administrator message, gateway drop. That order is the mechanism. When a test last month showed an engine deny with no administrator line, the gateway still allowed the session, and the defect was in the handoff rather than in the rule.\n\nDefenders trace denied and revoked sessions across those three records. Clocks must match or the order becomes a guess. The console that can push decisions should be limited to the platform team, not granted to every application owner who wants a quick allow. Test revocation on a live lab session and confirm the gate tears it down, not merely blocks the next login. If the administrator is down, decide in advance whether paths fail closed. An open failure turns a dead messenger into a quiet allow.\n\nStudents point at the engine or at the gateway and use this name for both. The engine produces the result. The gateway moves. This component is the messenger and the path controller between them. The separating fact is the handoff you can log. A weighed decision means you are looking at the engine. A dropped packet means you are looking at the gate. The instruction that told the gate what to do is the piece that belongs here.\n",
        'r0': 'The policy administrator carries allow, deny, or revoke from the engine to the enforcement point and can establish or tear down the path. It does not weigh posture, time, or identity itself. In ZT-441, decision_id 9f3c action DENY for j.patel and mail-app was pushed to gateway gw-app-02, and the drop follows that push. An engine deny with no administrator line left the gate open in a prior test, which isolates the fault to the handoff. Revocation has to ride the same path or a noon allow survives all afternoon. This component is not the staff job that edits written policy. Limit the console that can push decisions. Prefer a fail-closed result if the messenger is down. Evidence is the message that told the gate what to do.\n',
        'r1': 'This part carries the yes or the no to the door. It does not make the decision itself. It does not stand in the doorway. Allow, deny, and revoke are the messages it can send. A cut-off must travel the same path as a new request. A decision that never leaves the thinker changes nothing. The person who edits a written rule is a different role. Trace the message before you blame the door. If the messenger is down, decide whether paths stay shut. Point at the handoff when you use this name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a hotel desk at night. The manager in the back office decides whether a guest may go up. The clerk at the desk receives that decision and tells the door staff to let the guest pass or to keep the door shut. The clerk is not the one who weighed the reservation, and the clerk is not the door itself. A decision that stays in the back office never moves the door. A guest who was allowed at dinner can be stopped only if the clerk passes a later stop order. In the mail-app path, the administrator log for decision 9f3c is that clerk. The gateway is the door staff who act on what the clerk passed along.\n',
        'r4': 'Pull the engine result, the administrator message, and the gateway result for ZT-441. Confirm decision_id 9f3c says DENY before gw-app-02 drops the session. Order the three timestamps before you name the failing part. If the engine denied and the gate allowed, look for a missing handoff. Do not treat the written-policy editor as this component. Test a revocation and confirm a live session actually ends. Stop when the ticket shows the message that instructed the gate.\n',
        'exam': 'This component delivers the decision to the gate. The engine weighs the signals, so a reasoned allow is not this component. The gateway drops or opens the session, so the drop alone is not this component. A human editor of written policy is not this component. Revoke is part of the job, not only the first allow. A missing handoff explains a deny that never took effect. Fail closed if the messenger is down, or the outage becomes an allow.\n',
    },
    'Policy engine': {
        'body': "The policy engine is the part that decides. It takes signals about the asker and the request, compares them with the rules you loaded, and returns allow or deny. It does not stand in the doorway, and it does not push the result to the gate. Another component carries the result. If you only have a dropped session, you have not yet shown the decision.\n\nSignals are the raw material. Identity, group membership, device posture, time of day, and the sensitivity of the system can all matter, but only the signals your rules actually read will change the result. A healthy laptop at a normal hour can be allowed for mail and denied for payroll if the rules say so. The same person on a laptop that failed its health check can be denied for mail a minute later. The engine should record the inputs it used. A bare allow with no inputs cannot be explained when the user appeals.\n\nAt 14:10 on ticket ZT-442 the engine evaluated j.patel for the mail application. Device posture was compliant, the account was in mail-users, and the local hour sat inside the clinic window. The log line reads decision ALLOW, reason posture_ok and time_window, decision_id 4ab1. The gateway configuration was not edited by that evaluation. When the lab repeated the request with posture failed, the same account received DENY, reason posture_fail, decision_id 4ab2. Two results from one engine, differing by one signal, are the clearest way to teach the mechanism.\n\nDefenders version the rule set and test it with known subjects before it faces the clinic. Store those rules apart from the gateway configuration so a late tweak on the gate does not silently rewrite the decision. Sample real decisions each week and check that the logged inputs still match the rule you think is running. If the engine is unreachable, the design should fail closed for new sessions rather than let every request through. An allow that cannot be tied to a rule version is a finding, not a success.\n\nStudents see a gateway drop and name the engine, because the drop is the visible event. The drop is enforcement. Students also name the engine when they see the message that told the gate what to do, and that message is the administrator's handoff. A hand-written access list living only on a firewall can be a gate's local rule, and it is not this component unless something is weighing live signals into a decision. The separating fact is the weighing. Point at the inputs and the allow or deny they produced, as 4ab1 and 4ab2 do, and you are looking at the engine.\n",
        'r0': 'The policy engine weighs signals against rules and returns allow or deny. It does not configure the gateway and does not drop the session itself. On ZT-442 at 14:10, j.patel with compliant posture and membership in mail-users received ALLOW decision_id 4ab1, reason posture_ok and time_window. The same account with failed posture received DENY decision_id 4ab2. Log the inputs or an appeal cannot be checked. Keep the rule store separate from gateway configuration. Version the rules and test known subjects before clinic traffic hits them. If the engine cannot be reached, fail closed on new sessions. A firewall list with no live signals is not this component. Evidence is the weighed result, not the later drop.\n',
        'r1': 'This part thinks about the request. It returns yes or no. It does not stand at the door. It does not carry the answer to the door. A healthy device and a normal hour can lead to yes. A failed health check can lead to no for the same person. Write down the facts that produced the answer. A drop you can see is a different part. A message sent to the door is a different part. Keep the rules somewhere you can version. If this part is down, new requests should wait. Point at the weighing when you use this name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic engineer in a small room of gauges. The engineer reads pressure, time of day, and which wing is asking for heat, then writes yes or no on a slip. Someone else walks the slip to the valve, and the valve is what actually opens. The engineer does not turn the valve by hand. Two slips for the same wing, one yes and one no, can differ because a single gauge changed. A valve that slams shut is not proof you understand the gauges. In the mail request, decision 4ab1 is the slip that said yes while the laptop was healthy. Decision 4ab2 is the slip that said no after that health reading changed.\n',
        'r4': 'Open ZT-442 and copy decision_id 4ab1 with its inputs. Copy decision_id 4ab2 and confirm only posture changed. Do not edit the gateway while you are only checking the decision. Keep the rule version beside the two results. Separate the engine line from the later gateway drop. Separate the engine line from the handoff message. Stop when the ticket shows inputs, a result, and the rule version that produced them.\n',
        'exam': 'The weighed allow or deny is this component. A gateway drop is the enforcement point, not the engine. The message that tells the gate what to do is the administrator, not the engine. A static firewall list with no live signals is not the engine. Log inputs or the result cannot be explained. Fail closed when the engine cannot be reached. Two decisions that differ by one signal are the stem that proves the weighing.\n',
    },
    'Implicit trust zones': {
        'body': 'An implicit trust zone is an area of the network that still treats inside as safe enough. Once a laptop is on the office wireless network, applications accept it because of where it sits, with no fresh look at the person, the device health, or the request. Zero trust is the refusal of that habit. The design goal is to make these zones smaller, not to rename the old interior and keep the habit.\n\nThe mechanism is an assumption hiding in placement. Addresses on the office subnet are allowed to file servers, printers, and admin pages because a firewall at the internet edge already did its job. That edge does not see a stolen laptop that is already associated. Malware on a clinic PC inherits every permission the subnet was given. A password stolen from one desk becomes a key to the whole interior. The zone can be tiny, such as a single outdated application that trusts every caller behind one connector, or it can be the entire building LAN. Size is a finding. The label applies whenever location is doing the work of a decision.\n\nCORP-WIFI drops clients onto 10.1.0.0/16. File server fs-01 allows SMB from that whole subnet and does not ask for a device posture check. Ticket ARCH-12 calls the result a flat trust behind the firewall. In a test, a laptop on that wireless network opened a finance share with only a stolen password. No second question appeared. The same laptop on guest wireless could not open the share, which shows the trust was tied to the interior address, not to a rule about the user. That contrast belongs in the ticket next to the firewall diagram that still draws one safe inside.\n\nDefenders inventory every app that allows a source subnet and nothing else. Put an enforcement point in front of the sensitive ones so network membership is not enough. Split guest, clinical, and server networks so a hallway laptop is not already inside the payroll zone. Do not accept a diagram that shows a firewall on the edge and a cloud with the word trusted across the building. When a team claims zero trust because staff use a second factor at the VPN, ask what happens after the VPN address is assigned. If the answer is full interior access, the zone is still there.\n\nStudents call the office LAN a zero trust network because an internet firewall exists. The firewall is the old perimeter. The implicit zone is what remains behind it. A screened subnet for public servers is a placement pattern, and it is not this label unless the stem is about undeserved trust. A two-door lobby is a physical room, not a network assumption. The separating fact is the assumption itself. If being inside is treated as permission, you are looking at this zone, and zero trust has not arrived yet.\n',
        'r0': 'An implicit trust zone treats interior placement as permission, which zero trust tries to shrink rather than celebrate. CORP-WIFI puts clients on 10.1.0.0/16, and fs-01 allows SMB from that subnet with no posture check. ARCH-12 records a test laptop that opened a finance share with only a stolen password. The same laptop failed from guest wireless, so the trust was the address. An edge firewall does not remove the zone behind it. A VPN second factor does not remove it if the VPN address then reaches everything. Inventory apps that allow a subnet and nothing else. A screened subnet is a different pattern. Evidence is an allow based on location alone.\n',
        'r1': 'This zone treats the inside of the network as safe. Being on office wireless becomes a free pass. Apps stop asking who is calling. A stolen laptop inside inherits that pass. A firewall at the internet edge does not fix the inside. A remote login prompt may still leave the inside wide. Smaller zones are the goal. A guest network should not count as the inside. Look for a rule that trusts an address and nothing more. Zero trust keeps asking after you are inside.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a fairground where a wristband opens every tent, including the cash office, once you are past the front gate. The gate was real. The mistake is the band that means the same thing in every tent. A guest who never received a band cannot enter the cash office, which shows the band, not a fresh question, is what the tents trust. A guard who checks a list at the cash door is a different design. A turnstile far away does not change the band. On the clinic LAN, an address on the office wireless network is that wristband. ARCH-12 is the moment a test laptop cashed in the band with a stolen password and no second question.\n',
        'r4': 'Map which applications accept 10.1.0.0/16 with no posture check. Include fs-01 SMB in that list for ARCH-12. Repeat a test from CORP-WIFI and from guest wireless and save both results. Do not call the edge firewall a fix for the interior allow. Ask what a VPN address can reach after login. Separate this finding from a two-door lobby control. Stop when the ticket shows an allow that rests on location alone.\n',
        'exam': 'Inside-means-safe is this zone. An edge firewall does not erase it. A VPN prompt does not erase it if the session then reaches the whole interior. Zero trust is the effort to shrink the zone, not a new name for the same LAN. A screened subnet is not automatically this label. A physical two-door room is not this label. The stem to recognize is an app that accepts the office subnet and asks nothing further.\n',
    },
    'Subjects and systems': {
        'body': 'Access rules need two names. The subject is the person or the service that is asking. The system is the thing being asked. A sentence that names only one of them cannot be enforced, because the gate does not know which pair is allowed. Human users are subjects, and so are backup jobs, scanners, and deployment pipelines. A database does not become a subject merely because it is important. It is a system when something is asking it for data.\n\nThe mechanism is identity on both ends of the request. The subject presents an account, a certificate, or a workload identity. The system is a named application, host, or data store, not a whole subnet when the data is sensitive. The engine can weigh a request only after both identities are known. A rule that says the network may back up names neither side. A rule that says authenticated users may use internal systems names both sides so broadly that it is barely a rule. Non-human subjects are where teams get careless. The job runs every night, the password is shared, and nobody can say which host is allowed to be that subject.\n\nRule ZT-014 names subject svc-backup-night and system payroll-db-01. The subject may connect at 01:15 from host bk-01 only, on TCP 5432. Ticket ZT-014-R records a night when the job ran from new host bk-09. The engine denied it because the subject was bound to bk-01, not because the password was wrong. That deny is the control working. Widening the rule to any backup host would erase the binding that made the deny possible. The payroll database stays the system in both the success and the deny. The backup job stays the subject even though no person sat at a keyboard.\n\nDefenders inventory service accounts and tie each one to an owner and a host or certificate. Retire subjects whose owner has left. When a scanner must reach many systems, list those systems instead of writing all internal. Review rules quarterly for the phrase authenticated users on one side and a subnet on the other. Alert when a known subject appears from a host it was not bound to, as bk-09 did. Do not solve a deny by handing the service account to every server team.\n\nStudents treat only people as subjects, so a service-account misuse looks like a mysterious process rather than an access decision. Students also swap the words and call the database a subject because the incident is about payroll. The asker is the subject. The thing asked is the system. An interior zone that trusts every address skips this naming and lets location stand in for both. The separating fact is the pair. A complete rule, like ZT-014, names who is asking and what they are allowed to ask.\n',
        'r0': 'A subject is the person or service asking, and a system is the resource being asked. Both identities belong in the rule. ZT-014 allows subject svc-backup-night to reach system payroll-db-01 at 01:15 from host bk-01 only, on TCP 5432. When the job ran from bk-09, the engine denied it in ZT-014-R even though the secret was correct. Any-backup would have removed that deny. Authenticated-users to all-internal is barely a rule. Service accounts, scanners, and pipelines are subjects. The database is the system, not the subject. Bind each non-human subject to an owner and a host or certificate. Evidence is the named pair, plus the host binding that made the unexpected source fail.\n',
        'r1': 'Name the one who asks and the thing they ask. A person can be the one who asks. A backup job can be the one who asks. The payroll data store is the thing being asked. A rule that names only the network is unfinished. A service account is not a person. It is still an asker. Bind that asker to the right machine. A deny from the wrong machine can be the rule working. Do not call the data store the asker just because it matters. Write both names in the rule.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic appointment slip with two lines. One line names the nurse who is asking for a room. The other line names the room. A slip that says someone needs a room cannot be honored, because the desk does not know which pair is intended. A slip that says any nurse may use any room is barely a rule at all. The medication cabinet is not the nurse, even when the visit is about medication. A backup of that cabinet's inventory is still an asker if it requests the record. On the real rule, the night backup job is the nurse line and the payroll store is the room line. The deny from the new host is a slip that named the right nurse and the wrong doorway.\n",
        'r4': 'Read ZT-014 and write down the subject and the system as two separate names. Confirm svc-backup-night is bound to bk-01 and TCP 5432 at 01:15. On ZT-014-R, record that bk-09 was denied. Do not widen the rule to any host to make the job pass. List an owner for the service account. Separate this pair from a rule that only names a subnet. Stop when the ticket shows who asked, what they asked, and which host was allowed to ask.\n',
        'exam': 'The asker is the subject, and the resource is the system. Service accounts are subjects. A database is a system when it is being asked. A rule that names only one side is incomplete. A deny for the right secret from the wrong host can be success. Trusting a whole subnet skips this naming. Do not swap the words because the data feels more important than the job.\n',
    },
    'Policy enforcement point': {
        'body': 'The policy enforcement point is the component that actually opens or closes the session. It is the gate that moves. A decision sitting in a log does not stop a user. A message that nobody enforces does not stop a user. This component enables the connection, watches it, and can terminate it when the decision changes.\n\nIt sits in front of the system being asked. Common forms are an application gateway, a proxy, or an agent on the host that will not let the application listen except through the controlled path. The enforcement point does not invent allow or deny. It receives that result from the policy administrator, which received it from the engine. A direct route that reaches the server and skips the gateway is a bypass, and the control is then only a diagram. The gate should be able to end a session that is already open, not merely refuse the next login, because revoke is part of the design.\n\nOn ticket ZT-443, subject j.patel requested mail. Gateway gw-app-02 logged session 88af, action DROP, reason PA_DENY, on port 443. The user saw the application fail. The engine had returned DENY a moment earlier, and the administrator had forwarded that result. Priya saved the gateway line next to those two earlier lines. The gateway line is the enforcement evidence. In a second test, an engineer reached the mail host on a management address that skipped gw-app-02, and the session stayed up despite the same deny. That bypass is a broken gate, not a broken decision.\n\nDefenders place an enforcement point on every sensitive application and test the drop from a normal client. Alert on traffic that reaches the server subnet while avoiding the gateway. Align the gateway clock with the engine so the three lines can be ordered. Do not store the only copy of the business rules on the gateway and then call the gateway the engine. The gateway may cache a result briefly. It should not become a second, unreviewed policy source. When the gate is down, fail closed so an outage is not a wide open door.\n\nStudents name the engine when they see a drop, because they remember that something decided. The drop is this component. Students name this component when they only have a reasoned ALLOW in the engine log, and that log is the decision, not the moving gate. A human at a two-door lobby is a physical control, and the stem wants this name when the story is a session being cut. The separating fact is the component that opened or closed the path. Session 88af on gw-app-02 is that component in action.\n',
        'r0': 'The policy enforcement point opens, watches, or terminates the session. It does not weigh the signals. On ZT-443, gw-app-02 dropped session 88af for j.patel on port 443 with reason PA_DENY, after the engine deny was forwarded. A management address that skipped the gateway left the session up, which is a bypass. Revoke must end a live session, not only the next login. Agents and proxies count when they are the component that moves. Failing closed matters when the gate itself is down. Do not relocate the rule logic onto the gateway and rename it the engine. Evidence is the drop or the open, tied to the decision id that caused it.\n',
        'r1': 'This is the door that actually moves. A written yes does nothing until the door moves. A written no does nothing until the door moves. The door does not decide the question. It follows the answer it was given. It should be able to shut a session already open. A side path around the door makes the door a picture. If the door is broken, stay shut rather than swing wide. A person at a lobby is a different kind of door. Point at the drop or the open when you use this name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture the gate at a clinic loading dock. A clerk inside has already decided whether the truck may enter, and a runner has carried that answer to the gatehouse. The arm of the gate is what rises or stays down. The arm does not read the purchase order. If drivers learn a side lane with no arm, the gatehouse becomes scenery. An arm that cannot lower again cannot undo a truck that was waved in at dawn. In the mail path, gw-app-02 is that arm. Session 88af staying down is the arm obeying a no, and the management address that skipped it is the side lane.\n',
        'r4': 'Collect the gateway line for session 88af on ZT-443. Confirm it says DROP with reason PA_DENY on port 443. Place it after the engine result and the handoff in time order. Test whether a management address can skip gw-app-02. Do not rewrite gateway config while you are only gathering the drop. Note whether a live session can be ended, not only a new login. Stop when the ticket shows the component that opened or closed the path.\n',
        'exam': 'The moving gate is this component. The weighed decision is the engine, not the gate. The handoff message is the administrator, not the gate. A bypass path means the gate is not actually in front of the system. Ending a live session is part of enforcement. A lobby guard is a physical control, not this session component. Fail closed when the gate is down. The stem to recognize is a dropped or opened session tied to a decision.\n',
    },
    'Bollards': {
        'body': 'Bollards are short sturdy posts that stop a vehicle before it reaches a door, a storefront, or a pedestrian area. Bollards matter because a clinic entrance can be rammed by a car even when every network control is perfect. Bollards on ticket 7233 were steel posts at the curb that kept a vehicle from jumping onto the walkway at the main doors. Bollards in that incident did not stop people on foot, and they were not a login gateway and not a two-door lobby. Bollards are a physical preventive control aimed at vehicles, including accidents and deliberate ramming. Bollards may be fixed, removable for deliveries, or retractable, and a removable post that is left out is not protection. Bollards are often rated to stop a stated vehicle mass at a stated speed, using older K ratings or ASTM F2656 ratings such as M30 or M50. Bollards spaced so a car cannot pass between them, while people on foot still can, are doing the job correctly. Bollards are not an access-control vestibule, which manages people through interlocking doors, and they are not the logical gate that drops a session. Bollards: Stones or posts in front of a shop window that stop a runaway cart are the picture of bollards. Bollards fail when they are decorative plastic, too widely spaced, or removed for a loading event and never replaced. Bollards also fail if a driveway path simply goes around them to the same doors. Bollards: Ticket 7233 should include photos, spacing, and whether the posts are rated or merely architectural. Choose bollards when the threat is a vehicle reaching a facade or a crowd. Bollards: Choose a vestibule when the threat is a person tailgating through a lobby. Bollards: Choose a policy enforcement point when the threat is a session or a packet. Write bollards when you can point at posts in the vehicle path. Bollards: A fence may slow a person and still let a truck drive through a gate, so the gate needs bollards or an equivalent barrier. Bollards: Keep the curb and the clinic doors in the story, because that vehicle path is bollards. Bollards: Lighting and cameras help responders see bollards being challenged, and they do not replace the posts. Crash-rated bollards belong where a vehicle-borne threat is in the assessment, not on every interior hallway. Review bollards after construction, because temporary gaps become permanent if nobody puts the post back. Bollards are preventive for cars and irrelevant to a stolen password. Plan the pedestrian gap so bollards do not become a trip hazard or a barrier to stretchers. Bollards maintenance should include a monthly photo so a missing post is noticed before an incident. Bollards at loading docks need a procedure for who may remove them and how soon they return. Bollards specifications belong in the same folder as the site assessment that named the vehicle threat.',
        'r0': 'Bollards are vehicle barriers made of short sturdy posts, fixed, removable, or retractable. Rated bollards reference crash standards such as older K ratings or ASTM F2656 M30 and M50 classes. Bollards: Spacing should stop a car and still allow people on foot, which is the bollards design test. Ticket 7233 is bollards because posts kept a vehicle off the clinic walkway. Bollards: Access-control vestibules stop tailgating people and are not bollards. Bollards: Policy enforcement points drop sessions and are not bollards. Bollards: Decorative or missing posts are failed bollards. Bollards: A path around the posts defeats bollards. Choose bollards for vehicle threats to doors and storefronts. Bollards: Fences do not automatically cover the vehicle gate, so bollards may still be required there. Cameras do not substitute for bollards.',
        'r1': 'Bollards are stout posts that halt vehicles. Bollards stand in front of doors and windows. Bollards can be fixed or removable. Bollards should leave room for people on foot. A plastic lookalike is weak bollards. Bollards: A missing post during deliveries weakens bollards. Bollards are not a tiny two-door room. Bollards are not a software gate on a session. Bollards do not care about a stolen password. Bollards: Photograph the spacing when you inspect bollards. Bollards: A driveway around the posts defeats bollards. Bollards: Stretchers still need a safe path near bollards.',
        'r2': 'Bollards resemble short stone posts set in front of a bakery window so a runaway cart cannot smash the glass. Bollards are those posts, and they are aimed at wheels rather than at a person walking. Bollards: If the posts are far enough apart for the cart, bollards in this picture have failed. Bollards: Interlocking lobby doors that pass a single walker are another scene and are not bollards. Bollards: A clerk who refuses a session at a counter is a different scene and is not bollards. Hold bollards on stopping the vehicle before the glass. Bollards: Removable posts that stay in the shed are not bollards while the window is bare. Bollards: The picture stops at the posts and the cart, not at paperwork.',
        'r4': 'Photograph post spacing for Bollards on ticket 7233. Bollards: Confirm the posts are rated rather than decorative Bollards. Bollards: Walk the driveway for a bypass around Bollards. Separate Bollards from lobby doors that cycle one person at a time. Separate Bollards from any application gateway. Check that removable Bollards were put back after the last delivery. Bollards: Stop when vehicle blocking is the fact on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'Vehicle posts are bollards. Bollards: A two-door people trap is not bollards. A session-dropping gateway is not bollards. Bollards: Crash ratings such as ASTM F2656 belong to bollards discussions. Bollards: Wide gaps and plastic posts are failed bollards. Bollards: A path around the line defeats bollards. Bollards do not solve a stolen password.',
    },
    'Access-control vestibule': {
        'body': "An access-control vestibule is a small room with two doors that cannot be open at the same time. The outer door must close before the inner door will unlock. Older exam wording calls the same room a mantrap. The names are not opposites. A tailgater who follows a badged person through the first door is stuck in the room, and the office stays closed until the desk acts or the second person presents a badge of their own.\n\nThe mechanism is an interlock wired between the two strikes. The controller watches door position, not just badge events. If the outer door is still ajar, the inner strike stays locked even for a valid badge. That is stricter than a single reader, where a second person can walk through on the first person's access if they stay close. The room needs to be small enough that a crowd cannot pack in, and visible enough that reception can see who is waiting. Video of the room supports a later argument about who was inside. The interlock is the preventive part. The camera is a record after the fact.\n\nClinic lobby doors D1 and D2 form that room. At 08:14 the badge of A. Singh opened D1. A second person had no badge. The controller log recorded interlock hold on D2, and the inner strike did not release. Reception left the inner door locked until the visitor signed the book and a host came down. Ticket PHY-61 holds the badge event, the interlock line, and a still from the camera showing both people in the room with D2 shut. A single door at the same lobby, tested the year before, had let a follower through because nothing forced the first door to close first.\n\nDefenders test the interlock on a schedule, including a power cut, and write down whether each door fails locked or unlocked. A fail-unlocked inner door during a power cut undoes the room for the length of the outage. Do not prop either door with a wedge, and treat a wedge as a broken control rather than as a convenience. Pair the hardware with a person who can see the room, because the strikes cannot tell a planned escort from a stranger without a procedure. Keep the release of the inner door as its own logged action.\n\nStudents call any badge door by this name. One reader on one leaf is not a vestibule. A turnstile is a single lane, not two interlocking doors. A lobby whose two doors can stand open together is only a lobby. Students who think mantrap and vestibule are different designs are splitting one control into two. The separating fact is the interlock. If the first door must shut before the second opens, and PHY-61 can show a follower held between them, you are looking at this room.\n",
        'r0': "An access-control vestibule is a two-door interlock: the outer door closes before the inner door unlocks. Mantrap is the older name for the same room. At 08:14, A. Singh's badge opened D1, a second person had no badge, and the controller logged interlock hold on D2. PHY-61 keeps that line, the badge event, and a camera still of both people inside. A single reader does not force the first leaf shut, so a close follower can pass. Test power loss and record fail-locked or fail-unlocked for each door. A wedge in either leaf defeats the interlock. Reception release of the inner door should be its own log event. Evidence is the hold that stopped the second person.\n",
        'r1': 'This room has two doors, and only one opens at a time. The first door must shut before the second unlocks. A person who follows a badge can get stuck inside. The office stays closed until someone acts. One badge door is not this room. A spinning gate is not this room. Two doors that can open together are just a lobby. The older name mantrap means this same room. Test what the doors do when the power fails. Do not prop either door open.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clean-room entry where you step into a tiny chamber and the door behind you has to latch before the door ahead will release. You cannot wave a friend through on your heels, because the second leaf never opens while the first is ajar. A single locked door down the hall does not create that pause. A wide lobby with both leaves often open does not create it either. The chamber is the control, not the sign that asks people to badge in. At the clinic, D1 and D2 are that chamber. The hold on D2 at 08:14 is the second leaf refusing to open while a follower stood inside.\n',
        'r4': 'Pull the 08:14 badge event for A. Singh on D1. Pull the interlock hold for D2 and attach both to PHY-61. Save the camera still that shows the second person inside with D2 shut. Do not call a one-leaf reader this room. Record fail-locked or fail-unlocked for each door after a power test. Note any wedge as a broken interlock. Stop when the ticket shows the first door closing before the second can open.\n',
        'exam': 'Two interlocking doors are this control. Mantrap is the older name, not a rival design. A single badge door is not it. A turnstile is not it. Two doors that can be open together are a lobby. The follower stuck between the doors is the stem to recognize. Fail-unlocked on power loss is a failure mode. A camera records the room and does not replace the interlock.\n',
    },
    'Fencing': {
        'body': 'Fencing marks a boundary and delays entry to a yard, a roof edge, or a lot, and it works best with light and someone watching. Fencing matters because a generator yard without a visible boundary invites casual climbing that a camera then has to interpret. Fencing on ticket 7455 surrounded the generator yard with chain link tall enough that a casual climb was obvious on the adjacent camera. Fencing in that design delayed and exposed the climber, and it did not record the scene by itself and did not escort the person away. Fencing height is a classic exam detail: about three to four feet discourages casual trespass, six to seven feet is harder to climb, and eight feet or more with barbed or razor tape is aimed at determined intruders. Fencing gauge matters on chain link, because a lower gauge number means thicker wire that is harder to cut. Fencing should include a clear zone on both sides so hiding spots do not cancel the view. Fencing is not video surveillance, which records or watches, and it is not a person who can challenge and escort. Fencing: A garden fence that does not stop a determined thief but shows who stepped over is the picture of fencing, scaled up to a critical yard. Fencing fails when gates are left unlocked, when vegetation covers the mesh, or when a ladder is stored against the outside. Fencing also fails as a vault: delay is not the same as impossibility, so alarms and response still matter. Fencing: Ticket 7455 should note height, top guard, gate lock, and whether the camera actually sees the climb. Choose fencing when the stem shows a perimeter barrier that marks and slows. Fencing: Recording points at cameras, and a person who can intervene points at guards, which are separate labels from fencing. Write fencing when you can point at the barrier and the boundary it declares. Vehicle gates in fencing often need a separate vehicle barrier, because mesh will not stop a truck. Fencing: Keep the generator yard in the story, because the obvious climb is fencing doing its delay-and-reveal job. Roof fencing is the same idea when equipment sits where a climber could reach it from a neighboring structure. Review fencing after landscaping contracts, because new shrubs become ladders. Fencing signs can add a warning effect, and the mesh is still the fencing control. Without maintenance, fencing becomes a suggestion. Fencing inspections should walk the full perimeter, including the corners cameras do not favor. Fencing repairs ought to be ticketed the day a cut is found, not at the next quarterly stroll. Fencing gates need the same attention as the mesh, because the gate is where most entries actually happen. Fencing records should note the date a section was repaired so later incidents have a history.',
        'r0': 'Fencing marks and delays entry and should be paired with lighting and observation. Fencing: Common teaching heights are three to four feet for casual deterrence, six to seven feet to resist climbing, and eight feet with top guard for higher threat. Fencing: Chain-link gauge uses a lower number for thicker wire, which is a fencing exam detail. Clear zones keep fencing visible. Ticket 7455 is fencing because the generator-yard mesh made a casual climb obvious. Fencing: Video surveillance records, and security guards intervene, so those are not fencing. Fencing: Unlocked gates and stored ladders defeat fencing. Fencing delays and does not replace response. Choose fencing for a perimeter barrier rather than for a recording or a person. Fencing: Vehicle gates need more than mesh, so fencing alone may not stop a truck. Vegetation against the mesh cancels fencing visibility.',
        'r1': 'Fencing shows where the property starts. Fencing slows a person who wants to cross. Fencing works better with light and a watcher. Fencing: A short fence discourages casual hops. Fencing: A taller fence with sharp wire is more serious fencing. Fencing is not the camera that records the climb. Fencing is not the person who walks over to ask questions. An open gate cancels fencing. Bushes against the mesh hide fencing problems. A ladder left outside defeats fencing. Fencing buys time rather than making entry impossible. Fencing: Name the boundary when you mean fencing.',
        'r2': 'Fencing resembles a garden fence that will not stop a determined thief and still shows that someone stepped over. Fencing is the boundary and the delay, not the album of pictures and not the neighbor who comes outside. If the gate hangs open, fencing in this picture is only a suggestion. Fencing: A shop camera over a shelf is a different scene and is not fencing. Fencing: A clerk who asks who you came to see is a different scene and is not fencing. Hold fencing on the line that marks the yard and slows the crossing. Fencing: Light makes the crossing visible, and the light is a partner rather than the fencing itself. Fencing: The picture stops at the barrier and the obvious step over it.',
        'r4': 'Fencing: Measure height and note top guard for Fencing on ticket 7455. Fencing: Check gate locks and clear zones along Fencing. Fencing: Look for ladders or shrubs that defeat Fencing. Separate Fencing from the camera that recorded the climb. Separate Fencing from any guard who responded. Fencing: Confirm vehicle gates have more than mesh if Fencing must stop a truck. Fencing: Stop when delay and boundary are the control you name.',
        'exam': 'Boundary plus delay is fencing. Recording is not fencing. A responding person is not fencing. Height and top guard are fencing exam details. Fencing: Lower gauge means thicker chain link on fencing items. Fencing: Open gates and adjacent ladders defeat fencing. Fencing is not a substitute for response. Open gates are failed fencing even when the mesh is tall.',
    },
    'Video surveillance': {
        'body': 'Video surveillance records or watches a space so you can see what happened, and sometimes who, after the moment has passed. Video surveillance matters because a badge log can miss a door that was propped, while the loading-dock image still shows the prop at 02:10. Video surveillance on ticket 7566 showed a person bracing the dock door at 02:10, which the badge system never recorded as an entry. Video surveillance in that clip is evidence after the fact, and it did not lock the door and did not challenge the person. Video surveillance is primarily detective, and it becomes deterrent only when people can see the cameras and believe they work. Video surveillance needs light, time synchronization, and a retention period, or the clip is dark, undated, or already overwritten. Video surveillance may use fixed cameras or pan-tilt-zoom cameras, and infrared illuminators help the camera see in the dark without being the same thing as an intrusion sensor. Video surveillance is not fencing, which delays a climb, and it is not a person who walks over to ask a question. Video surveillance: A shop camera that shows an empty shelf later, without having held the door, is the picture of video surveillance. Video surveillance fails when the lens is covered, the clock is wrong, the retention is a few hours, or nobody is assigned to review alarms. Video surveillance also fails as a preventive control if the design assumes the camera will stop an entry by itself. Video surveillance: Ticket 7566 should include the timestamp, the camera name, and the note that the badge log was silent. Choose video surveillance when the stem shows a recording or a live watch that explains a past scene. Video surveillance: Choose fencing when the stem shows a barrier, and choose security guards when a person intervenes. Write video surveillance when you can point at the image and what it proved. Video surveillance: Privacy rules may limit where video surveillance may point, especially toward private rooms, so aim and signage belong in the design. Video surveillance: Keep the propped door at 02:10 in the story, because the missed badge event is why video surveillance mattered. Video surveillance: Motion-triggered recording saves storage and can miss a slow approach if the sensitivity is wrong. Video surveillance: Export original files for investigations so video surveillance evidence is not only a recompressed phone photo of a monitor. Video surveillance: A visible dome with no recorder is closer to a warning than to useful video surveillance. Review who can delete video surveillance files, because the suspect should not be the retention admin. Video surveillance supports investigations and does not replace the lock. Without a working lamp, video surveillance at that dock is a black frame. Video surveillance signage can strengthen the warning effect while the recording remains the detective value.',
        'r0': 'Video surveillance records or monitors a scene and is mainly detective, deterrent only if obvious. Ticket 7566 is video surveillance because the dock camera showed a propped door at 02:10 that badges missed. Video surveillance: Light, clock sync, and retention are required for useful video surveillance. Video surveillance: Infrared illuminators help cameras see and are not intrusion sensors. Video surveillance: Fencing delays entry, and security guards can intervene, so those are not video surveillance. Video surveillance: A camera does not lock a door, so it is not preventive by itself. Choose video surveillance when the evidence is the image. Video surveillance: Covered lenses, short retention, and wrong clocks ruin video surveillance. Privacy limits where video surveillance may aim. Video surveillance: Deletion rights on the recorder are part of trustworthy video surveillance. Video surveillance: Motion-only recording can miss slow movement if video surveillance sensitivity is poor.',
        'r1': 'Video surveillance remembers the scene. Video surveillance does not lock the door. Video surveillance can show who propped a door. Video surveillance needs light to see. Video surveillance needs a correct clock. Video surveillance needs to keep the clip long enough. Video surveillance: A visible camera may scare people, and that is extra. Video surveillance: A hidden camera only helps after you look. Video surveillance is not the fence. Video surveillance is not the person who can ask questions. A dark picture means video surveillance failed that night. Video surveillance: Name the camera and the time together.',
        'r2': 'Video surveillance resembles a shop camera above a shelf that later shows the shelf empty. Video surveillance does not hold the door shut while the item leaves. Video surveillance is the memory of the scene, not the lock and not the clerk who might have spoken. Video surveillance: If the shop is dark, video surveillance in this picture records nothing useful. Video surveillance: A garden boundary is a different scene and is not video surveillance. Video surveillance: A person who walks over and asks a question is a different scene and is not video surveillance. Hold video surveillance on what can be shown afterward. Video surveillance: The picture stops at the recording, not at prevention.',
        'r4': 'Video surveillance: Export the 02:10 clip for Video surveillance on ticket 7566. Video surveillance: Confirm the clock on that Video surveillance file matches the badge system. Video surveillance: Note that the badge log missed the prop which Video surveillance caught. Separate Video surveillance from the fence around the yard. Separate Video surveillance from any guard response. Video surveillance: Check retention and who can delete Video surveillance files. Video surveillance: Stop when the image is the evidence, not a claim that the camera locked the door.',
        'exam': 'Video surveillance: A recording of a past scene is video surveillance. A fence is not video surveillance. A guard is not video surveillance. Video surveillance: Cameras do not by themselves prevent entry. Video surveillance: Visible cameras can also deter, and hidden ones mostly detect. Video surveillance: Wrong clocks and short retention weaken video surveillance. Video surveillance: Illuminators for cameras are not the same as intrusion sensors.',
    },
    'Security guards': {
        'body': "Security guards are people who can challenge, escort, and respond when a sensor or a camera cannot judge intent. Security guards matter because a propped door on video still needs someone who will walk over and deal with the person. Security guards on ticket 7677 walked a visitor to the conference room and stayed until the host arrived, instead of pointing at a map. Security guards in that escort used judgment, which a fence cannot do and a recording cannot do in the moment. Security guards can be deterrent by being visible, detective by noticing odd behavior, and preventive when they physically stop entry. Security guards are limited by cost, fatigue, and social engineering, because a confident story can talk a person into an exception. Security guards should work from post orders that say what to check, whom to call, and when to refuse. Security guards are not fencing, which is a barrier, and they are not video surveillance, which is a recording. Security guards: A receptionist who actually looks up and asks who you are here for is a softer form of the same idea as security guards. Security guards fail when they abandon the desk, share badges, or skip the escort the post orders require. Security guards also fail if they have no radio or duress path when a situation turns physical. Security guards: Ticket 7677 should name the guard, the visitor, the host, and the time the escort ended. Choose security guards when the stem shows a person who can question and act. Security guards: Choose video surveillance when the stem shows only a recording, and choose fencing when the stem shows mesh or a gate. Write security guards when human judgment is the control you can point at. Security guards: Two-person rules for sensitive rooms are often carried out by security guards or by the staff they supervise. Security guards: Keep the escort to the conference room in the story, because that human action is security guards. Security guards: A guard who watches monitors is still security guards if they can dispatch a response, and the monitors alone are video surveillance. Security guards: Background checks and training are part of making security guards trustworthy. Security guards: Review post orders after the building changes, or security guards will follow a map that no longer matches the doors. Security guards: Contract guards need the same post orders as employees, or the logo on the shirt becomes the only standard. Security guards cannot be everywhere, so they pair with locks and cameras rather than replacing them. Without authority to refuse, security guards become greeters. Security guards shift overlaps should include a spoken handoff so a visitor mid-escort is not abandoned. Security guards incident notes should name the host who accepted the visitor, not only the guard's own initials.",
        'r0': 'Security guards are humans who challenge, escort, and respond, which sensors cannot fully do. Visible security guards deter, observant ones detect, and blocking ones prevent, depending on the action. Ticket 7677 is security guards because a visitor was escorted until the host arrived. Post orders define what security guards must check and when they must refuse. Security guards: Social engineering is a primary way security guards are defeated. Security guards: Fencing is a barrier and video surveillance is a recording, so neither is security guards. Choose security guards when a person judges intent and acts. Security guards: A receptionist who challenges visitors can function as security guards. Security guards: Two-person rules often rely on security guards or supervised staff. Security guards: Contract staff still need post orders or they are not reliable security guards. Security guards: Fatigue and abandoned posts are security guards failure modes.',
        'r1': 'Security guards are people who can ask why you are here. Security guards can walk you to the right room. Security guards can refuse to let you wander. Security guards can notice trouble a fence cannot judge. Security guards: A camera can remember, and security guards can act now. Security guards can speak, while a fence only slows someone. Security guards: A smooth talker may fool security guards. Written post orders keep security guards consistent. An empty desk means security guards are not happening. Security guards: Name the escort when you describe security guards. Security guards: A greeter with no authority is not really security guards. Security guards should be paired with locks.',
        'r2': 'Security guards resemble a night clerk who looks up and asks who you came to see, then walks you there. Security guards are that question and that walk, not a camera and not a fence. Security guards: If the clerk never looks up, security guards in this picture are absent. Security guards: A recording of the lobby is a different scene and is not security guards. Security guards: A boundary of mesh outside is a different scene and is not security guards. Hold security guards on the human judgment. Security guards: A charming story that gets a wave-through is how security guards fail. Security guards: The picture stops when a person, not a device, decides and moves.',
        'r4': "Security guards: Read the escort notes for Security guards on ticket 7677. Security guards: Confirm post orders required that escort before praising Security guards. Security guards: Ask whether the visitor was challenged or only greeted. Separate Security guards from dock camera footage. Separate Security guards from the yard fence. Security guards: Check duress or radio procedures for Security guards. Security guards: Stop when a person's judgment and action are the control.",
        'exam': 'Security guards: A person who can challenge and escort is security guards. A camera is not security guards. A fence is not security guards. Social engineering targets security guards. Post orders define security guards duties. Security guards: Visible presence can deter, and intervention can prevent. An unmanned post means security guards are not in effect.',
    },
    'Access badges': {
        'body': "Access badges are tokens a door checks, and a lost or leftover badge must be disabled the same day the person should no longer enter. Access badges matter because a former contractor on ticket 7788 still opened the side door a week after the contract ended. Access badges in that failure were never revoked in the controller, so the card remained a valid something-you-have. Access badges do not prove the holder is the owner unless a second factor such as a PIN or a guard's look is added. Access badges at 125 kHz proximity are widely cloned with inexpensive tools, while 13.56 MHz smart cards can hold certificates and resist casual copying better. Access badges with magnetic stripes are easier to skim than modern smart cards and should not guard serious rooms alone. Access badges are bypassed by tailgating, which is why a reader without a vestibule or a watchful desk is incomplete. Access badges are not protective lighting, which only removes darkness, and they are not infrared sensors, which notice heat or a broken beam. Access badges: A hotel keycard that should die at checkout, not next month, is the picture of access badges. Access badges fail when termination checklists skip the controller, when spare cards sit in a drawer enabled, or when one card is shared. Access badges also fail open on some doors during a power cut, so know the fail mode before you trust the reader. Access badges: Ticket 7788 should include the card number, the disable time, and the entries that happened after the last work day. Choose access badges when the stem shows a card or token presented to a reader. Access badges: Choose lighting when the stem shows illumination, and choose an infrared sensor when the stem shows detection of heat or a beam. Write access badges when the token's life cycle is the issue, especially revoke-on-exit. Access badges: Photo badges help a guard match a face, and the photo is not the electronic credential by itself. Access badges: Keep the former contractor in the story, because the still-working card is access badges. Access badges: Visitor badges should expire the same day, or they become a stack of live keys. Access badges: Review badge reports for cards that have not been used in months and for cards used at odd hours. Access badges are something you have, and a copied card is still that factor until it is disabled. Without a prompt disable, access badges outlive the relationship they were meant to represent. Access badges: Multi-technology cards that speak both old proximity and newer smart protocols should have the weak interface turned off. Access badges audits belong on the same checklist as account disables. Access badges reports should be reviewed weekly for cards used after the recorded end date. Access badges enrollment should require a manager approval so spare cards are not minted from the front desk.",
        'r0': "Access badges are door tokens, and revocation must happen the day the person leaves. Access badges: 125 kHz proximity cards are easy to clone, and 13.56 MHz smart cards are stronger access badges. Access badges: A PIN or a visual check adds a second factor to access badges. Tailgating bypasses access badges entirely. Ticket 7788 is access badges because a former contractor's card still opened the side door. Access badges: Protective lighting and infrared sensors are different topics and are not access badges. Access badges: Shared cards and enabled spares defeat access badges. Access badges: Fail-open readers need to be known before relying on access badges. Choose access badges when the stem is about the token and its disablement. Access badges: Visitor cards should expire the same day as a matter of access badges hygiene.",
        'r1': 'Access badges are cards the door checks. Access badges must be turned off when someone leaves. Access badges can be stolen and still work until disabled. Access badges: A copied card can fool weak access badges. Access badges do not stop someone walking in behind you. A PIN makes access badges harder to abuse. Access badges are not the lamp over the door. Access badges are not a beam that notices a walker. Access badges: A hotel card that dies at checkout fits access badges. Access badges: Spare live cards are a pile of extra access badges. Access badges: Same-day shutoff is the rule for access badges. Access badges: Name the card and the disable time together.',
        'r2': "Access badges resemble a hotel keycard that ought to die at checkout rather than next month. Access badges are that card and the moment it stops working. Access badges: If the card still opens the room after the guest has gone, access badges in this picture failed. Access badges: A porch lamp belongs to illumination and is not access badges. Access badges: An invisible beam across a stockroom is a different scene and is not access badges. Hold access badges on the token and on canceling it. Access badges: A card everyone shares is not serious access badges. Access badges: The picture stops at the credential's life, not at lighting or at sensing motion.",
        'r4': 'Access badges: Find the card that still worked for Access badges on ticket 7788. Access badges: Disable it and record the time under Access badges. Access badges: Search for other live cards belonging to departed people. Separate Access badges from lamps and from motion beams. Check whether Access badges require a PIN on serious doors. Access badges: Confirm visitor cards expire the same day. Access badges: Stop when revocation, not illumination, is the fix you document.',
        'exam': 'A door token is access badges. Same-day revocation is the access badges requirement. Access badges: 125 kHz proximity is the clonable access badges technology. Access badges: 13.56 MHz smart cards are stronger access badges. Tailgating bypasses access badges. Lighting is not access badges. Access badges: An infrared beam is not access badges. Shared cards break access badges accountability.',
    },
    'Protective lighting': {
        'body': "Protective lighting clears darkness around doors, fences, and parking so people and cameras can actually see. Protective lighting matters because ticket 7899 found the rear-door camera useless until the lamp above it was replaced. Protective lighting in that repair made the existing camera meaningful, and the lamp itself did not badge anyone in or sense a beam break. Protective lighting is commonly taught as four types: continuous, standby or trip lighting that comes on with an event, movable search lighting, and emergency lighting when power fails. Protective lighting aimed outward as glare can hide a guard's position while lighting the approach, which is a classic design note. Protective lighting supports deterrence by making sneaking feel exposed, and it supports detection by giving cameras a picture. Protective lighting is not access badges, which are tokens, and it is not infrared sensors, which detect heat or a broken beam. Protective lighting: A porch lamp that keeps a package thief from working unseen is the picture of protective lighting. Protective lighting fails when lamps are out, when trees shadow the door, when light shines into a camera and blinds it, or when motion lights never trigger. Protective lighting also fails if the color is so harsh that camera images cannot show clothing color, which some sodium lamps do. Protective lighting: Ticket 7899 should show the dark frame before the lamp repair and the usable frame after. Choose protective lighting when the stem is about illumination, glare, or a camera that cannot see for lack of light. Protective lighting: A card or token points at badges, and heat or a beam points at sensors, which are not this lighting topic. Write protective lighting when darkness is the defect you correct. Protective lighting: Emergency lighting is for safe exit during a power cut and is not the same design goal as continuous protective lighting on a fence line. Protective lighting: Keep the dead lamp and the blind camera in the story, because that dependency is protective lighting. Protective lighting: Uniform coverage matters more than one blinding spotlight that leaves pockets of dark beside it. Review protective lighting at night, not at noon, or you will miss the real shadows. Backup power for protective lighting should be tested, especially for critical gates. Protective lighting: Vandal-resistant fixtures belong where lamps are easy to smash. Without maintenance, protective lighting becomes a dark pole. Protective lighting does not replace a lock, and a bright door can still be unlocked. Protective lighting: Document lamp types on the site plan so replacements match the protective lighting design. Protective lighting work orders should include a night retest, not only a daytime bulb swap. Protective lighting spares should match color so investigators are not handed a rainbow of useless footage. Protective lighting at the rear door should be retested after the lamp swap so the camera frame is usable.",
        'r0': 'Protective lighting removes darkness so people and cameras can see. Protective lighting: Four types are continuous, standby or trip, movable, and emergency protective lighting. Protective lighting: Glare lighting toward an approach can conceal a guard while exposing an intruder. Ticket 7899 is protective lighting because a dead lamp made the rear camera useless. Protective lighting: Access badges are tokens, and infrared sensors detect, so neither is protective lighting. Protective lighting: Blinding glare into a lens and deep shadows are protective lighting design faults. Choose protective lighting when the problem is visibility. Protective lighting: Sodium lighting can distort camera color and weaken protective lighting for investigations. Protective lighting: Emergency lights serve egress and are a different goal from fence-line protective lighting. Test protective lighting at night. Protective lighting: A bright unlocked door is still not secured by protective lighting alone.',
        'r1': 'Protective lighting makes sneaking harder. Protective lighting helps people see. Protective lighting helps cameras see. Protective lighting: A dead lamp can blind a camera at night. Protective lighting can stay on all the time. Protective lighting can turn on when something happens. Protective lighting can be a movable beam. Protective lighting can come on when the power fails. Protective lighting is not a card for the door. Protective lighting is not a sensor that feels heat. Dark corners mean protective lighting is incomplete. Protective lighting: Check the site at night before you trust protective lighting.',
        'r2': "Protective lighting resembles a porch lamp that keeps a package thief from working unseen. Protective lighting is the light, not a keycard and not an invisible trip beam. Protective lighting: If the lamp is out, protective lighting in this picture is gone and the porch is a hiding place again. Protective lighting: A hotel card belongs to tokens and is not protective lighting. Protective lighting: A beam across a doorway is a different scene and is not protective lighting. Hold protective lighting on removing darkness. Protective lighting: A light that shines into the watcher's eyes fails protective lighting for that watcher. Protective lighting: The picture stops at seeing, not at locking and not at sensing.",
        'r4': 'Protective lighting: Compare dark and lit frames for Protective lighting on ticket 7899. Protective lighting: Replace or repair the lamp and retest the camera at night. Protective lighting: Note glare and shadow pockets along the approach. Separate Protective lighting from badge tokens. Separate Protective lighting from heat or beam sensors. Protective lighting: Identify whether the fixture is continuous, trip, movable, or emergency. Protective lighting: Stop when visibility is the control you restored.',
        'exam': 'Illumination for seeing is protective lighting. Protective lighting: Continuous, standby, movable, and emergency are the four protective lighting types. Protective lighting: A dead lamp that blinds a camera is a protective lighting failure. Badges are not protective lighting. Protective lighting: Infrared intrusion sensors are not protective lighting. Protective lighting: Glare toward an intruder is a protective lighting technique. Protective lighting: Night testing is required to judge protective lighting.',
    },
    'Infrared sensors': {
        'body': 'Infrared sensors notice heat or a broken invisible beam, which fits doorways, short halls, and closet openings better than a huge open bay. Infrared sensors matter because ticket 8004 had a beam across the records closet that tripped after hours when someone stepped through. Infrared sensors in that closet were active beams, a transmitter and a receiver, and the alarm was the broken path rather than a badge read. Passive infrared sensors are different: they watch for a change in heat moving across zones, and they do not emit a beam you can break. Infrared sensors of the passive kind false-alarm near HVAC vents, heaters, and sometimes pets unless the unit is chosen to ignore small heat. Infrared sensors are not access badges, which are tokens, and they are not protective lighting, which only makes a scene visible. Infrared sensors are also not the infrared illuminators bolted to cameras, which help a lens see in the dark and do not alarm by themselves. Infrared sensors: A store doorway crossed by an unseen light, closer to a chime than to a scale, is the picture of infrared sensors when the device is an active beam. Infrared sensors fail when the beam is aimed at glass that reflects it, when the lens is dirty, or when the passive unit faces a sun-warmed window. Infrared sensors in a wide warehouse often cannot cover the volume, which is why a single beam is a poor choice there. Infrared sensors: Ticket 8004 should say whether the device was passive or active, because that choice changes the bypass and the false-alarm story. Choose infrared sensors when the stem shows heat-in-motion or a broken beam at a door or short hall. Infrared sensors: Weight on a mat points at pressure devices, and radio filling a large volume points at microwave devices. Write infrared sensors when detection physics is heat or a light beam, not a card and not a lamp. Dual-technology units pair infrared sensors with another method to cut false alarms, and the infrared half is still this topic if that is what the question describes. Infrared sensors: Keep the closet beam in the story, because the after-hours trip is infrared sensors. Infrared sensors: A person who crawls under an active beam can bypass infrared sensors that are mounted too high. Infrared sensors: Review mounting height and pet immunity before you blame infrared sensors for every night alarm. Infrared sensors: Power loss behavior should be known so infrared sensors do not silently stop. Infrared sensors detect, and they do not lock the door by themselves. Infrared sensors: Document active versus passive on the device list so the next technician does not swap the troubleshooting steps. Without that distinction, infrared sensors become a vague word for any gadget on the wall. Infrared sensors walk tests should include a crawl and a normal stride at the real mounting height. Infrared sensors tickets should record passive or active in the first line so the next shift does not guess.',
        'r0': 'Infrared sensors are either passive heat-motion detectors or active beams between a sender and a receiver. Passive infrared sensors do not emit a beam, and active infrared sensors do. Infrared sensors: HVAC vents and sun-warmed windows cause false alarms on passive infrared sensors. Ticket 8004 is infrared sensors because a closet beam broke after hours. Camera illuminators are not infrared sensors, because they do not alarm. Infrared sensors: Access badges and protective lighting are not infrared sensors. Infrared sensors: A single beam is a weak fit for a wide warehouse. Choose infrared sensors for doorways and short halls using heat or a beam. Infrared sensors: Crawling under a high beam bypasses active infrared sensors. Dual-technology units may include infrared sensors plus another method. Infrared sensors: Detection is not the same as locking the door.',
        'r1': 'Infrared sensors notice heat or a broken beam. Infrared sensors fit doors and short halls. Some infrared sensors feel body heat moving. Some infrared sensors use a beam you interrupt. Infrared sensors: A heater vent can trick infrared sensors. Infrared sensors are not a door card. Infrared sensors are not a lamp that helps you see. Infrared sensors are not a camera light for the dark. Infrared sensors struggle to cover one wide warehouse. Infrared sensors: Name whether the device beams or feels heat. Infrared sensors detect, and they do not lock the door. Infrared sensors: Mount the beam where people cannot duck it.',
        'r2': "Infrared sensors resemble an unseen thread of light across a stockroom doorway that notices when a person breaks it. Infrared sensors in this picture are that thread, or a sense of warmth moving, not a lamp and not a key. Infrared sensors: If the thread is higher than a crawling person, infrared sensors can be slipped under. Infrared sensors: Illumination from a porch lamp is a separate control and is not infrared sensors. Infrared sensors: A checkout card is a token topic and is not infrared sensors. Hold infrared sensors on noticing the crossing. Infrared sensors: A camera's helper light that does not alarm is not infrared sensors. Infrared sensors: The picture stops at detection in a short space, not at covering a giant room with radio waves.",
        'r4': 'Infrared sensors: Identify whether the closet device beams or senses heat for Infrared sensors on ticket 8004. Infrared sensors: Record the after-hours trip time beside Infrared sensors. Infrared sensors: Check HVAC vents that could false-alarm passive Infrared sensors. Separate Infrared sensors from badge readers and from lamps. Separate Infrared sensors from camera illuminators that do not alarm. Infrared sensors: Try a low crawl if you are testing an active beam. Infrared sensors: Stop when heat or a broken beam is the detection method you name.',
        'exam': 'Infrared sensors: Heat motion or a broken beam is infrared sensors. Infrared sensors: Passive units do not emit a beam, and active units do. Badges are not infrared sensors. Lighting is not infrared sensors. Camera illuminators are not infrared sensors. Infrared sensors: Wide open bays are a poor match for a single infrared sensors beam. Infrared sensors: HVAC causes false alarms on passive infrared sensors. Infrared sensors: Detection does not equal a locked door.',
    },
    'Pressure sensors': {
        'body': 'Pressure sensors alarm from weight on a mat, a pad, or a floor section where a foot has to land. Pressure sensors matter because ticket 8115 alarmed from a mat inside the records room while the door contact stayed quiet. Pressure sensors in that case revealed a person standing inside after the door switch had been defeated or held. Pressure sensors are spot detectors, not volumetric ones, so they only know about the patch they cover. Pressure sensors are not microwave sensors, which flood a volume with radio energy, and they are not ultrasonic sensors, which use sound people cannot hear. Pressure sensors: A loose stair that speaks only when a foot lands is the picture of pressure sensors. Pressure sensors fail when the mat is visible and people step over it, when furniture covers it, or when the sensitivity ignores a light step. Pressure sensors also fail if a heavy cart is stored on the mat and the alarm is then bypassed as a nuisance. Pressure sensors: Ticket 8115 should show the mat alarm time and the door-contact state that did not change. Choose pressure sensors when the stem shows weight at a specific place. Pressure sensors: Choose microwave sensors for a wide bay, and choose ultrasonic sensors only if the room will reflect sound well. Write pressure sensors when the evidence is weight, not motion in open air. Under-carpet pads are still pressure sensors even when you cannot see a mat. Pressure sensors: Keep the quiet door contact and the loud mat in the story, because that mismatch is pressure sensors earning their place. Pressure sensors: Animals and dropped boxes can trip pressure sensors, so placement should be where only the threat must step. Pressure sensors: Review bypass logs so a mat that is always shunted is not imaginary pressure sensors. Pressure sensors do not see someone who uses a different path around the pad. Combine pressure sensors with a door contact when you need both the opening and the step. Pressure sensors: Calibration belongs on the maintenance sheet for pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: Without a defined step point, pressure sensors are the wrong tool and a volumetric sensor should be considered instead. Pressure sensors: Document the mat location on the floor plan so movers do not cover pressure sensors with a cabinet. Pressure sensors detect presence by weight and do not identify who stepped. Pressure sensors sensitivity should be rechecked after carpet is replaced because pile height changes the trip point. Pressure sensors diagrams should show the bypass route a person could walk if the mat is obvious. Pressure sensors in records rooms should sit where a person must stand to reach the shelf, not only at the threshold. Pressure sensors maintenance should log the last time someone stepped on the mat during a test and heard the alarm. Pressure sensors that stay silent during that test are a failed control even if the mat still looks installed.',
        'r0': 'Pressure sensors alarm on weight at a mat, pad, or floor location. Pressure sensors: They are spot detectors, unlike volumetric microwave or ultrasonic sensors. Ticket 8115 is pressure sensors because the mat tripped while the door contact stayed closed. Pressure sensors: Stepping over a visible mat bypasses pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: A cart parked on the mat causes nuisance alarms and dangerous bypasses. Choose pressure sensors when feet must land in a known spot. Pressure sensors: Microwave sensors cover large open volumes and are not pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: Ultrasonic sensors use inaudible sound and are not pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: Under-carpet pads still count as pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: Door-contact mismatch plus a mat alarm is strong pressure sensors evidence. Pressure sensors: Weight detection does not name the person.',
        'r1': 'Pressure sensors notice weight where a foot must land. Pressure sensors can be a mat or a pad under a carpet. Pressure sensors watch a spot, not a whole room. Pressure sensors: People can step over visible pressure sensors. Pressure sensors can trip while the door switch stays quiet. Pressure sensors are not radio filling a big bay. Pressure sensors are not silent sound bouncing in a room. Pressure sensors: A heavy cart left on the mat ruins pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: Name the mat when you mean pressure sensors. Pressure sensors do not tell you who stepped. Place pressure sensors where the threat cannot walk around. Pressure sensors: A loose stair that speaks underfoot fits pressure sensors.',
        'r2': 'Pressure sensors resemble a loose stair that creaks only when a foot lands on that tread. Pressure sensors are the tread, not a ripple across a whole pond and not a squeak lost in curtains. Pressure sensors: If someone steps to the side, pressure sensors in this picture stay silent. Pressure sensors: Radio filling a warehouse is a different scene and is not pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: Sound that soft drapes swallow is a different scene and is not pressure sensors. Hold pressure sensors on weight at one place. Pressure sensors: A box stored on the tread makes pressure sensors shout until someone disconnects them. Pressure sensors: The picture stops at the footfall, not at identifying the walker.',
        'r4': 'Pressure sensors: Compare mat time and door-contact state for Pressure sensors on ticket 8115. Pressure sensors: Photograph mat placement so a bypass path is obvious. Pressure sensors: Check for furniture or carts sitting on Pressure sensors. Separate Pressure sensors from radio volumetric devices. Separate Pressure sensors from inaudible sound devices. Pressure sensors: Confirm the mat is not permanently bypassed. Pressure sensors: Stop when weight at a spot is the method you document.',
        'exam': 'Pressure sensors: Weight at a specific spot is pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: Microwave volume sensing is not pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: Ultrasonic sound sensing is not pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: Stepping over the mat bypasses pressure sensors. Pressure sensors: A quiet door contact plus a mat alarm is a classic pressure sensors clue. Parked carts cause false pressure sensors alarms. Pressure sensors do not identify the person.',
    },
    'Microwave sensors': {
        'body': "Microwave sensors flood a volume with radio energy and notice movement that disturbs the field, which fits large open rooms. Microwave sensors matter because ticket 8226 covered a warehouse bay too wide for a single beam, so the team chose a volumetric radio sensor. Microwave sensors in that bay used Doppler shift: motion changes the returned radio, and the unit alarms on that change. Microwave sensors can see through thin walls, glass, or partitions, which sometimes alarms on motion outside the protected room. Microwave sensors are not pressure sensors, which need weight on a pad, and they are not ultrasonic sensors, which listen for inaudible sound that soft furnishings absorb. Microwave sensors: A pond surface that ripples when anything moves, even at the far edge, is the picture of microwave sensors. Microwave sensors fail when fluorescent lights, moving fans, or loose metal vibrate in the field and create constant trips. Microwave sensors also fail the design intent if the unit is so sensitive that forklifts in the next aisle are the real source. Microwave sensors: Ticket 8226 should state why a beam was rejected and what the radio unit's coverage pattern is. Choose microwave sensors when the stem shows a large open volume and radio or Doppler detection. Microwave sensors: Choose pressure sensors for a mat, and choose ultrasonic sensors when the stem discusses sound and soft rooms. Write microwave sensors when the physics is radio energy in a volume, not weight and not a short doorway beam. Microwave sensors: Monostatic units keep transmitter and receiver together, and bistatic units separate them, which changes how you aim microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: Keep the wide bay in the story, because coverage that a beam cannot do is microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: Metal racks can shadow parts of the field, so a walk test is required after the layout changes. Review mounting height so microwave sensors cover the floor a crawler would use and not only the space above the racks. Microwave sensors detect motion in the field and do not identify the mover. Microwave sensors: Fluorescent-light interference is an old but still taught nuisance for some microwave sensors. Without a walk test, microwave sensors are a drawing rather than a proven pattern. Microwave sensors: Document the frequency band only if the site standard requires it, and still explain the volumetric job in plain terms. Pair microwave sensors with another technology when false alarms from outside motion are unacceptable. Microwave sensors are a detection choice for open space, not a lock. Microwave sensors seasonal reviews should repeat the walk test when holiday displays change the aisles. Microwave sensors aimed at exterior glass need a note explaining which outside motions are acceptable nuisance. Microwave sensors coverage sketches should be updated when racking is added, because metal shadows the field. Microwave sensors nuisance logs should separate outside-aisle motion from real interior trips. Microwave sensors purchased for a bay should be rejected if the pattern cannot see a crawl along the floor.",
        'r0': 'Microwave sensors are volumetric radio-motion detectors, often using Doppler shift. Microwave sensors: They suit wide spaces that a single beam cannot cover, as in ticket 8226. Microwave sensors: Penetration of thin walls can make microwave sensors alarm on outside motion. Microwave sensors: Pressure mats and ultrasonic sound devices are not microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: Fans, vibrating metal, and some lighting cause false trips on microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: Monostatic and bistatic aiming differ for microwave sensors. Choose microwave sensors for large open volumes sensed by radio. Microwave sensors: A walk test is mandatory after racks move. Microwave sensors do not identify who moved. Microwave sensors: Crawler height must be inside the pattern or microwave sensors miss a low approach. Microwave sensors: Combining technologies reduces outside-motion false alarms.',
        'r1': 'Microwave sensors notice motion in a big open room. Microwave sensors use radio energy, not a floor mat. Microwave sensors can feel movement beyond a thin wall. Microwave sensors beat one beam across a wide bay. Microwave sensors handle curtains better than sound sensors do. Fans can trick microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: Walk the space after you install microwave sensors. Microwave sensors do not say who moved. Microwave sensors: Racks can block part of the field. Microwave sensors: Name radio in the room when you mean microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: A pond ripple at the far edge fits microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: Do not call a weight mat microwave sensors.',
        'r2': "Microwave sensors resemble a pond whose whole surface ripples when something moves, even near the far bank. Microwave sensors are that wide disturbance, not a single loose stair and not a squeak lost in heavy curtains. Microwave sensors: If the pond is actually the neighbor's pond through a thin fence, microwave sensors may react to the wrong motion. Microwave sensors: Weight on one tread belongs with mats and is not microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: Sound swallowed by drapes is a different scene and is not microwave sensors. Hold microwave sensors on covering a large open volume. Microwave sensors: A fan that keeps the surface shaking will exhaust anyone listening for real ripples. Microwave sensors: The picture stops at motion in a wide field, not at naming the mover.",
        'r4': 'Microwave sensors: State why a beam could not cover the bay for Microwave sensors on ticket 8226. Microwave sensors: Walk-test the pattern, including low and behind racks. Microwave sensors: Note outside motion that trips Microwave sensors through thin walls. Separate Microwave sensors from mats and from inaudible sound units. Microwave sensors: Check fans and vibrating metal inside the field. Microwave sensors: Record monostatic or bistatic placement. Microwave sensors: Stop when radio volumetric detection is the method you name.',
        'exam': 'Microwave sensors: Radio motion in a volume is microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: A pressure mat is not microwave sensors. Ultrasonic sound is not microwave sensors. Microwave sensors: Wide bays are the usual microwave sensors setting. Microwave sensors: Wall penetration causes outside false alarms. Doppler shift is the microwave sensors idea to remember. Walk tests prove microwave sensors coverage. Microwave sensors do not identify the person.',
    },
    'Ultrasonic sensors': {
        'body': "Ultrasonic sensors do a volumetric job with sound people cannot hear, and they struggle when heavy curtains, carpet, and soft furniture swallow the reflections. Ultrasonic sensors matter because ticket 8337 rejected them for a plush conference room full of drapes and chose another technology instead. Ultrasonic sensors in that decision were a poor fit, which is as important as knowing where they would have worked. Ultrasonic sensors typically listen for Doppler change in reflected sound above the range of human hearing. Ultrasonic sensors false-alarm when air currents from HVAC move drapes or when a ringing telephone vibrates the field. Ultrasonic sensors are not microwave sensors, which use radio and cope better with soft rooms, and they are not pressure sensors, which only know about weight on a pad. Ultrasonic sensors: A bat's cry that bounces off a hard wall and gets lost in heavy curtains is the picture of ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors can work in a hard-walled storeroom with little fabric, where reflections stay strong. Ultrasonic sensors fail the conference-room case precisely because the room is designed to absorb sound. Ultrasonic sensors: Ticket 8337 should say the room was absorptive and name the technology that replaced the sound sensor. Choose ultrasonic sensors when the stem shows inaudible sound in a space that reflects it. Ultrasonic sensors: Choose microwave sensors for a drape-heavy or very large bay, and choose pressure sensors for a mat. Write ultrasonic sensors when the physics is sound, not radio and not weight. Do not confuse ultrasonic sensors with the audible door chime a shop plays on purpose. Ultrasonic sensors: Keep the drapes in the story, because absorption is the defining limit of ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors: Animals that hear high sound may react to ultrasonic sensors even when humans do not. Ultrasonic sensors: Review HVAC registers aimed at the unit before blaming ultrasonic sensors for random daytime alarms. Ultrasonic sensors: A glass office with hard surfaces is a kinder site for ultrasonic sensors than a fabric-lined boardroom. Ultrasonic sensors detect motion via sound reflections and do not identify the mover. Ultrasonic sensors: Document the frequency range only as a site detail, and lead with the absorption problem. Pairing ultrasonic sensors with another method is common where air movement cannot be tamed. Ultrasonic sensors are a detection choice that the room's softness can veto. Ultrasonic sensors should be reevaluated when a room is redecorated with new drapes or acoustic panels. Ultrasonic sensors false alarms during HVAC startup deserve a time correlation before anyone relocates the unit. Ultrasonic sensors in hard storerooms still need a walk test after boxes are stacked, because cardboard can soak up sound. Ultrasonic sensors should not be the only detector where people hang coats and drapes that move in drafts. Ultrasonic sensors documentation ought to say why the room was judged reflective enough for sound.",
        'r0': 'Ultrasonic sensors are volumetric detectors that use inaudible sound and Doppler shift on reflections. Ultrasonic sensors: Soft furnishings, drapes, and carpet absorb the energy and make ultrasonic sensors unreliable. Ticket 8337 is ultrasonic sensors because a drape-filled conference room was a bad site. Ultrasonic sensors: HVAC air and vibrating objects cause false alarms for ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors: Microwave sensors use radio and tolerate soft rooms better, and pressure sensors use weight. Hard-walled rooms suit ultrasonic sensors better than plush ones. Choose ultrasonic sensors only when reflections will survive. Ultrasonic sensors: Audible shop chimes are not ultrasonic sensors. Animals may hear ultrasonic sensors even if people do not. Ultrasonic sensors do not identify the mover. Ultrasonic sensors: Absorption is the exam limit to remember.',
        'r1': 'Ultrasonic sensors use sound people cannot hear. Ultrasonic sensors notice motion when that sound bounces back. Heavy curtains swallow ultrasonic sensors. Soft furniture weakens ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors fail in a plush meeting room. Ultrasonic sensors: A hard storeroom is a better place for ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors: Air from a vent can trick ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors are not radio in a warehouse. Ultrasonic sensors are not a mat underfoot. Ultrasonic sensors: A bat lost in drapes fits ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors: Name sound, not radio, for ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors do not say who moved.',
        'r2': "Ultrasonic sensors resemble a bat's cry that bounces off a hard wall and disappears into heavy curtains. Ultrasonic sensors are that cry and the lost echo, not a radio pond and not a creaking stair. Ultrasonic sensors: If the room is built to swallow sound, ultrasonic sensors in this picture cannot do their job. Ultrasonic sensors: Weight on a tread belongs with mats and is not ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors: Radio across a wide bay is a different scene and is not ultrasonic sensors. Hold ultrasonic sensors on reflected sound. Ultrasonic sensors: Moving cloth in a draft will imitate an intruder for ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors: The picture stops when the room's softness decides whether the cry comes back.",
        'r4': 'Ultrasonic sensors: Describe the drapes and carpet before accepting Ultrasonic sensors on ticket 8337. Ultrasonic sensors: If the room absorbs sound, reject Ultrasonic sensors and name the replacement. Ultrasonic sensors: Listen for HVAC drafts that would false-alarm Ultrasonic sensors. Separate Ultrasonic sensors from radio volumetric units. Separate Ultrasonic sensors from floor mats. Ultrasonic sensors: Prefer hard surfaces when you do place Ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors: Stop when inaudible sound and absorption are the reasons in the ticket.',
        'exam': 'Ultrasonic sensors: Inaudible sound and Doppler reflections are ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors: Drapes and soft rooms defeat ultrasonic sensors. Microwave radio is not ultrasonic sensors. Pressure mats are not ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors: Hard rooms are the fair test for ultrasonic sensors. HVAC movement causes ultrasonic sensors false alarms. Audible chimes are not ultrasonic sensors. Ultrasonic sensors do not identify the mover.',
    },
    'Honeypots': {
        'body': 'Honeypots are decoy systems that should never receive real work, so any touch is suspicious and worth a page. Honeypots matter because production servers are noisy, while a box nobody should log into makes the attacker obvious. Honeypots on ticket 8448 mimicked a file share, logged every login attempt, and paged the on-call person. Honeypots in that design held no real payroll data, which is mandatory if you do not want the decoy to become a leak. Honeypots come in low interaction, where services are emulated, and high interaction, where a real system offers richer behavior and more risk. Honeypots placed inside a live network to detect intruders are production honeypots, while research honeypots exist to study tools and can be more dangerous to run. Honeypots must be isolated so a compromise cannot pivot into real hosts or attack other organizations from your address space. Honeypots are not honeynets, which are whole decoy networks, and they are not bait documents or fake secrets. Honeypots: A dummy wallet on a counter, where the person who grabs it is the one you watch, is the picture of honeypots. Honeypots are detective controls, and they are a poor preventive control because they do not stop the intruder from also attacking real hosts. Honeypots are not entrapment in the legal sense used for government inducement, and calling them entrapment is a common exam miss. Honeypots: Ticket 8448 should show that no business account was allowed to use the decoy and that an alert fired on first contact. Choose honeypots when the stem shows one fake system. Honeypots: Several decoy systems watched as an intruder moves between them are honeynets, not a single honeypot. Honeypots: Choose honeyfiles or honeytokens when the bait is a document or a fake secret rather than a host. Write honeypots when you can point at a system that should have zero legitimate sessions. Honeypots: Cowrie-style emulation of a remote shell is an example of a low-interaction honeypot. Honeypots: Keep the fake share and the page in the story, because unused plus alarmed is honeypots. High-interaction honeypots need careful containment or they become a free attack platform. Review whether honeypots are patched enough to stay up and vulnerable enough to be interesting, which is a real tension. Honeypots: Legal and privacy review still applies before honeypots collect attacker traffic.',
        'r0': 'Honeypots are single decoy hosts that should see no legitimate work. Any connection to honeypots is suspicious and should alert. Low-interaction honeypots emulate services, and high-interaction honeypots expose more of a real system. Production honeypots sit where intruders might wander, and research honeypots study behavior at higher risk. Isolation is required so honeypots cannot attack outward. Honeypots: Honeynets are networks of decoys, and honeyfiles or honeytokens are data, not honeypots. Ticket 8448 is honeypots because a fake share paged on login attempts. Honeypots: Entrapment is the wrong legal label for private honeypots. Choose honeypots for one decoy system. Honeypots: Cowrie is a familiar low-interaction honeypot for shell emulation. Honeypots: Staff who store real files on the box have ended its life as honeypots.',
        'r1': 'Honeypots are a fake machine. Honeypots should never get real work. Honeypots call you when someone knocks. Honeypots are one box, not a whole fake street. Honeypots are not a fake document. Honeypots are not a fake password. Honeypots: A dummy wallet on a counter fits honeypots. Honeypots do not stop every other attack. Honeypots must not be a launch pad. Do not call private honeypots entrapment. Keep real secrets off honeypots. Honeypots: If staff use the box, it is no longer honeypots.',
        'r2': 'Honeypots resemble a dummy wallet left on a counter so the person who grabs it reveals themselves. Honeypots are that wallet-as-a-system, one object nobody on staff should touch. Honeypots: A whole row of empty shops would be a larger scene and is not a single honeypot. Honeypots: A marked bill in a drawer is a different bait and is not honeypots. Honeypots: A spare key that opens nothing is a secret-style lure and is not honeypots. Hold honeypots on one decoy that should stay untouched. Honeypots: If the wallet contains a real paycheck, the picture has become a dangerous leak. Honeypots: The picture stops when a touch on the unused system is the alarm.',
        'r4': 'Honeypots: Confirm no production account may use the decoy named in Honeypots on ticket 8448. Honeypots: Generate a test touch and verify Honeypots page the on-call. Check that Honeypots cannot route attacks toward real hosts. Separate Honeypots from a network of many decoys. Separate Honeypots from a bait file or a fake key. Honeypots: Record low or high interaction for the Honeypots build. Honeypots: Stop when one unused system is the control you describe.',
        'exam': 'One decoy system is honeypots. Honeypots: A network of decoys is not honeypots. A bait file is not honeypots. A fake credential is not honeypots. Honeypots: Low interaction emulates, and high interaction is riskier. Private honeypots are not legal entrapment. Isolation prevents honeypots from becoming attack infrastructure. Honeypots: Any legitimate use means it is no longer a clean honeypot.',
    },
    'Honeynets': {
        'body': 'Honeynets are small networks of decoys, used when you want to watch how an intruder moves, not just that one box was touched. Honeynets matter because a single fake host shows a knock, while a fake segment shows the tools and the path after the knock. Honeynets on ticket 8559 were a lab segment of servers that production accounts were forbidden to use. Honeynets in that segment included more than one system so lateral movement could be observed between them. Honeynets are often placed behind a honeywall that captures traffic and contains attacks so they do not escape. Honeynets cost more and risk more than one honeypot, because there is more surface and more to misconfigure. Honeynets are not a single honeypot, and they are not bait documents or powerless secrets. Honeynets: A row of empty storefronts, where footsteps are not customers, is the picture of honeynets. Honeynets fail when a production route leaks into the segment or when an admin uses a decoy as a scratch server. Honeynets also fail if the containment wall is omitted and the decoys can scan the internet. Honeynets: Ticket 8559 should list the decoy hosts, the ban on production accounts, and how containment is enforced. Choose honeynets when the stem says several decoy systems or a fake network. Honeynets: Choose honeypots when only one system is described. Honeynets: Choose a file or a token when no system is described at all. Write honeynets when the goal is watching movement across decoys. Research use of honeynets can record attacker tooling, and that goal is different from a simple internal tripwire. Honeynets: Keep the forbidden production accounts in the story, because separation from real work is honeynets. Document data-flow so honeynets logs do not accidentally store real customer content. Honeynets: Review firewall rules quarterly so a temporary test route does not become a permanent bridge. Honeynets are still detective in purpose, even though they are larger than one host. Without containment, honeynets are an ungoverned lab. Name honeynets only when the plurality of decoys is real. Honeynets reviews should confirm the honeywall still contains outbound scans.',
        'r0': "Honeynets are multiple decoy systems arranged so an intruder's path among them can be studied. Honeynets: A honeywall often captures and contains honeynets traffic. Honeynets: Production accounts should be barred from honeynets, as on ticket 8559. Honeynets: One fake host is a honeypot, not a honeynet. Honeynets: Files and fake secrets are not honeynets. Containment failure lets honeynets attack outward. Choose honeynets when the stem describes a fake segment or several decoys. Research honeynets may focus on tooling rather than a simple alert. Honeynets: Real data must not land on honeynets by accident. Honeynets: A bridge route into production ends the safety of honeynets. Honeynets remain a detective approach at a larger scale.",
        'r1': 'Honeynets are several fake buildings, not a single fake house. Honeynets let you watch someone move between decoys. Honeynets should be off limits to real work accounts. Honeynets need a wall so attacks cannot spill out. Honeynets cost more care than a single fake box. Honeynets: A row of empty shops fits honeynets. Honeynets: One wallet on a counter is not honeynets. A marked bill is not honeynets. A fake key is not honeynets. Honeynets: If staff store real files there, honeynets are contaminated. Honeynets: Name more than one decoy when you say honeynets. Keep real customer data off honeynets.',
        'r2': 'Honeynets resemble a row of empty storefronts where any footsteps are not customers. Honeynets are the whole row and the way someone walks from one door to the next. Honeynets: One dummy wallet is a smaller scene and is not honeynets. Honeynets: A marked bill in a till is a different bait and is not honeynets. Honeynets: A useless spare key is a credential lure and is not honeynets. Hold honeynets on movement across several decoys. Honeynets: If a shop in the row starts selling real goods, honeynets have been polluted. Honeynets: The picture stops when the path between empty places is what you study.',
        'r4': 'Honeynets: List every decoy host that belongs to Honeynets on ticket 8559. Honeynets: Prove production accounts cannot log into those Honeynets. Honeynets: Test that the containment wall blocks outbound attacks from Honeynets. Separate Honeynets from a single decoy host. Separate Honeynets from bait documents and fake secrets. Honeynets: Look for temporary routes that bridge Honeynets to real servers. Honeynets: Stop when several decoys and containment are both described.',
        'exam': 'Several decoy systems make honeynets. Honeynets: One decoy system does not make honeynets. A honeywall contains honeynets. A bait file is not honeynets. A fake credential is not honeynets. Production use contaminates honeynets. Outbound attack paths mean honeynets are not contained. Watching lateral movement is the honeynets goal. A bridge into production ruins honeynets containment.',
    },
    'Honeyfiles': {
        'body': "Honeyfiles are decoy documents that legitimate jobs never open, so an open, a copy, or an exfiltration is the signal. Honeyfiles matter because you may not notice a quiet copy of a real spreadsheet, while a file nobody should touch can alarm immediately. Honeyfiles on ticket 8660 were a workbook named salaries-full placed where payroll staff were told never to click, and an open created an alert. Honeyfiles in that folder were not real salary data, or the trap would have been another copy of the secret. Honeyfiles can carry a unique string or a remote beacon so the document phones home when opened off the share. Honeyfiles are not honeypots, which are systems, and they are not honeynets, which are networks of systems. Honeyfiles are not honeytokens, which are fake credentials, keys, or records whose use, not merely a file-open story, is the point. Honeyfiles: A marked bill in the till, recognized later because of the mark, is the picture of honeyfiles. Honeyfiles fail when staff are not told to avoid them and someone opens one during normal work. Honeyfiles also fail if the beacon is blocked and nobody notices the open locally. Honeyfiles: Ticket 8660 should show the file name, the account that opened it, and that payroll's procedure forbids that open. Choose honeyfiles when the stem shows a decoy document. Honeyfiles: A fake password, API key, or database row being used points at honeytokens. Honeyfiles: A decoy host points at honeypots rather than at a document. Write honeyfiles when opening or taking the document is the alarm. Honeyfiles: Keep the forbidden workbook in the story, because the open is honeyfiles. Place honeyfiles where an attacker would look and a careful employee would not. Honeyfiles: Review alerts so a single mistaken click is coached and a bulk copy is escalated. Honeyfiles are detective, and they do not encrypt the real payroll file. Without a clear do-not-open rule, honeyfiles become booby traps for your own staff. Honeyfiles: Name the document, not the server, when the bait is honeyfiles. Honeyfiles alerts should include the account, the host, and whether the file left the share.",
        'r0': 'Honeyfiles are decoy documents that legitimate work should never open. Honeyfiles: An open, copy, or outbound transfer of honeyfiles is the alert. Honeyfiles: Beacons or unique strings can show that honeyfiles were opened elsewhere. Ticket 8660 is honeyfiles because salaries-full was opened against instructions. Honeyfiles: Real salary content must not be stored inside honeyfiles. Honeyfiles: Honeypots are hosts, honeynets are decoy networks, and honeytokens are fake secrets. Choose honeyfiles for a document bait. Honeyfiles: Staff who are not warned will trip honeyfiles during ordinary work. Honeyfiles are detective and do not protect the real file by themselves. Honeyfiles: A blocked beacon still needs a local open alert or honeyfiles go quiet.',
        'r1': 'Honeyfiles are fake documents. Honeyfiles should never be opened by real jobs. Opening honeyfiles is the alarm. Copying honeyfiles off the share is also an alarm. Honeyfiles are not a fake computer. Honeyfiles are not a whole fake network. Honeyfiles are not a fake password by themselves. Honeyfiles: A marked bill in a drawer fits honeyfiles. Tell staff which file is honeyfiles. Honeyfiles: Do not put real secrets inside honeyfiles. Honeyfiles: A single curious click still needs a human look. Honeyfiles: Name the document when you mean honeyfiles.',
        'r2': "Honeyfiles resemble a marked bill in the till that you can recognize later if it turns up somewhere else. Honeyfiles are that marked document, not the cash register and not a key that opens nothing. Honeyfiles: If clerks are never told which bill is marked, honeyfiles in this picture will be spent by accident. Honeyfiles: A dummy wallet treated as an entire machine is a host lure and is not honeyfiles. Honeyfiles: A row of empty shops is a network lure and is not honeyfiles. Hold honeyfiles on the document that should stay untouched. Honeyfiles: A mark that cannot be seen later wastes honeyfiles. Honeyfiles: The picture stops when the document's movement is the signal.",
        'r4': 'Honeyfiles: Place the forbidden workbook and confirm the do-not-open rule for Honeyfiles on ticket 8660. Honeyfiles: Open it from a test account and verify Honeyfiles alert. Check that Honeyfiles contain no real salary rows. Separate Honeyfiles from decoy servers and decoy networks. Separate Honeyfiles from a fake API key. Record who opened Honeyfiles and from where. Honeyfiles: Stop when the document open is the evidence.',
        'exam': 'A decoy document is honeyfiles. A decoy host is not honeyfiles. A decoy network is not honeyfiles. Honeyfiles: A fake credential is a honeytoken, not honeyfiles. Opening the document is the honeyfiles signal. Honeyfiles: Real secrets inside the bait ruin honeyfiles. Unwarned staff will trip honeyfiles. Honeyfiles detect and do not replace encryption.',
    },
    'Honeytokens': {
        'body': 'Honeytokens are fake credentials, records, or keys that have no real privilege, so using them means the secret was stolen or leaked. Honeytokens matter because a quiet copy of a repository can sit unnoticed until the fake key is tried from the internet and alarms. Honeytokens on ticket 8771 were a dummy API key planted in a private repository that had no production rights. Honeytokens in that key triggered an alert the moment it was presented from an outside address. Honeytokens can be fake database rows, fake email addresses, or canary tokens that call home when used. Honeytokens are not honeyfiles, which alarm when a document is opened, even though a document might contain a honeytoken. Honeytokens are not honeypots or honeynets, which are systems and networks rather than a secret. Honeytokens: A spare house key that opens nothing, hidden where only a snooper would look, is the picture of honeytokens. Honeytokens fail when the fake secret accidentally works on a real service, which turns the trap into a live credential. Honeytokens also fail if they are so obviously fake that an attacker never uses them, or so well hidden that they are never stolen in a meaningful test. Honeytokens: Ticket 8771 should show the key id, the source address of the use, and proof the key had zero real privilege. Honeytokens: Exercise of a fake secret is the honeytokens case. Honeytokens: Choose honeyfiles when the stem shows a document open without emphasizing a usable secret. Honeytokens: A decoy machine is a honeypot, which is outside honeytokens. Write honeytokens when use of the bait proves theft or leakage. Honeytokens: Keep the repository key in the story, because presentation from the internet is honeytokens. Honeytokens: Rotate or revoke the monitoring behind honeytokens if the token format changes. Honeytokens: Review who can read the file that stores honeytokens so your own broad admin groups do not trip them daily. Honeytokens are detective evidence of exposure, not a substitute for removing real secrets from code. Without a distinct alarm channel, honeytokens look like ordinary authentication failures. Honeytokens: Name the fake secret, not the server, when the control is honeytokens. Honeytokens drills should fire the dummy key from a lab address and confirm the page arrives.',
        'r0': 'Honeytokens are fake secrets, keys, or records with no real authority. Using honeytokens is evidence of theft or leakage, as when ticket 8771 saw a dummy API key from the internet. Canary-style honeytokens call home on use. Honeytokens: Honeyfiles alarm on document access, and a file may hold a honeytoken but is not the same label. Honeytokens: Honeypots and honeynets are systems and networks, not honeytokens. Honeytokens: A honeytoken that actually works on production is a dangerous mistake. Choose honeytokens when a fake credential is exercised. Honeytokens: Obviously fake bait may never be used, which weakens honeytokens. Broad admin access can trip honeytokens during normal work. Honeytokens do not replace secret-scanning and removal of real keys. Distinct alerts keep honeytokens from hiding in ordinary failures.',
        'r1': 'Honeytokens are fake secrets. Honeytokens open nothing real. Using honeytokens proves someone took them. Honeytokens can be a dummy key in a private project. Honeytokens can be a fake row or a fake mailbox. Honeytokens are not a fake computer. Honeytokens are not a street of fake computers. Honeytokens are not just a document you open. Honeytokens: A spare key that fits no lock fits honeytokens. Never let honeytokens work on a real service. Honeytokens: If the bait is too silly, nobody will use honeytokens. Honeytokens: Name the secret, not the server, for honeytokens.',
        'r2': "Honeytokens resemble a spare house key that opens nothing and is hidden where only a snooper would look. Honeytokens are that useless key, and finding it in a stranger's hand means the drawer was searched. Honeytokens: A marked bill you recognize by ink is a different bait and is not honeytokens. Honeytokens: An emulated computer on a counter is a host decoy and is not honeytokens. Honeytokens: Several vacant storefronts together are a network decoy and are not honeytokens. Hold honeytokens on a secret that should never succeed. Honeytokens: If the key suddenly opens the real door, honeytokens have become a live danger. Honeytokens: The picture stops when use of the useless secret is the proof.",
        'r4': 'Honeytokens: Plant a no-privilege API key and record its id as Honeytokens for ticket 8771. Honeytokens: Trigger the key from a test address and confirm Honeytokens alert. Honeytokens: Prove the key cannot call production before you trust Honeytokens. Separate Honeytokens from a decoy document that merely should not be opened. Separate Honeytokens from decoy hosts and decoy networks. Honeytokens: Limit who can view the file that stores Honeytokens. Honeytokens: Stop when use of a powerless secret is the evidence.',
        'exam': 'Honeytokens: A fake secret with no power is honeytokens. Honeytokens: Using it proves exposure, which is the honeytokens point. A decoy host is not honeytokens. A decoy network is not honeytokens. Honeytokens: A document-open alert is honeyfiles, not automatically honeytokens. Honeytokens: A working production key is not a safe honeytoken. Canary callbacks are a honeytokens technique. Ordinary login failures can hide honeytokens if alerts are not distinct.',
    },
    'Change approval': {
        'body': 'Change approval is the recorded yes from someone allowed to accept the risk of an edit before that edit is made. Change approval matters because a firewall rule typed at midnight with no ticket and no approver has nobody who accepted the outage risk. Change approval on ticket 8882 was missing when an on-call admin changed a clinic rule without a change record. Change approval in a healthy process names the approver, the time, and the exact request, and it is not the same as the person who will perform the work. Change approval often sits with a change advisory board for normal changes, with a faster path for true emergencies that is still recorded. Change approval for standard, pre-authorized changes can be granted in advance, and that grant is still change approval rather than a free-for-all. Change approval is not change ownership, which names who will carry the work and answer afterward. Change approval is not the full list of stakeholders, who feel the impact and may not hold the authority to say yes. Change approval: A cafe owner initialing a menu change before the ink is final is the picture of change approval. Change approval fails when the requester approves their own high-risk edit, which breaks separation on systems that feed financial reports. Change approval also fails when emergency work is done and the after-the-fact record is never written. Change approval: Ticket 8882 should be completed by identifying who had authority and whether they actually said yes. The label change approval fits when the stem shows a decision to accept risk, or the lack of that decision. Change approval: Ownership is the wrong label when the only fact is a team name with no yes. Change approval: Stakeholder notice is the wrong label when the only fact is a missing signature from an approver. Write change approval when you can point at the yes or at its absence. Emergency change approval should name the incident that justified speed and the time the formal record was added. Change approval: Keep the midnight rule in the story, because no ticket and no approver is change approval failing. Change approval: Verbal hallway yes without a record will not survive an audit of change approval. Change approval: Multiple approvers may be required when the blast radius crosses teams, and each yes is part of change approval. Rejected requests are still change approval outcomes and should stay visible. Change approval: A tool that can push a config is not a substitute for change approval. Change approval: Review who is allowed to approve so departed managers are not still the only name on change approval routes. Change approval is the decision, recorded, before or immediately after an emergency, not the typing itself. Change approval routes should be tested so a departed manager is not the only person who can say yes. Change approval records should include the request id that matches the diff someone is about to type.',
        'r0': 'Change approval is a recorded acceptance of change risk by someone with authority. Change approval: Normal changes often use a change advisory board, and emergencies still need change approval on a short clock. Change approval: Standard changes may carry pre-granted change approval. Change approval: The implementer is not automatically the approver, which protects separation. Change approval: Change ownership names who carries the work, and stakeholders are people affected, so those are not change approval. Ticket 8882 lacks change approval because a midnight firewall edit had no ticket and no yes. Change approval: Self-approval of high-risk financial-system edits fails change approval separation. Change approval: After-the-fact silence on emergencies also fails change approval. Change approval: The label fits a yes or a missing yes, not a team nickname. Change approval: Verbal approval without a record is weak change approval. Change approval: Rejections should remain visible as change approval history.',
        'r1': 'Change approval is the recorded yes before the edit. Change approval names who accepted the risk. Change approval can be a board for ordinary edits. Change approval can be a fast path for real emergencies. Change approval still needs a record after a fast path. Change approval: The person doing the work is not always change approval. Change approval: A team nickname is not change approval. Change approval: People who will be inconvenienced are not automatically change approval. Change approval: A owner initialing a menu fits change approval. Change approval: No ticket at midnight means change approval did not happen. Change approval: Keep the yes in writing for change approval.',
        'r2': 'Change approval resembles a newspaper editor initialing a headline before the ink is committed. Change approval is that initial, not the reporter who will carry the story and not every reader who will feel it. Change approval: If the editor never sees the headline, change approval in this picture was skipped. Change approval: A single named cook responsible for a soup is the carrier of the work and is not change approval. Change approval: Warning the diners is a different duty and is not change approval. Hold change approval on the authorized yes. Change approval: A shout across the kitchen that nobody writes down is flimsy change approval. Change approval: The picture stops at the decision to accept the change, not at performing it.',
        'r4': 'Change approval: Open ticket 8882 and look for a recorded yes before the firewall edit. Change approval: Name the person who had authority to give Change approval. Change approval: If the edit was an emergency, record the after-action Change approval time. Separate Change approval from the individual who will roll the change back. Separate Change approval from everyone who should have been warned. Change approval: Reject self-approval on high-risk financial paths. Change approval: Stop when the yes, or its absence, is what the ticket shows.',
        'exam': 'Change approval: A recorded yes from an authorized person is change approval. Change approval: The implementer is not automatically change approval. Change approval: A team name is not change approval. Change approval: Affected users are stakeholders, not automatically change approval. Emergencies still require change approval. Missing midnight tickets fail change approval. Self-approval breaks separation inside change approval.',
    },
    'Change ownership': {
        'body': 'Change ownership names the one person who will carry the change and answer questions after it lands. Change ownership matters because a ticket that says only network team leaves nobody who will roll a rule back if mail breaks. Change ownership on ticket 8993 was blank in substance when the assignee field said network team and no human accepted the work. Change ownership is a name, a way to reach that person during the window, and a duty to coordinate the return path. Change ownership is not change approval, which is the yes that accepts risk and may come from a different person. Change ownership is not the stakeholder list, which can be long and still contain nobody accountable for execution. Change ownership: One cook who owns a new soup, rather than the kitchen in general, is the picture of change ownership. Change ownership fails when the named person is on leave during the window and no deputy is written down. Change ownership also fails when several people each think another one has it. Change ownership: Ticket 8993 should be corrected to a single reachable human before the window starts. The label change ownership fits when the stem asks who will perform and answer, not who approved. Change ownership: Approval can be a board while change ownership stays with one implementer, and both facts can be true. Change ownership: Stakeholder communication may be a task the owner performs, and the audience is still not the owner. Write change ownership when you can point at one name. Change ownership: Shared inboxes are a contact method, not a substitute for change ownership. Change ownership: Keep the mail-break rollback question in the story, because someone must be ready, and that someone is change ownership. After the change, change ownership includes staying available for the hypercare period you promised. Change ownership: Review ownership when contractors rotate, or the only person who understood the change has left. Change ownership: A runbook does not replace change ownership, because a document cannot answer the phone. Escalation paths support change ownership and do not rename the whole department as the owner. Without change ownership, success and failure are both anonymous. Change ownership: Put the phone number next to the name so change ownership is real at 02:00. Change ownership should be visible on the calendar entry, not only inside a closed chat. Change ownership: One owner can delegate tasks and still keep change ownership. Change ownership during hypercare includes answering within the promised minutes, not the next business day. Change ownership notes should say who performed the rollback if the primary owner called it. Change ownership cannot be a rotating mailbox that nobody watches during the window. Change ownership reviews after the change should be written by that named person.',
        'r0': 'Change ownership is one named person who carries the change and answers afterward. Change ownership: A department label such as network team is not change ownership, which ticket 8993 violated. Change ownership: The approver who accepted risk may be a different person from change ownership. Change ownership: Stakeholders feel impact and are not automatically change ownership. Change ownership: A deputy must be named if the owner will be away during the window. Change ownership: Hypercare availability is part of change ownership. Change ownership: Shared mailboxes do not replace a human change ownership assignment. Change ownership: Runbooks support the owner and are not change ownership themselves. Change ownership: The label fits the question of who will act and explain. Escalation lists supplement change ownership. Anonymous changes mean change ownership was never set.',
        'r1': 'Change ownership is one person by name. Change ownership carries the work. Change ownership answers questions after it lands. Change ownership: A whole team is not change ownership. Change ownership is not the person who only said yes. Change ownership: People who lose service are not automatically change ownership. Change ownership: Name a backup human if the owner is away. Change ownership: A shared inbox is not change ownership. Change ownership: A document cannot answer the phone for change ownership. Change ownership: Put a number next to the name for change ownership. Change ownership: Stay available after the change as part of change ownership. Change ownership stays with one person who hands out tasks.',
        'r2': 'Change ownership resembles one named cook who owns a new soup, not the kitchen as a crowd. Change ownership is that cook, including being there if the soup fails. Change ownership: A manager who only nods at the menu supplies the yes and is not change ownership. Change ownership: Diners who must be warned are a different group and are not change ownership. Hold change ownership on a single reachable person. Change ownership: If the cook is on holiday and nobody else is named, change ownership has a hole. Change ownership: A recipe card helps the cook and is not itself change ownership. Change ownership: The picture stops at personal accountability for carrying the change.',
        'r4': 'Change ownership: Replace the network team assignee with one person for Change ownership on ticket 8993. Change ownership: Add a phone or pager the owner will answer during the window. Change ownership: Name a deputy if that person might be offline. Separate Change ownership from the approver who accepted risk. Separate Change ownership from the list of affected departments. Change ownership: Confirm the owner can perform the rollback. Change ownership: Stop when one human, not a department, is accountable.',
        'exam': 'One named human is change ownership. Change ownership: A team name is not change ownership. Change ownership: The approver may be someone else. Change ownership: Affected users are not the owner by default. A deputy belongs in change ownership if the primary is away. Hypercare is part of change ownership. Change ownership: A runbook is not a substitute for change ownership.',
    },
    'Stakeholders': {
        'body': "Stakeholders are the people who feel a change: the requester, the operators, and anyone whose work stops or changes. Stakeholders matter because payroll was not told that a file server would reboot during Friday close, so the close failed even though a board had approved the window. Stakeholders on ticket 9004 were missing the payroll lead, who learned about the reboot from a dark share. Stakeholders are an audience and a communication duty, not the single owner who carries the work and not automatically the person who signs the risk acceptance. Stakeholders should be listed with how and when they will be told, including a path for them to object before the window. Stakeholders include business owners, help desk, security, and vendors when the vendor's service is in the path. Stakeholders: A train announcement before a car is uncoupled is the picture of stakeholders, because the riders are the ones who feel the stop. Stakeholders fail when the list is only technical teams and the business calendar is ignored. Stakeholders also fail when notice goes out after the reboot has already started. Stakeholders: Ticket 9004 should add the payroll lead and the message that should have gone out before Friday. The label stakeholders fits when the stem shows people affected who were or were not informed. Stakeholders: Approval is a different fact, and ownership is a different fact, even if one human appears on more than one list. Write stakeholders when you can point at who feels the change and whether they were told. Stakeholders: A RACI chart can help find stakeholders, and the informed row is not the same as the accountable owner. Stakeholders: Keep the Friday close in the story, because surprise downtime for a business process is a stakeholders failure. Customers can be stakeholders when the change touches their portal, even if they do not attend the board. Review stakeholders when the change scope grows, because a small patch can become a payroll outage. Stakeholders: Silence from a stakeholder is not consent unless the process says it is. Stakeholders: Without a stakeholder list, communication is whoever happened to be in the chat. Stakeholders: Put the notice time on the ticket so stakeholders communication can be proved later. Stakeholders may request a different window, and that request is input, not itself the approval. Stakeholders: Contractors on site that day are stakeholders if their work depends on the system. Stakeholders are about impact on people, not about the rollback steps. Stakeholders for a Friday payroll close include the lead who signs off timesheets, not only the server operator. Stakeholders who work nights need a notice that reaches them before they arrive, not a morning email. Stakeholders may demand a different slot, and that demand is input to scheduling rather than a silent veto unless policy says so. Stakeholders lists should be rechecked when a vendor bridge is added at the last hour. Stakeholders proof is the sent message and the audience, stored on the ticket.",
        'r0': 'Stakeholders are requester, operators, and anyone whose work the change will disturb. Stakeholders: Informing them is part of handling stakeholders, and surprise Friday reboots fail that duty as in ticket 9004. Stakeholders: The approver and the single owner are different roles from the broader stakeholder set. Stakeholders: Business calendars such as payroll close belong in the stakeholder check. Stakeholders: Notice after the outage has started is failed stakeholder communication. RACI informed parties are often stakeholders and are not automatically accountable. Stakeholders: Customers and on-site contractors can be stakeholders. Stakeholders: Silence is not consent unless the process defines it that way. Stakeholders: The label fits who is affected and whether they were told. Stakeholders: Scope growth requires a fresh stakeholder look. Stakeholders: Proof of notice belongs on the ticket.',
        'r1': 'Stakeholders are people whose work will feel the change. Stakeholders include the person who asked and the people who operate it. Stakeholders include anyone who must stop work. Tell stakeholders before the window, not after. Stakeholders: A Friday close is a clue that stakeholders were missed. Stakeholders: The person who says yes is not the whole stakeholder list. Stakeholders: The one owner is not the whole stakeholder list. Stakeholders: A train announcement to riders fits stakeholders. Stakeholders: Silence is not the same as agreement. Write down when stakeholders were told. Stakeholders: Grow the list if the change grows. Customers can be stakeholders too.',
        'r2': 'Stakeholders resemble passengers who must be told before a train car is uncoupled. Stakeholders are those riders and the crew whose work changes, not the single officer who approved the cut. Stakeholders: If the announcement comes after the car is already loose, stakeholders were not respected. Stakeholders: One captain who owns the maneuver is a different role and is not the whole stakeholder picture. Stakeholders: An initial on a form is a different act and is not stakeholder notice. Hold stakeholders on who feels the stop and whether they heard in time. Stakeholders: A quiet car is not proof the riders agreed. Stakeholders: The picture stops at affected people and the warning they were owed.',
        'r4': "Stakeholders: List who loses work during the Friday reboot for Stakeholders on ticket 9004. Stakeholders: Add the payroll lead if that name is missing from Stakeholders. Record the time Stakeholders were notified. Separate Stakeholders from the approver's signature. Separate Stakeholders from the single person who will run the change. Stakeholders: Ask whether scope growth added new Stakeholders. Stakeholders: Stop when affected people and their notice are documented.",
        'exam': 'Affected people are stakeholders. Stakeholders: Notice before impact is the stakeholder duty. Stakeholders: The approver is not the entire stakeholder list. Stakeholders: The owner is not the entire stakeholder list. Stakeholders: A missed payroll close is a stakeholder failure mode. Stakeholders: Silence is not automatic consent. Customers can be stakeholders. Stakeholders: Proof of notice belongs with stakeholder handling.',
    },
    'Impact analysis': {
        'body': 'Impact analysis lists what can break, who is affected, and how bad that is, before the change starts. Impact analysis matters because rebooting an identity server also stops wireless sign-in, which a narrow ticket can forget. Impact analysis on ticket 9115 never mentioned that clinic wifi authentication depends on that identity server, so the outage was wider than planned. Impact analysis is a forecast written in advance, not the lab evidence that the change worked and not the steps for undoing it. Impact analysis should name dependencies, duration, security effects, and the worst plausible miss. Impact analysis includes who cannot work, which ties to stakeholders, while the analysis itself is the map of consequences. Impact analysis: A plumber who asks which rooms lose water before closing the main valve is the picture of impact analysis. Impact analysis fails when it copies a template that says low with no dependency check. Impact analysis also fails when it ignores security impact, such as a temporary any-any opening. Impact analysis: Ticket 9115 should be updated with the wireless sign-in dependency and a revised severity. The label impact analysis fits a before-the-fact map of breakage. Impact analysis: Test results are what a pilot actually did, and a backout plan is how you return to the last good state. Write impact analysis when you can point at predicted effects, not at a log from a lab. Impact analysis: Security, privacy, and safety effects belong in impact analysis alongside uptime. Impact analysis: Keep the identity server and wireless sign-in link in the story, because the hidden dependency is impact analysis. Impact analysis: Quantify duration and breadth so low, medium, and high are not vibes. Review impact analysis when scope changes, because a new package can pull new dependencies. Impact analysis: A diagram of upstream and downstream systems makes impact analysis harder to fake. Without impact analysis, the window is a guess. Impact analysis: Put the blast radius in the ticket in sentences a business owner can read. Impact analysis does not approve the change and does not assign the owner, though both consume it. Impact analysis: Hidden batch jobs are a favorite miss in impact analysis. Impact analysis: Repeat the question what else stops if this stops until the list stalls. Impact analysis for identity work should name wireless sign-in, VPN, and badge lookups that die with the directory. Impact analysis worksheets should separate a five-minute reboot from a forty-minute dependency chain. Impact analysis must mention logging or detection gaps if those tools go dark during the change. Impact analysis readers on the business side should not need to decode a port list to see they lose Friday close. Impact analysis updates belong in the ticket before approval, not as a surprise during the window. Impact analysis for this identity reboot should state that badge lookups fail when the directory is down.',
        'r0': 'Impact analysis predicts what breaks, who suffers, and how badly, before work begins. Impact analysis: Dependencies, duration, and security side effects belong in impact analysis. Impact analysis: Ticket 9115 missed that wireless sign-in depends on the identity server. Impact analysis: Pilot logs are test results, and undo steps are a backout plan, not impact analysis. Impact analysis: Template low ratings without a dependency check are fake impact analysis. Impact analysis: Security exceptions such as a temporary any-any rule are impact analysis material. Impact analysis: The label fits a forecast, not a lab transcript. Impact analysis: Business-readable blast radius is part of good impact analysis. Impact analysis: Scope changes require a new impact analysis pass. Impact analysis: Hidden jobs and upstream identity services are common impact analysis misses. Impact analysis: The analysis informs approval and does not replace it.',
        'r1': 'Impact analysis writes what can break before you touch it. Impact analysis names who is hurt and how badly. Impact analysis looks for hidden dependencies. Impact analysis: A valve that also drains other rooms fits impact analysis. Impact analysis: A lab trial is not impact analysis. Undo steps are not impact analysis. Impact analysis: Saying low without looking is weak impact analysis. Impact analysis: Security side effects belong in impact analysis. Impact analysis catches sign-in that dies with identity. Impact analysis: Write the blast radius in plain words. Update impact analysis if the scope grows. Impact analysis: Ask what else stops until the answers stop.',
        'r2': 'Impact analysis resembles an electrician tracing which rooms go dark before pulling a breaker. Impact analysis is that tracing, done before the breaker moves. Impact analysis: A sample repair on a spare circuit is a different activity and is not impact analysis. Impact analysis: A note about how to push the breaker back on is a different activity and is not impact analysis. Hold impact analysis on predicted darkness. Impact analysis: If the map ignores a room, impact analysis is incomplete. Impact analysis: The picture stops at the forecast of who loses power. Impact analysis: Carrying out the repair is later and is not impact analysis.',
        'r4': 'Impact analysis: List systems that stop if the identity server reboots for Impact analysis on ticket 9115. Impact analysis: Include wireless sign-in if it depends on that server. Impact analysis: Rate duration and breadth in the Impact analysis. Separate Impact analysis from pilot logs. Separate Impact analysis from rollback steps. Impact analysis: Add security effects such as temporary wide-open rules. Impact analysis: Stop when the forecast of breakage is written before the change.',
        'exam': 'Impact analysis: A before-the-fact map of breakage is impact analysis. Lab evidence is not impact analysis. Rollback steps are not impact analysis. Impact analysis: Hidden dependencies are the classic impact analysis miss. Impact analysis: Security side effects belong in impact analysis. Impact analysis: A template rating without investigation is not real impact analysis. Approval consumes impact analysis and is not the same artifact.',
    },
    'Test results': {
        'body': 'Test results are evidence that a change worked in a safe place, or that it failed and must not go forward. Test results matter because a note that says looks fine hid crashed pilot laptops and would have pushed a bad agent to the clinic. Test results on ticket 9226 were the pilot logs showing the new agent crashed three machines, which contradicted the cheerful summary. Test results are records from a lab, a sandbox, or a pilot, not a forecast of impact and not the undo procedure. Test results should include what was tried, what passed, what failed, who ran it, and the raw output. Test results that are only a feeling are not test results. Test results: A tailor sewing a sample cuff before cutting the whole bolt is the picture of test results. Test results fail when the pilot group is nothing like production, so success there predicts nothing. Test results also fail when failures are edited out of the attachment. Test results: Ticket 9226 should block promotion until the crash is fixed and a new run is attached. The label test results fits evidence from a trial. Test results: Impact analysis is the prediction made before the trial, and a backout plan is how production returns to the last good state. Write test results when you can point at logs, screenshots, or measured outcomes from a safe environment. Negative results are successful test results if they honestly stop a bad release. Test results: Keep the crashed pilot laptops in the story, because the contradiction with looks fine is test results. Test results: Version the build you tested so test results are tied to the bits you might deploy. Test results: Review whether production data was copied unsafely into the lab as part of producing test results. Without test results, approval is hope. Test results: Automated pipeline output can be test results if a human can read the pass or fail. Test results: A demo in production is not a substitute for test results collected beforehand. Test results: Peer review of the diff is useful and is not the same artifact as test results. Test results: Retest after each fix so test results match the candidate you will actually ship. Test results belong in the ticket, not in a private chat that disappears. Test results for the agent crash should include the three host names and the faulting module. Test results that pass only on a lab image unlike the clinic build are not predictive. Test results attachments should be readable without a private screenshot tool. Test results sign-off should name the human who compared the logs with the exit criteria. Test results from an older build must be marked stale when the candidate changes. Test results should say whether the pilot image matched clinic patch level.',
        'r0': "Test results are pass or fail evidence from a lab, sandbox, or pilot. Test results: Feelings such as looks fine are not test results, which ticket 9226 showed when pilot laptops crashed. Test results: Raw logs, the build identity, and the tester's name belong with test results. Test results: Impact analysis predicts, and a backout plan undoes, so neither is test results. A non-representative pilot weakens test results. Honest failures are valid test results because they stop a bad push. Test results: The label fits trial evidence, not a forecast. Test results: Production demos are not prior test results. Test results: Pipeline output can serve as test results when it is readable and retained. Test results: Retest after fixes or the attached test results describe an old build. Test results: Peer review is not a substitute for test results.",
        'r1': 'Test results show the trial, not a feeling. Test results can be a pass or a fail. Test results come from a safe place first. Crashed pilot machines are test results. Test results: A note that says looks fine is not test results. Test results: A prediction of harm is not test results. Test results: Steps to undo are not test results. Test results: A tiny sample before the full cut fits test results. Match test results to the exact build you will ship. Test results: Keep the raw output with test results. Test results: A failure that stops the release is useful test results. Test results are not a first demo in the live clinic.',
        'r2': 'Test results resemble a tailor sewing one sample cuff before cutting the whole bolt of cloth. Test results are that sample and what it taught, not a guess about the coat and not a plan to reattach the old sleeve. Test results: If the sample cuff tears, test results say stop. Test results: A list of rooms that might go dark is a different activity and is not test results. Test results: Instructions for putting the old sleeve back are a different activity and are not test results. Hold test results on observed evidence from a safe trial. Test results: A cheerful sentence that hides a tear is not test results. Test results: The picture stops at the sample, before the bolt is cut.',
        'r4': 'Attach pilot logs to Test results on ticket 9226. Test results: Mark the crashed machines instead of writing looks fine. Test results: Record the build identity beside Test results. Separate Test results from the earlier impact forecast. Separate Test results from the rollback procedure. Test results: Retest after the fix and replace the old Test results. Test results: Stop when evidence, not optimism, decides whether to proceed.',
        'exam': 'Trial evidence is test results. Looks fine is not test results. Test results: A forecast of impact is not test results. Undo steps are not test results. Failed pilots are valid test results when they halt a release. Test results: The tested build must match the release candidate. Non-representative labs weaken test results. Test results: Production-first demos are not prior test results.',
    },
    'Backout plan': {
        'body': "Backout plan is the path back to the last known-good state, written before you need it. Backout plan matters because a new firewall rule blocked mail and nobody had saved the previous ruleset. Backout plan on ticket 9337 was absent, so the only idea was to keep editing live until mail returned. Backout plan includes the saved prior config, the commands or clicks to restore it, who decides to invoke it, and the deadline inside the window. Backout plan is not impact analysis, which predicts damage, and it is not test results, which show whether a trial passed. Backout plan should be rehearsed when the system allows, because an untested undo is a story. Backout plan: A painter who keeps yesterday's color can until the new coat proves itself is the picture of backout plan. Backout plan fails when the backup of the config is older than the last emergency tweak. Backout plan also fails when the window is too short to actually perform the return. Backout plan: Ticket 9337 should not proceed until the previous ruleset is exported and the decision deadline is written. The label backout plan fits the undo path prepared in advance. Write backout plan when you can point at how to get the last good state back. Database changes need a backout plan that respects transactions and backups, not only a config file copy. Backout plan: Keep the unsaved ruleset in the story, because that missing artifact is backout plan. Backout plan: Decision criteria belong in the backout plan, such as mail failure lasting more than ten minutes. Backout plan: A forward fix can be an alternative, and it is not a backout plan unless returning to known-good is impossible and that limit is explicit. Backout plan: Review disk space and permissions so the restore in the backout plan can actually run. Without a backout plan, courage is the only control. Backout plan: Put the prior version identifier in the ticket. Backout plan ownership should be the same reachable human who owns the change, or a named deputy. Backout plan: Partial backout, where only some servers return, must be described or the plan is fantasy. Backout plan time counts inside the maintenance window, not after users are already angry. Backout plan: The existence of backups elsewhere does not equal a backout plan for this specific edit. Backout plan for the mail rule should name the exported ruleset file and where it is stored. Backout plan decision lines should say what symptom, for how many minutes, triggers the return. Backout plan practice on a spare firewall is worth more than a paragraph nobody has tried. Backout plan partial steps must say which clinic sites return first. Backout plan storage should be reachable when the changed system itself is broken.",
        'r0': 'Backout plan is a pre-written return to the last known-good state. Backout plan: Saved configs, restore steps, a decision deadline, and a decider belong in a backout plan. Ticket 9337 had no backout plan because the prior firewall ruleset was not saved. Backout plan: Impact analysis forecasts harm, and test results show trials, so neither is a backout plan. Backout plan: An untested undo is a weak backout plan. Backout plan: Windows must be long enough to execute the backout plan. Backout plan: The label fits the prepared undo, not a vibe that you will think of something. Backout plan: Forward-fix-only approaches are not a backout plan unless return is truly impossible and documented. Backout plan: Version identifiers of the prior state belong in the backout plan. Backout plan: Partial restores must be spelled out. Backout plan time is part of the maintenance window.',
        'r1': "Backout plan says how to undo the change before you do it. Backout plan needs the last good copy saved. Backout plan needs a person who may call the undo. Backout plan needs enough time to actually undo. A missing ruleset means backout plan was skipped. Backout plan: A forecast of damage is not backout plan. Backout plan: A lab pass is not backout plan. Backout plan keeps yesterday's paint until the new coat dries. Backout plan: We will figure it out is not backout plan. Backout plan: Name the version you can return to. Backout plan: Count undo time inside the window for backout plan. Backout plan: A general backup habit is not this change's backout plan.",
        'r2': "Backout plan resembles a painter keeping yesterday's color can on the counter until the new coat proves itself. Backout plan is that can and the choice to reopen it, decided before the painting starts. Backout plan: If the old can was thrown away, backout plan in this picture is gone. Backout plan: A list of rooms that might look wrong is a different activity and is not backout plan. Backout plan: A sample brushstroke on a board is a different activity and is not backout plan. Hold backout plan on the prepared return. Backout plan: A promise to invent a new color if this one fails is not backout plan. Backout plan: The picture stops at the ability to get the last good color back.",
        'r4': 'Backout plan: Export the current ruleset before editing and attach it as the Backout plan for ticket 9337. Backout plan: Write the deadline and the person who may invoke the Backout plan. Backout plan: Time the restore once in a safe place if you can. Separate the Backout plan from impact notes. Separate the Backout plan from pilot logs. Backout plan: Include database or partial-server steps if those exist. Backout plan: Stop when a known-good return path is written before the change.',
        'exam': 'Backout plan: A prepared return to known-good is a backout plan. Backout plan: No saved prior ruleset means no backout plan. Forecasts are not a backout plan. Backout plan: Pilot logs are not a backout plan. Backout plan: We will figure it out is not a backout plan. Backout plan: The window must include time to execute the backout plan. Backout plan: Version identifiers belong in the backout plan.',
    },
    'Maintenance window': {
        'body': "Maintenance window is the agreed clock time when interruption is allowed. Maintenance window matters because a reboot that starts at 10:00 on a clinic morning was scheduled as whenever, which is not a window. Maintenance window on ticket 9448 collided with patient intake because nobody had reserved an after-hours slot. Maintenance window should include time to implement, test, and back out, not only the optimistic minutes of the change itself. Maintenance window is not a standard operating procedure, which is the standing how-to, and it is not an allow list, which names what may run. Maintenance window: A library that reshelves only after closing is the picture of maintenance window. Maintenance window fails when it overlaps payroll, clinical peaks, or a freeze the business already declared. Maintenance window also fails when it is so short that backout cannot finish before the building opens. Maintenance window: Ticket 9448 should be moved to a published slot and the clinics told. The label maintenance window fits when the question is when interruption is allowed. Maintenance window: Restricted activities are a different idea: the window is not permission for unrelated rewrites. Write maintenance window when you can point at start, end, and who agreed. Maintenance window: Freeze periods are the absence of a maintenance window for non-emergency work. Maintenance window: Keep the 10:00 clinic reboot in the story, because timing against care is maintenance window. Maintenance window: Emergency work may break a maintenance window and then owes an explanation. Maintenance window: Review the calendar for other teams' windows so two changes do not collide. Without a maintenance window, every hour is equally wrong. Maintenance window: Put the timezone on the maintenance window so remote staff do not arrive at the wrong hour. Recurring maintenance window slots help routine patching and still need a fresh impact look. A maintenance window does not approve the change by itself. Maintenance window: Customers with contracts may constrain the maintenance window more tightly than internal preference. Maintenance window: Document the communication that announced the maintenance window. Maintenance window: The window is the when, and the procedure is the how. Maintenance window announcements should reach clinics in their own timezone before the reboot is locked. Maintenance window length must cover the forty-minute identity case, not a five-minute fantasy. Maintenance window collisions with payroll close should be visible on one shared calendar. Maintenance window emergency breaks still owe a note explaining why the clock was ignored. Maintenance window owners should confirm the building is actually quiet at the start, not only on paper. Maintenance window records should show the actual start and end beside the plan. Maintenance window planning should reject a 10:00 clinic slot for an identity reboot. Maintenance window buffers should assume the wireless sign-in dependency adds delay. Maintenance window slots should be booked on the shared clinic calendar before the reboot is announced.",
        'r0': 'Maintenance window is the agreed interval when interruption may occur. Maintenance window: It must fit implement, test, and backout, which ticket 9448 ignored by using a clinic morning. Whenever is not a maintenance window. Maintenance window: Standard operating procedures describe how, and allow lists describe what may run, so those are not a maintenance window. Maintenance window: Freeze periods mean no routine maintenance window. Maintenance window: The label fits the clock, not the step list. Maintenance window: Restricted extra work is not authorized merely because a maintenance window exists. Maintenance window: Time zones belong on the maintenance window. Maintenance window: Overlapping teams can collide inside the same maintenance window. Emergency breaks of a maintenance window require an explanation. Maintenance window: The window does not replace approval. Maintenance window: Customer contracts may narrow the maintenance window.',
        'r1': 'Maintenance window is the hour you were given for risky work. Maintenance window has a start and an end. Maintenance window must leave time to undo. Maintenance window should not be a busy clinic morning. Whenever is not a maintenance window. Maintenance window: The standing how-to is not a maintenance window. Maintenance window: A list of allowed programs is not a maintenance window. Maintenance window: Reshelving after closing fits a maintenance window. Maintenance window: Freeze times mean no casual maintenance window. Tell people the maintenance window in the right clock. Two teams in one maintenance window can collide. Maintenance window: The window alone is not a yes to every idea.',
        'r2': 'Maintenance window resembles a library that reshelves books only after the doors close. Maintenance window is that closed hour, not the checklist of how to shelve and not a list of who may enter the stacks. Maintenance window: If shelving starts while readers are still in the aisles, the maintenance window was ignored. Maintenance window: A card of repeated steps is a different tool and is not the maintenance window. Maintenance window: A guest list at a door is a different tool and is not the maintenance window. Hold the maintenance window on the agreed time. Maintenance window: A window too short to undo the work is a poor maintenance window. Maintenance window: The picture stops at when interruption is allowed.',
        'r4': 'Maintenance window: Move the 10:00 reboot off the clinic morning for the Maintenance window on ticket 9448. Maintenance window: Set start, end, and timezone on the Maintenance window. Maintenance window: Include backout time inside the Maintenance window. Separate the Maintenance window from the standing procedure. Separate the Maintenance window from allow-list technology. Maintenance window: Check freeze dates and other teams before locking the Maintenance window. Maintenance window: Stop when the agreed interruption time is published.',
        'exam': 'Maintenance window: The agreed interruption time is a maintenance window. Whenever is not a maintenance window. Maintenance window: How-to steps are not a maintenance window. Maintenance window: Allow lists are not a maintenance window. Maintenance window: The window must include backout time. Maintenance window: Clinic-hour reboots violate a sane maintenance window. Maintenance window: Freezes cancel routine maintenance windows. Maintenance window: The window is not approval and not a license for extra projects.',
    },
    'Standard operating procedure': {
        'body': "Standard operating procedure is the step-by-step way the organization already agreed to do a repeated job. Standard operating procedure matters because a new hire invented a personal method for firewall rules and skipped the peer check the clinic had written down. Standard operating procedure on ticket 9559 was the published rule-change checklist that the new hire did not open. Standard operating procedure is the standing how, not the clock window for a particular night and not an allow list of programs. Standard operating procedure: A pilot's preflight card flown the same way every time is the picture of standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure should be versioned, approved, and easy to find at the moment of the work. Standard operating procedure fails when it drifts from reality and people quietly follow a better undocumented path. Standard operating procedure also fails when it is so long that nobody can use it under pressure. Standard operating procedure: Ticket 9559 should point the new hire at the checklist and record the deviation. The label standard operating procedure fits a repeated, agreed method. Standard operating procedure: A one-time change ticket uses the procedure and is not itself the procedure. Standard operating procedure: Policy says what and why, while standard operating procedure says how. Write standard operating procedure when you can point at the agreed steps for a job done often. Standard operating procedure: Updates to the procedure belong in the same change discipline as the systems they describe. Standard operating procedure: Keep the invented firewall method in the story, because bypassing the checklist is a standard operating procedure failure. Standard operating procedure: Local notes taped to a monitor are a smell that the standard operating procedure is wrong or hidden. Standard operating procedure: Review procedures after incidents so the standard operating procedure learns. Without a standard operating procedure, every new hire invents a dialect. Standard operating procedure: Training should practice the standard operating procedure, not a slide that contradicts it. Emergency deviations from standard operating procedure should be written down and reviewed. Standard operating procedure: The procedure is not permission to work at 10:00 if the window says otherwise. Standard operating procedure: Allow lists may be one step inside a standard operating procedure and are a different control. Standard operating procedure: Name the document id when you cite a standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure for firewall edits should include the peer check the new hire skipped. Standard operating procedure copies taped to monitors are a signal the shared document is stale. Standard operating procedure drills should make the new hire perform the checklist once before solo work. Standard operating procedure deviations during emergencies belong in the ticket the same night. Standard operating procedure reviews after incidents should change the steps that failed, not only the blame.",
        'r0': "Standard operating procedure is the approved step list for a repeated task. Standard operating procedure: Ticket 9559 is a standard operating procedure miss because a new hire improvised firewall changes. Standard operating procedure: Version, approval, and findability belong to a living standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure: Maintenance windows are when, and allow lists are what may run, so those are not the procedure. Standard operating procedure: Policy states intent, and the standard operating procedure states how. Standard operating procedure: Undocumented local habits mean the standard operating procedure is stale or unused. Standard operating procedure: The label fits the standing method, not a single night's ticket. Standard operating procedure: Emergency deviations should be recorded and reviewed. Standard operating procedure: Training must match the standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure: A one-time change record is not the procedure itself. Standard operating procedure: Overlong procedures fail under pressure.",
        'r1': "Standard operating procedure is the agreed steps for repeated work. Standard operating procedure is written and shared. Standard operating procedure is not a private habit. Standard operating procedure was skipped by an inventing new hire. Standard operating procedure is not tonight's clock. Standard operating procedure is not a list of allowed apps. Standard operating procedure can be a card used every time. Update the standard operating procedure when reality changes. Practice the standard operating procedure in training. Standard operating procedure: Emergencies that skip steps should be written down. The standard operating procedure says how, not merely why. Standard operating procedure: Name the document when you follow standard operating procedure.",
        'r2': "Standard operating procedure resembles a pilot's preflight card used the same way on every flight. Standard operating procedure is that card, not the hour the airport allows maintenance and not a passenger manifest. Standard operating procedure: If each pilot invents checks, standard operating procedure has collapsed. Standard operating procedure: A reserved hour after closing is a different idea and is not standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure: A list of permitted guests is a different idea and is not standard operating procedure. Hold standard operating procedure on the repeated agreed steps. Standard operating procedure: A card nobody can find during the rush is a failed standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure: The picture stops at how the familiar job is done.",
        'r4': "Standard operating procedure: Open the firewall checklist that is the Standard operating procedure for ticket 9559. Standard operating procedure: Compare the new hire's steps to that Standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure: Record the deviation instead of ignoring it. Separate the Standard operating procedure from tonight's clock window. Separate the Standard operating procedure from application allow lists. Update the Standard operating procedure if the safe method has truly changed. Standard operating procedure: Stop when the agreed repeated steps are what you cite.",
        'exam': 'Standard operating procedure: Agreed repeated steps are a standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure: A private habit is not a standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure: The maintenance clock is not a standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure: Allow lists are not a standard operating procedure. Standard operating procedure: Policy is why, and the procedure is how. Standard operating procedure: Stale procedures cause silent drift. Standard operating procedure: Emergency skips should be reviewed.',
    },
    'Allow lists and deny lists': {
        'body': "An allow list states what may run or connect, and it refuses everything it does not name. A deny list states what is blocked, and it misses everything it does not name. The default is the whole difference. Unknown software dies on an allow list. Unknown software runs on a deny list. Teams that hear deny and think it sounds stricter have the comparison backward.\n\nOn a laptop, application control checks a publisher, a path, or a hash before a program starts. If the clinic allow list contains only approved publishers, a new installer from a mail attachment does not start, even though nobody has seen that file before. A deny list of known-bad hashes stops those hashes and lets the new file through. Mail gateways still use deny lists of known-bad senders because the universe of legitimate mail is too wide to name. That can be a reasonable choice for mail and a weak choice for programs. Say which list you mean. A block event can be reviewed later, but the block itself is the preventive act. The review is a second control.\n\nA staff member downloaded setup.exe from a vendor message onto a clinic laptop. AppLocker wrote event 8004, a block, because the publisher was not on the allow list. Ticket END-90 holds the event, the file name, and the user. Last year's malware hash list would not have contained setup.exe. The file was new. The allow list did not need a prior sighting. Turning the control off so the installer could run would have restored the weaker default. The right follow-up is a change that adds the publisher if the software is truly needed, or a refusal if it is not.\n\nDefenders keep the allow list complete enough for clinical work and short enough to review. Add software through a change with an owner, not through a hallway exception that never expires. Read the block log so a broken bedside application is fixed by an approved update rather than by a local disable. Where a deny list is the honest design, such as known-bad mail domains, record the feed you trust and how fresh it is. A stale deny list is a list of yesterday's problems. Name the default in the standard: unnamed programs must not run.\n\nStudents call every block an allow list. Blocking one named bad domain, while every other domain still resolves, is deny-list behavior. Students also describe a deny list as the tighter control because the word sounds negative. Ask what happens to a program the list has never named. If it is stopped, you are looking at an allow list. If it runs, you are looking at a deny list. That question is the separating fact, and event 8004 on an unseen installer is the allow-list answer.\n",
        'r0': 'An allow list permits named software or connections and refuses the unnamed rest. A deny list blocks named items and allows everything else. AppLocker event 8004 on END-90 blocked setup.exe because its publisher was absent from the clinic allow list. A hash deny list from last year would have missed that new file. Mail often uses a deny list of known-bad senders because legitimate mail cannot all be named. Application control should default the other way. Add a publisher through a change, not by disabling the control. A block is preventive, and reading the block log is a later review. The test is simple: an item the list has never seen should fail on an allow list and succeed on a deny list.\n',
        'r1': 'An allow list names what may run, and it stops the rest. A deny list names what is banned, and it misses the rest. Unknown software dies on an allow list. Unknown software runs on a deny list. The allow list is the tighter default. A block of one bad name is not automatically an allow list. Mail can reasonably ban known-bad senders. Laptops should not use that same loose default for programs. Add new software through a change. Do not turn the control off to make an installer work. Ask what happens to a file nobody has seen before.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic kitchen that accepts only the ingredients on this week's delivery sheet. A jar that is not on the sheet goes back, even if nobody has declared that brand poisonous. A forbidden-jar list works the other way: named bad jars are turned away, and a brand-new jar walks in. The delivery sheet is stricter because silence means no. The forbidden list is looser because silence means yes. A tasting note written after a bad meal is not the same as the sheet at the door. On the laptop, the publisher list is the delivery sheet. Event 8004 is the jar of setup.exe sent back because it was not on that sheet.\n",
        'r4': 'Open event 8004 on END-90 and record the file name, user, and publisher. Confirm setup.exe was blocked because it was unnamed, not because its hash was famous. Do not disable application control to let the installer run. If the software is required, open a change to add the publisher. Keep a mail deny list in a separate note from the laptop allow list. State the default in the ticket in one sentence. Stop when the note says what would happen to a file nobody has listed.\n',
        'exam': 'An allow list stops what it has never named. A deny list allows what it has never named. That default is the separating fact. One blocked bad domain is deny-list behavior if everything else still works. Disabling the control is not an update to the list. A block log review does not change which list you built. The unseen installer that cannot start is the allow-list stem.\n',
    },
    'Restricted activities': {
        'body': "Restricted activities are jobs the change window forbids, such as schema edits or production data copies that were not in the approved request. Restricted activities matter because someone used a maintenance window to attempt a risky database rewrite that the ticket never described. Restricted activities on ticket 9771 were that rewrite, which was scope creep dressed up as being already in the window. Restricted activities exist so a window is not a free pass for every idea that occurs to the person holding the keyboard. Restricted activities are not downtime, which is how long users cannot work, and they are not a service restart, which is one specific technical action. Restricted activities: Fixing an oven is not permission to remodel the dining room, which is the picture of restricted activities. Restricted activities should be listed in the change template so nobody has to guess the forbidden column. Restricted activities often include bulk data extracts, schema changes, firewall any-any experiments, and unrelated patch bundles. Restricted activities fail as a control when the list is blank and culture says the window means do whatever. Restricted activities: Ticket 9771 should be stopped or re-approved as a new change, not quietly continued. The label restricted activities fits work that is outside the authorized scope during an otherwise valid window. Write restricted activities when you can point at an action the approval did not include. Emergency exceptions to restricted activities still need a named approver. Restricted activities: Keep the unapproved database rewrite in the story, because extra ambition is restricted activities. Restricted activities: Review command history after the window if restricted activities are a known temptation on that team. Restricted activities: A service restart that was in the ticket is not a restricted activity. Restricted activities: Downtime estimates do not authorize extra tasks. Without restricted activities, change scope is a suggestion. Restricted activities: Put examples in the standard so restricted activities are concrete. Restricted activities: Vendors on a bridge call are still bound by restricted activities. Restricted activities: If the rewrite is truly needed, it gets its own approval rather than hiding inside tonight's window. Restricted activities protect the backout plan, because surprise edits make the undo path wrong. Restricted activities: Name the forbidden work, not merely the clock, when you enforce restricted activities. Restricted activities on database hosts include unapproved schema edits and ad hoc copies of production tables. Restricted activities lists should be visible in the change template so the temptation is named in advance. Restricted activities discovered mid-window should pause the work until a new approval exists. Restricted activities by a vendor on a bridge still count, even if the vendor calls the rewrite a quick fix. Restricted activities protect the saved backout, because a surprise schema edit makes yesterday's export incomplete.",
        'r0': 'Restricted activities are out-of-scope tasks forbidden during a change window. Restricted activities: Schema rewrites, production copies, and unrelated upgrades are typical restricted activities. Ticket 9771 is restricted activities because a database rewrite was never in the request. Restricted activities: Downtime is a duration, and a service restart is one action, so those are not restricted activities. Restricted activities: A blank forbidden list makes the window a free pass. Restricted activities: The label fits scope creep during an approved window. Emergencies that violate restricted activities still need an approver. Restricted activities: In-ticket restarts are not automatically restricted activities. Restricted activities: Surprise edits invalidate the backout assumptions. Restricted activities: The label is about what must not be done, not about how long users are down. Vendors share the same restricted activities. Restricted activities: New scope needs a new approval.',
        'r1': 'Restricted activities are jobs the window does not allow. Restricted activities include surprise database rewrites. Restricted activities include copying live data for fun. Restricted activities: The oven repair is not a permit to move walls. Restricted activities are not the count of minutes users wait. Restricted activities are not an already approved service restart. Restricted activities: A blank ban list invites restricted activities. Restricted activities: Stop and seek a new yes for extra work. Restricted activities: Name the forbidden task in the template. Vendors must honor restricted activities too. Restricted activities: Extra edits ruin the undo plan. Restricted activities: Stick to the ticket to avoid restricted activities.',
        'r2': 'Restricted activities resemble a plumber who was hired to fix a leak and is forbidden from redesigning the staircase. Restricted activities are that boundary on the job, not the hours the water will be off and not the act of relighting one burner that was requested. Restricted activities: If the plumber opens the staircase anyway, restricted activities were ignored. Restricted activities: A posted count of dark minutes is a different fact and is not restricted activities. Restricted activities: Relighting the requested burner is a different fact and is not restricted activities. Hold restricted activities on work that was never approved. Restricted activities: A culture that says you are here so do anything deletes restricted activities. Restricted activities: The picture stops at the limit of the agreed job.',
        'r4': 'Restricted activities: Compare the live commands with the approved request for Restricted activities on ticket 9771. Restricted activities: Halt database rewrites that were not listed. Restricted activities: Require a new approval before any extra scope. Separate Restricted activities from the downtime estimate. Separate Restricted activities from a restart the ticket already allowed. Tell vendors the same Restricted activities list. Restricted activities: Stop when out-of-scope work is identified and contained.',
        'exam': 'Restricted activities: Out-of-scope work during a window is restricted activities. Downtime length is not restricted activities. Restricted activities: An approved service restart is not automatically restricted activities. Restricted activities: Schema edits and data copies are common restricted activities. A free-pass culture defeats restricted activities. Restricted activities: New scope needs new approval. Restricted activities: Surprise edits break the backout plan. Restricted activities: The forbidden list should be explicit.',
    },
    'Downtime': {
        'body': "Downtime is the stretch when users cannot do the work, and that stretch belongs in the ticket before it starts. Downtime matters because an identity reboot took forty minutes while the desk had planned for five. Downtime on ticket 9882 was that gap between the promised five minutes and the real forty, which blew the morning schedule. Downtime estimates should include implementation, verification, and backout, not a best-case reboot timer. Downtime is not restricted activities, which are forbidden extra tasks, and it is not the mere fact that a service restart will occur. Downtime: A drawbridge that stays up for a posted count of minutes is the picture of downtime, because the number is the promise. Downtime fails when it is described as a brief blip while a dependency chain makes it long. Downtime also fails when partial downtime, such as wireless sign-in dead while phones still work, is reported as zero. Downtime: Ticket 9882 should be corrected with the measured forty minutes and the dependency that caused it. The label downtime fits the duration users cannot work. Write downtime when you can point at a planned or actual outage length. Communicate downtime to stakeholders in plain language and in the right timezone. Downtime: Keep the five-versus-forty contrast in the story, because honesty about length is downtime management. Downtime: Maintenance windows must be long enough for the stated downtime plus backout. Unplanned downtime still gets recorded so the next estimate improves. Service restarts often cause downtime for that service's users even when the host stays up. Application restarts cause downtime for unsaved work in that application. Without a downtime number, the business cannot staff around the change. Put both planned and actual downtime on the ticket after the window. Downtime of a security control, such as logging going dark, is still downtime worth naming. Downtime: Review historical actuals before you copy last year's optimistic downtime. Customers may have contractual downtime limits that are tighter than internal comfort. Downtime is a duration fact, not a permission to do extra projects. Downtime for the identity reboot should be restated as forty minutes once the wireless dependency is known. Downtime notices should tell the front desk what still works and what does not. Downtime of logging or authentication is a security event as well as a user-facing wait. Downtime history from the last three similar changes should inform the next estimate. Downtime actuals belong next to the plan so the gap is visible to the next approver. Downtime staff planning includes who covers phones while the scheduling app is dark. Downtime communication should say forty minutes when that is the honest number, not a brief blip. Downtime for logging darkness should be called out beside user-facing downtime. Downtime figures belong in the stakeholder note before the identity reboot starts.",
        'r0': 'Downtime is how long users cannot perform the work. Downtime: Plans must include implement, test, and backout, which ticket 9882 missed when five minutes became forty. Partial outages still count as downtime for the broken function. Downtime: Restricted activities are forbidden tasks, and a service restart is an action, so those are not the duration itself. Downtime: The label fits the time users are blocked. Stakeholder notices should carry the downtime figure and timezone. Historical actuals should correct optimistic downtime. Downtime: Logging or authentication gaps are security downtime worth stating. Downtime: Windows must be sized for the downtime plus return time. Unplanned downtime is still recorded. Downtime: The number is not a license for extra scope. Contractual limits may cap acceptable downtime.',
        'r1': 'Downtime is how long the work is stopped. Downtime must be said before it starts. Downtime: Five minutes planned and forty minutes real is a downtime miss. Count undo time inside downtime. A dark function is downtime even if other functions live. Downtime is not the list of forbidden extra jobs. Downtime is not the name of a restart by itself. Tell people the downtime in plain words. Downtime: A posted span of minutes fits downtime. Downtime: Write both the plan and the actual downtime. Security tools going dark are downtime too. Downtime: Do not hide a long outage inside the word blip.',
        'r2': 'Downtime resembles a drawbridge that stays up for a posted count of minutes while boats pass. Downtime is that posted span, not a rule against remodeling the bridge and not the act of relighting one lamp on the bridge. Downtime: If the bridge stays up far longer than posted, downtime was misstated. Downtime: Forbidden extra construction is a different limit and is not the downtime figure. Downtime: Relighting one lamp is a different action and is not the downtime figure. Hold downtime on the length of interrupted work. Downtime: A sign that says a moment while the wait is a meal is dishonest downtime. Downtime: The picture stops at the duration people cannot cross.',
        'r4': 'Downtime: Compare planned and actual minutes for Downtime on ticket 9882. Include dependency delays in the Downtime number. State partial Downtime such as sign-in down while other apps stay up. Separate Downtime from forbidden extra tasks. Separate Downtime from the restart action itself. Tell stakeholders the Downtime figure and timezone. Downtime: Stop when the duration of lost work is explicit.',
        'exam': 'Lost-work duration is downtime. Downtime: Five versus forty minutes is a downtime estimation failure. Partial outages still create downtime. Restricted activities are not downtime. Downtime: A restart is an action, not the downtime number. Backout time belongs inside downtime planning. Blip is not an acceptable downtime description. Security-control darkness is downtime too.',
    },
    'Service restart': {
        'body': "Service restart stops and starts one background service, which can drop active sessions for that service while the rest of the machine stays up. Service restart matters because restarting the mail transfer service kicks open connections even though the server itself never reboots. Service restart on ticket 9993 dropped clinic mail flow for several minutes while file shares on the same host stayed available. Service restart is smaller than a full reboot and still interrupts that service's users. Service restart is not downtime as a concept, though it causes some downtime, and it is not restricted activities unless the restart was outside the ticket. Service restart is not an application restart, which bounces a user-facing program and its unsaved screens rather than a system service. Service restart: Relighting one burner while the rest of the stove stays lit is the picture of service restart. Service restart on Windows is often done from the services console or a service command, and on Linux from a service manager such as systemctl. Service restart of a mail transfer agent affects SMTP listeners, commonly port 25, and submissions on related ports, without powering off the host. Service restart fails when dependent services are not ordered correctly and come back before the directory they need. Service restart also fails when nobody warns users that their current connections will die. Service restart: Ticket 9993 should name the service, the host, and that the host did not reboot. The label service restart fits one daemon or service cycled. Write service restart when the machine stays up and only that service's sessions drop. Service restart: Keep the mail connections in the story, because the partial blast is service restart. Service restart: A reboot is the wrong word if the operating system never stopped. Application teams may see service restart as an outage of their feature even if you call it minor. Service restart: Clustered services can restart one node while others serve, which changes the downtime story. Without a dependency order, service restart becomes a self-inflicted outage. Service restart: Document the exact service name so the wrong service is not cycled. Service restart should have a backout, which may be starting the previous version of that service. Health checks after service restart belong in the window. Service restart is a precise action, not a mood. Service restart of the mail transfer agent should name the unit and confirm file shares stayed up. Service restart order should bring the directory back before services that query it. Service restart warnings should reach people with open mail sessions, not only the on-call chat. Service restart health checks should send a test message, not only query a process list. Service restart backout should know how to return the previous package of that one service.",
        'r0': "Service restart cycles one background service and drops that service's sessions while the host stays up. Ticket 9993 is a service restart of mail transfer that left file shares running. Service restart: It is smaller than a reboot and still an interruption. Service restart: Application restart targets a user program and unsaved screens, which is not the same as service restart. Service restart: Downtime is the duration, and restricted activities are forbidden extra work. Service restart: Linux systemctl and the Windows services console are typical service restart tools. Service restart: Mail transfer restarts affect SMTP on port 25 and related submission ports. Service restart: Dependency order matters or the service restart fails on startup. Service restart: The label fits one service cycled, not a full power cycle. Service restart: Warn users whose connections will drop. Service restart: A backout may be restoring the prior service build.",
        'r1': "Service restart cycles one background helper. Service restart leaves the computer itself running. Service restart drops people using that helper. Service restart: Mail can die while files stay up during service restart. Service restart is smaller than a full reboot. Service restart still interrupts that one function. Service restart is not a user's unsaved form by itself. Service restart is not the count of lost minutes by itself. Warn people before a service restart. Service restart: Start helpers in the right order after service restart. Service restart: Name the exact helper you will service restart. Check health after the service restart.",
        'r2': 'Service restart resembles relighting one burner while the other burners keep their flame. Service restart is that one burner, and the stove as a whole stays on. Service restart: A full blackout of the kitchen is a bigger event and is not service restart. Service restart: Closing one cookbook on the counter is a different event and is not service restart. Hold service restart on one background function and the sessions it drops. Service restart: If another burner depended on the first, service restart can cascade. Service restart: The picture stops at one service returning, not at rebooting the building. Service restart: People using that burner need a warning before service restart.',
        'r4': 'Service restart: Name the service and the host for Service restart on ticket 9993. Service restart: Confirm the operating system never rebooted. Warn users whose connections Service restart will drop. Order dependencies before the Service restart. Separate Service restart from an application bounce that loses unsaved forms. Separate Service restart from the downtime minutes you will also report. Service restart: Stop when one service cycle, not a full reboot, is the action.',
        'exam': 'Service restart: One service cycled with the host up is service restart. Service restart: A full reboot is not service restart. Service restart: An application bounce that loses forms is not service restart. Service restart: Mail can stop while other roles on the host continue. Service restart: SMTP port 25 is a common service restart impact. Dependency order belongs in service restart. Service restart: Downtime is the duration caused, not the action name.',
    },
    'Application restart': {
        'body': 'Application restart bounces one program, and users of that program lose unsaved state while other programs stay up. Application restart matters because the scheduling app was restarted at lunch and three clerks lost forms they had not saved. Application restart on ticket 10014 was that lunch bounce, done without a warning to save. Application restart is not a service restart of a background daemon, though some apps are implemented as services, and the user-visible loss of a form is the clue. Application restart is not a full server reboot, and it is not a statement about legacy age or about dependency maps. Application restart: Closing one cookbook while the rest of the kitchen stays open, and losing the recipe that was only on the counter, is the picture of application restart. Application restart should be preceded by a save warning and a short freeze on new entries. Application restart fails when session state lived only in memory and the team assumed the database had every keystroke. Application restart also fails when users are mid-call and the softphone is the application you bounce. Application restart: Ticket 10014 should record who was told to save and which forms could not be recovered. The label application restart fits one program cycled with unsaved work at risk. Write application restart when other programs and the host remain available. Application restart: Keep the three lost forms in the story, because unsaved state is application restart. Application restart: Clustered applications can drain one node before application restart so users move first. Without a drain, application restart is a hard kick. Application restart: Document whether caches must be warmed after application restart or the first users will see errors. Application restart: A browser refresh is not always an application restart of the server-side program. Distinguish application restart from deploying a new build, which may include the restart plus new bits. Backout of an application restart may mean starting the previous build, not merely clicking start. Health checks after application restart should use a real user path, not only a process list. Application restart windows should avoid payroll and clinical documentation peaks. Tell the desk which application restart is coming so they do not blame the network. Application restart is narrower than a machine reboot and wider than doing nothing to that program. Application restart at lunch should have been preceded by a save banner for the scheduling clerks. Application restart evidence includes which three forms could not be recovered from memory. Application restart in a cluster should drain the node so users land on a peer first. Application restart health checks should submit a real schedule row, not only see a process. Application restart timing should avoid payroll and clinical documentation peaks. Application restart notes should name the three clerks who lost unsaved schedule forms.',
        'r0': 'Application restart cycles one user-facing program and drops unsaved in-memory state. Application restart: Other programs and the host can stay up, which ticket 10014 violated in communication when lunch forms vanished. Application restart: Service restart language fits a background daemon, and a reboot fits the whole machine. Application restart: Legacy status and dependency maps are different labels. Application restart: Warn users to save before application restart. Application restart: In-memory forms are not safe just because a database exists. Drain clustered nodes before application restart when you can. Application restart: A process list is a weak health check compared with a real user path. Application restart: Deploying new bits is more than application restart if the code changed. Application restart: Backout may require the previous build. Application restart: Avoid peak documentation hours for application restart. Application restart: The label fits lost unsaved work in one program.',
        'r1': 'Application restart bounces one program people are using. Application restart can throw away unsaved forms. Application restart: Other programs can stay open during application restart. Application restart: The computer can stay on during application restart. Application restart: Warn people to save before application restart. Application restart is not a full machine reboot. Application restart is not only a hidden helper service. Application restart: A cookbook closed on the counter fits application restart. Application restart: Memory-only typing may vanish in application restart. Application restart: Move users off a node first when you can. Application restart: Check a real task after application restart. Application restart: Do not restart the scheduling app at the busiest hour.',
        'r2': 'Application restart resembles closing one cookbook on a busy counter while every other book stays open. Application restart loses the recipe that existed only as notes on that open page. Application restart: The kitchen does not close, which is why application restart is narrower than shutting the building. Application restart: Relighting a hidden burner is a different action and is not application restart. Application restart: Calling the oven old is a different fact and is not application restart. Hold application restart on one program and the unsaved work inside it. Application restart: A warning to write the recipe down should come first. Application restart: The picture stops when that program returns and the unsaved page is gone.',
        'r4': "Application restart: Warn clerks to save before Application restart on ticket 10014. Application restart: Name the program and confirm other programs stayed up. Record forms that Application restart destroyed. Separate Application restart from a background service cycle. Separate Application restart from a full reboot. Application restart: Health-check a real scheduling task after Application restart. Application restart: Stop when one program's unsaved state is the impact you describe.",
        'exam': 'Application restart: One program bounced, with unsaved work lost, is application restart. Application restart: A full reboot is not application restart. Application restart: A background service cycle is not the same label when the clue is a user form. Application restart: Warn-to-save is part of safe application restart. Application restart: In-memory state is the risk. Application restart: Other programs can remain available. Application restart: Peak hours are a bad time for application restart.',
    },
    'Legacy applications': {
        'body': 'Legacy applications are old programs the business still needs, often unable to meet a new control without a workaround. Legacy applications matter because the billing app only speaks an old sign-in method and cannot prompt a second factor. Legacy applications on ticket 10125 were kept reachable only from one admin jump host instead of being exposed to the clinic network. Legacy applications in that design used a compensating stand-in rather than a pretend claim that modern sign-in exists inside the binary. Legacy applications often depend on retired protocols such as SMBv1, NTLM, or TLS 1.0 that current guidance says to disable. Legacy applications are not fixed by an application restart, which only bounces the process, and they are not the same fact as a dependency map. Legacy applications: An old oven with no thermostat that you do not leave unattended is the picture of legacy applications. Legacy applications fail when someone publishes them to the internet because a scanner found them convenient. Legacy applications also fail when the last person who understood them leaves and there is no runbook. Legacy applications: Ticket 10125 should name the missing control, the jump host, and the plan to replace or wrap the app. The label legacy applications fits age plus inability to meet a current safeguard. Write legacy applications when the workaround is required because the software cannot change soon. Legacy applications: Keep the jump host in the story, because isolation is how you live with legacy applications. Legacy applications: Vendors who are gone cannot patch legacy applications, so compensating controls and monitoring are the honest options. Data inside legacy applications still needs backup and access review. Legacy applications: A restart does not modernize legacy applications. Dependencies of legacy applications may be equally old and should be listed before a change. Without a retirement date, legacy applications become permanent architecture. Extra logging around legacy applications only helps if someone reads it. Legacy applications: Network placement is often the real control for legacy applications. Legacy applications: Authentication proxies in front of legacy applications can add a factor the app itself cannot. Legacy applications: Document the protocol weakness so the next hardening project does not enable SMBv1 for convenience. Legacy applications are a business constraint, not a compliment. Legacy applications that still need SMBv1 should say so in the exception record with an expiry. Legacy applications behind a jump host should be retested when the host is patched. Legacy applications without a vendor need a named internal owner anyway. Legacy applications data still requires backup and an access review on the same cycle as newer systems. Legacy applications exposed to the internet are an incident waiting, not a convenience. Legacy applications that require TLS 1.0 should not be reachable except through the documented jump host.',
        'r0': 'Legacy applications are old business programs that cannot meet a current control without a workaround. Legacy applications: Old sign-in, SMBv1, NTLM, or TLS 1.0 are typical legacy application limits. Legacy applications: Ticket 10125 isolates billing behind a jump host because multi-factor prompts are impossible in the app. Legacy applications: Application restart does not modernize legacy applications. Legacy applications: Dependency lists are a different artifact. Internet exposure of legacy applications is a serious failure. Legacy applications: Missing vendors mean no patch, so compensating controls and monitoring remain. Legacy applications: The label fits age plus a control that will not fit. Retirement dates keep legacy applications from becoming invisible forever. Legacy applications: Proxies can add a factor in front of legacy applications. Legacy applications: Extra logs require a reader. Legacy applications: Protocol exceptions must be documented or hardening will undo them.',
        'r1': 'Legacy applications are old programs you still need. Legacy applications often cannot take the new lock. Legacy applications may speak only an old sign-in. Put legacy applications behind a guarded door. Do not place legacy applications on the open internet. Restarting legacy applications does not modernize them. Legacy applications are not merely a map of helpers. Legacy applications: An oven with no thermostat fits legacy applications. Legacy applications may have no maker left to fix them. Legacy applications: Set a date to retire legacy applications. Watch legacy applications more closely meanwhile. Legacy applications should record the old weakness in writing.',
        'r2': 'Legacy applications resemble an old oven with no thermostat that nobody should leave unattended. Legacy applications are that oven, still required for a dish, not a promise that a new safety valve will appear if you relight it. Legacy applications: Closing the oven door and opening it is not a cure. Legacy applications: A list of pots the soup needs is a different fact and is not legacy applications. Hold legacy applications on age plus a control the program cannot accept. Legacy applications: Putting the oven in the dining room where anyone can touch it is how legacy applications get people hurt. Legacy applications: The picture stops at babysitting the old tool until it can be replaced. Legacy applications: A date on the wall for a new oven belongs with legacy applications.',
        'r4': "Legacy applications: Name the control the billing app cannot do for Legacy applications on ticket 10125. Legacy applications: Restrict reach to the jump host and record that path. List weak protocols the Legacy applications still require. Separate Legacy applications from a simple process bounce. Separate Legacy applications from a dependency diagram alone. Legacy applications: Set a retirement or review date. Legacy applications: Stop when the workaround is honest about the app's age.",
        'exam': 'Legacy applications: Old software that cannot take a new control is legacy applications. Legacy applications: A jump host is a common stand-in for legacy applications. Legacy applications: SMBv1, NTLM, and TLS 1.0 are typical legacy application baggage. Restarting does not modernize legacy applications. Legacy applications: Internet exposure is the wrong place for legacy applications. Legacy applications: Dependencies are a different label. Legacy applications: A retirement date belongs on legacy applications. Legacy applications: Unread extra logs do not secure legacy applications.',
    },
    'Dependencies': {
        'body': "Dependencies are the other systems a change needs, and the systems that need it in return. Dependencies matter because the badge system looks standalone until you learn every name check calls the directory server. Dependencies on ticket 10236 were that hidden directory lookup, so a directory restart also stopped badges. Dependencies include upstream systems you call and downstream systems that call you. Dependencies are not the legacy label, which is about an old program's inability to take a new control, and they are not an application restart. Dependencies: A clock tower whose hands stop when the cable snaps is the picture of dependencies, because the visible part needed a hidden part. Dependencies fail when the diagram shows a single box and the runtime shows five. Dependencies also fail when change order is wrong and an app starts before the directory it requires. Dependencies: Ticket 10236 should list the directory, the protocol port such as LDAP 389 or LDAPS 636, and the badge symptom. The label dependencies fits the relationship, not the age of the software. Write dependencies when you can point at what else breaks or what else must be up. Dependencies: Keep the badge-to-directory link in the story, because the surprise coupling is dependencies. Dependencies: Startup order is a practical output of dependency analysis. Circular dependencies need a deliberate break or the restart will deadlock. Vendors and SaaS endpoints are dependencies even when you do not host them. Dependencies: Without a dependency list, impact analysis is guesswork and downtime estimates are fiction. Review dependencies after integrations are added, because a small script can become critical. Dependencies: Authentication, name resolution, and time sync are favorite hidden dependencies. Dependencies: Document both directions: who this system needs, and who needs this system. Dependencies: A maintenance window must respect the slowest dependency, not only the box you intended to touch. Service restarts cascade along dependencies if you ignore order. Dependencies should be tested by stopping the upstream in a safe environment when possible. Dependencies: The badge outage was a dependency failure, not a badge hardware failure. Dependencies for badges include the directory on LDAP 389 or LDAPS 636 and also time sync if certificates are checked. Dependencies should be proven by stopping the upstream in a lab and watching the badge console fail. Dependencies in both directions belong on the diagram before the window is booked. Dependencies on a SaaS identity provider count even when the clinic does not host that server. Dependencies that form a loop need a documented start order or the restart deadlocks. Dependencies reviews after each integration keep a small script from becoming a silent critical path. Dependencies for the badge outage should be proven in a lab before the production window. Dependencies on time sync belong in the same list when certificate checks are enabled.",
        'r0': 'Dependencies are upstream and downstream systems a change relies on or that rely on it. Ticket 10236 is dependencies because badges query the directory on every lookup. Dependencies: LDAP 389 and LDAPS 636 are common directory dependency ports. Dependencies: Legacy age and application restarts are different labels. Wrong start order exposes dependencies. SaaS endpoints count as dependencies. Dependencies: Impact and downtime estimates require the dependency list. Circular dependencies can deadlock a restart. Dependencies: Time sync, name resolution, and authentication are common hidden dependencies. Dependencies: The label fits the coupling, not the symptom alone. Dependencies: Both directions belong in the map. Dependencies: The slowest dependency sizes the window. Dependencies: Badge failures during a directory stop are dependency evidence, not a reader hardware fault.',
        'r1': 'Dependencies are the other things this change needs. Dependencies are also the things that need this change. Dependencies: Badges that ask a directory have dependencies. Dependencies: If the directory stops, badges stop, and that is dependencies. Dependencies are not just a label for old software. Dependencies are not the act of bouncing one app. Dependencies: A clock that stops when a cable snaps fits dependencies. Hidden links surprise you during dependencies failures. Dependencies: Start systems in an order that respects dependencies. Outside services can be dependencies too. Dependencies: Write both directions when you map dependencies. Dependencies: The slowest link sets the wait.',
        'r2': 'Dependencies resemble a clock tower whose hands freeze when an unseen cable snaps. Dependencies are that cable and the hands together, not a claim that the clock is old and not the act of restarting the motor. Dependencies: If nobody knew about the cable, dependencies were undocumented. Dependencies: An ancient motor that cannot take a modern brake is a different fact and is not dependencies. Dependencies: Restarting the motor alone is a different action and is not dependencies. Hold dependencies on what else stops or must start. Dependencies: A tower that looks standalone can still have dependencies. Dependencies: The picture stops at the hidden link that explains the frozen hands.',
        'r4': 'Dependencies: Trace what the badge system calls before you call it independent for Dependencies on ticket 10236. Dependencies: Record the directory and the port as Dependencies. Dependencies: Stop the directory in a test and watch badges. Separate Dependencies from the mere age of the software. Separate Dependencies from an application bounce. Dependencies: Map who else calls this system. Dependencies: Stop when both directions of coupling are written down.',
        'exam': 'Upstream and downstream links are dependencies. Dependencies: Badges that query a directory are a dependency example. Dependencies: LDAP and LDAPS ports belong in dependency notes. Legacy status is not dependencies. An application restart is not dependencies. Start order follows dependencies. SaaS targets are dependencies. Dependencies: Hidden identity and time services are classic dependencies.',
    },
    'Updating diagrams': {
        'body': "Updating diagrams means the picture of the network or the system matches what you just changed, on the same day if you can. Updating diagrams matters because a new guest network existed while the wall diagram still showed one flat network. Updating diagrams on ticket 10347 corrected the logical drawing so the guest segment, its firewall, and its isolation were visible. Updating diagrams covers logical topology, physical racks, and data-flow pictures, not only a single slide. Updating diagrams is not updating policies and procedures, which change the words people must follow, and it is not version control, which keeps history of files. Updating diagrams: A trail map redrawn the day a bridge moves is the picture of updating diagrams. Updating diagrams fail when the only copy lives on a departed employee's laptop. Updating diagrams also fail when the guest network is drawn but the firewall rules that define it are omitted. Updating diagrams: Ticket 10347 should attach the revised diagram and retire the flat-network poster. The label updating diagrams fits a stale picture after a real change. Write updating diagrams when responders would navigate the old drawing and be wrong. Updating diagrams: Keep the guest network in the story, because the missing segment is updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: Data-flow diagrams need the same refresh when a new SaaS path appears. Updating diagrams: Rack elevations matter when a firewall moves rooms and nobody updates the physical sheet. Without updating diagrams, incident response follows folklore. Updating diagrams: Version control can store the diagram file, and the act of updating diagrams is still the content change. Updating diagrams: Review diagrams after every project close, not only after outages. Updating diagrams: Label the date on the drawing so people can see whether updating diagrams happened. Updating diagrams: A photograph of a whiteboard is weak updating diagrams unless it is stored where the team looks. Updating diagrams: Both east-west and north-south paths belong in the refreshed picture. Updating diagrams: Segmentation claims are unbelievable if updating diagrams still shows one flat LAN. Updating diagrams: Tie the diagram version to the change ticket. Updating diagrams is part of finishing the change, not a nice-to-have next quarter. Updating diagrams for the guest network should show the firewall, not a single flat cloud. Updating diagrams stored only on a departed laptop do not help the next incident. Updating diagrams includes data-flow arrows when a new SaaS path appears. Updating diagrams should be dated and linked to ticket 10347 so responders trust the sheet. Updating diagrams of racks matter when a firewall moves rooms and the elevation still shows the old slot. Updating diagrams should retire the flat-network poster so the night shift does not follow it during an incident. Updating diagrams of the guest segment must show the firewall that enforces isolation, not only a colored box.",
        'r0': 'Updating diagrams refreshes logical, physical, and data-flow pictures to match the change. Ticket 10347 is updating diagrams because a guest network was missing from a flat drawing. Updating diagrams: Policy text and file history are different duties from updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: A diagram only on a private laptop will not be updated for the team. Updating diagrams: Draw isolation and firewall boundaries, not only a cloud shape. Updating diagrams: The label fits a stale map after a real topology change. Incident response depends on updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: Date the drawing. Updating diagrams: Whiteboard photos need a shared home. Updating diagrams: SaaS paths belong in data-flow updates. Updating diagrams: A flat LAN drawing contradicts a segmentation project until updating diagrams occurs. Updating diagrams: Link the diagram to the change ticket. Finishing the change includes updating diagrams.',
        'r1': 'Updating diagrams fixes the picture the same day you move the wires. Updating diagrams includes the guest network you just added. Updating diagrams includes racks and data paths, not only one slide. Updating diagrams must reveal a hidden new segment. Updating diagrams is not the rule text people follow. Updating diagrams is not the history log of a file by itself. Updating diagrams can be a trail map redrawn after a move. Updating diagrams: Store the picture where the team can find it. Date the picture when updating diagrams. Responders get lost without updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: Show the firewall, not only a blob. Updating diagrams: Tie the new picture to the change ticket.',
        'r2': "Updating diagrams resembles a trail map corrected the day a footbridge is moved. Updating diagrams are that corrected map, not the park rulebook and not the stack of older maps kept in a tin. Updating diagrams: If hikers still carry the old map, updating diagrams did not reach them. Updating diagrams: A rewritten rule about dogs on the trail is handbook work and is not updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: A dated notebook of every map edit is history-keeping and is not updating diagrams. Hold updating diagrams on the picture matching the ground. Updating diagrams: A map in someone's pocket is not shared updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: The picture stops when the current path is what the drawing shows.",
        'r4': 'Updating diagrams: Replace the flat drawing with the guest segment for Updating diagrams on ticket 10347. Updating diagrams: Include the firewall boundary on the new picture. Updating diagrams: Retire the old poster so people stop using it. Separate Updating diagrams from handbook wording. Separate Updating diagrams from the file-history system that stores the drawing. Updating diagrams: Date the diagram and link the change ticket. Updating diagrams: Stop when a responder would navigate the new picture correctly.',
        'exam': 'Updating diagrams: A current topology picture is updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: A flat drawing after a new segment means updating diagrams was skipped. Policy wording is not updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: File history is not the same duty as updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: Logical, physical, and data-flow views all count. Private laptop copies fail updating diagrams. Updating diagrams: Date and ticket links belong on the drawing. Incident response depends on updating diagrams.',
    },
    'Updating policies and procedures': {
        'body': 'A change is unfinished while the page people are told to follow still describes the old practice. Updating policies and procedures means bringing those written rules into line with what the organization does now. Otherwise the night shift will obey the page, and the new setting will look optional, wrong, or unknown.\n\nThe mechanism sits in the change path, not in a later documentation dream. The template should name every procedure, handbook page, and posted card the change touches. The owner of each page revises it in the same window, with a revision and a date. The old sentence and the new sentence both belong on the ticket so a reviewer can see the delta. Training is the moment you tell the people who actually do the work. Leaving both versions in the drawer only creates confusion, so the stale copy has to be removed or marked so it cannot be mistaken for current. Version history of a firewall file is useful and still is not this step. A diagram of cables is not this step either.\n\nRemote wipe became mandatory in POL-MDM-03 revision 6. Handbook page 14 still said the wipe was optional. Change CHG-2402 was marked complete after the console setting changed. Ticket DOC-19 reopened it. A night supervisor, reading page 14, told a colleague that wipe was optional and delayed the report of a lost phone by hours. The console and the handbook disagreed. People followed the handbook. The fix was a revised page 14, a note in the shift log, and removal of the loose photocopy that still said optional. The console setting had been correct the whole time. The control people could see was not.\n\nDefenders add the documentation task before the change window, not after someone files an incident. Quote both sentences. Name the page owner. Check the places staff actually look, including shared drives and laminated cards, not only the policy library. Do not close CHG-2402 while page 14 contradicts the setting. When the practice changes again, the revision number moves again. An undated edit on a wiki is hard to defend because nobody can show which sentence was current on the night of the loss.\n\nStudents update the network drawing and think the procedures are done. The drawing shows where cables go. It does not tell a supervisor whether wipe is mandatory. Students also point at version control of a script and stop. History of a config file does not replace the handbook. A golden laptop image is a build, not the instruction a person reads. The separating fact is the page the worker is told to obey. If that page still describes the old practice, as page 14 did, this update is not finished.\n',
        'r0': 'Updating policies and procedures makes the written rule match the new practice in the same change window. POL-MDM-03 revision 6 made remote wipe mandatory, while handbook page 14 still said optional. CHG-2402 was closed on the console change alone. DOC-19 reopened it after a supervisor delayed a lost-phone report by following page 14. Quote the old sentence and the new sentence. Remove stale photocopies. A network diagram update is a different task. Config version history is a different task. A golden image is a build, not the handbook. Evidence is the revised page the night shift can open, with a revision and a date.\n',
        'r1': 'Change the page people are told to follow. If the page is old, people will do the old thing. A new setting in a console is not the page. Write the new rule in the same window as the change. Quote the old line and the new line. Take the stale copy out of the drawer. A picture of the cables is not this page. A history of a config file is not this page. A standard laptop build is not this page. Do not close the change while the handbook disagrees.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a warehouse closing checklist clipped to the cage. Last month the door code changed, and the checklist still prints the old code, so the closer sets the wrong alarm and leaves proud of a finished list. The alarm panel itself has the new code. The failure is the paper the closer trusts. A fresh floor plan on the office wall does not fix the checklist. A saved copy of yesterday's panel config does not fix it either. The clinic handbook is that checklist. Page 14 still saying wipe is optional is the old door code, and DOC-19 is the morning you discover the closer followed it.\n",
        'r4': 'Compare POL-MDM-03 revision 6 with handbook page 14. Quote the mandatory sentence and the optional sentence on DOC-19. Revise page 14 before you close CHG-2402 again. Remove loose copies that still say optional. Tell the night supervisors in the shift log. Do not treat a network drawing update as this task. Stop when the page a worker would obey matches the console setting.\n',
        'exam': 'The page people follow has to match the new practice. A finished console change is not that page. A network diagram is not that page. Version history of a config is not that page. A standard image is not that page. The stem to recognize is a handbook that still says optional after the rule became mandatory. Close the change only after the stale sentence is gone.\n',
    },
    'Version control': {
        'body': "Version control keeps each revision of a config or a script, recording who changed it and how to restore an older copy. Version control matters because an infrastructure file edited on a laptop with no history left nobody able to see yesterday's version. Version control on ticket 10569 started when that file moved into a shared repository with an author, a message, and a diff. Version control is not updating diagrams, which is about the picture matching the network, and it is not updating policies and procedures, which is about the rules people must follow. Version control: A bound lab notebook with dated pages that are never torn out is the picture of version control. Version control fails when the real edit happens on a server by hand and is never committed. Version control also fails when everyone shares one login, so the author field is meaningless. Version control: Ticket 10569 should show the commit id, the author, and the parent version that can be restored. The label version control fits history and restorability of the file, not the approval to make the change. Write version control when you can point at a prior revision and who made the latest one. Version control: A commit is not change approval, which is a separate yes from an authorized person. Version control: Keep the laptop-only file in the story, because missing history is absent version control. Version control: Branch protections can require review before merge, and that review is still not a substitute for the business approver. Version control: Tags mark a release so you can return to the bits that were actually deployed. Version control: Secrets do not belong in version control repositories. Version control: Binary firewall exports can be stored in version control if the format is diffable or at least retrievable. Without version control, backout of a config is archaeology. Version control: Pull request comments help and do not rename the system into something other than version control. Version control: Review who can force-push, because destroying history defeats version control. Version control: Infrastructure as code is a way to use version control, not a different definition. Version control: The repository location should be known to the team, not only to one engineer. Version control: Diff the change against the ticket so the committed text matches the approved text. Version control preserves yesterday, and someone still has to decide whether yesterday was correct. Version control for the infrastructure file should show an author other than a shared robot account. Version control history should let ticket 10569 restore yesterday's text without the laptop that used to hold it. Version control protections should block force-push on the main line so history cannot be erased casually. Version control is not a substitute for the business yes that approved the change. Version control repositories should not contain live passwords or API keys.",
        'r0': 'Version control stores revisions, authors, and a path back to an older config or script. Ticket 10569 is version control because a laptop file with no history moved into a shared repository. Version control: Diagrams and policy wording are different updates from version control. Version control: A commit is not change approval. Version control: Shared accounts make the author field useless. Version control: Hand edits on servers that are never committed bypass version control. Version control: Tags identify deployed bits. Version control: Secrets do not belong in the repository. Version control: Force-push rights can destroy the history version control exists to keep. Version control: The label fits restorability and attribution. Infrastructure as code uses version control and is not a second meaning. Backout depends on version control for config text. Version control: The committed diff should match the approved request.',
        'r1': 'Version control keeps every revision. Version control names who changed the file. Version control lets you return to yesterday. Version control: A laptop file with no history lacks version control. Version control is not the network picture. Version control is not the rule text by itself. Version control can be a notebook of dated pages. Version control needs a personal login, not a shared one. Version control is not the same as permission to edit. Version control: Do not store real secrets in version control. Version control: Mark the release you actually shipped. Version control: Put the repository where the team can reach it.',
        'r2': "Version control resembles a bound lab notebook whose dated pages are never torn out. Version control is that stack of pages, including who wrote each one and the ability to copy an older page forward. Version control: A corrected trail map is a different task and is not version control. Version control: A rewritten rule about penalties is a different task and is not version control. Hold version control on history that can be restored. Version control: A shared pencil with no names makes the notebook a poor version control story. Version control: Throwing away yesterday's page defeats version control. Version control: The picture stops at recoverable history, not at whether the experiment was wise.",
        'r4': "Version control: Import the infrastructure file into a shared repository for Version control on ticket 10569. Version control: Record the author and the commit id. Version control: Show that yesterday's text can be restored. Separate Version control from diagram updates. Separate Version control from handbook wording. Version control: Confirm the commit is not being treated as the business approval. Version control: Stop when history and a return path exist for the file.",
        'exam': 'Version control: Revision history with an author and a restore path is version control. Version control: A laptop with no history lacks version control. Diagrams are not version control. Version control: Policy text updates are not version control. Version control: A commit is not approval. Version control: Shared logins ruin attribution. Version control: Secrets do not belong in version control. Version control: Force-push can destroy the history.',
    },
}
